 before any other rewards if you don't yet have it.\010Map Area  |  Monsters That Drop Coffer Key\010Ru'Aun Gardens\010Flamingo, Sprinkler, Groundskeeper\010Temple of Uggalepih\010Mimic, Temple Bee, Tonberry Dismayer, Tonberry Maledictor, Tonberry Pursuer, Tonberry Stabber, Hover Tank, Iron Maiden, Temple Guardian\010Den of Rancor\010Bifrons, Cutlass Scorpion, Mousse, Succubus Bats, Tonberry Beleaguerer, Tonberry Slasher, Tonberry Trailer\010Kuftal Tunnel\010Scavenger Crab, Stygian Pugil, Cave Worm, Haunt, Sabotender Sediendo, Robber Crab, Sand Lizard, Recluse Spider, Deinonychus, Goblin Alchemist, Goblin Bandit, Goblin Mercenary, Goblin Tamer\010The Boyahda Tree\010Bark Spider, Death Cap, Moss Eater, Robber Crab, Knight Crawler, Mourioche, Old Goobbue\010Ve'Lugannon Palace\010Detector, Mystic Weapon, Ornamental Weapon, Dustbuster\010Ifrit's Cauldron\010Mimic, Dire Bat, Volcano Wasp, Old Opo-opo, Volcanic Gas, Dodomeki\010Quicksand Caves\010Sand Spider, Helm Beetle, Sand Eater, Antican Hastatus, Antican Princeps, Antican Signifer, Sabotender Bailaor, Sand Lizard\010Sea Serpent Grotto\010Bog Sahagin, Marsh Sahagin, Swamp Sahagin, Rock Crab, Mimic, Blubber Eyes, Razorjaw Pugil\010Labyrinth of Onzozo\010Cockatrice, Goblin Bouncer, Goblin Hunter, Goblin Enchanter, Mushussu, Goblin Miner, Goblin's Leech, Flying Manta\010Pso'Xja\010Camazotz, Labyrinth Lizard, Gargoyle, Blubber Eyes, Cryptonberry Cutter, Cryptonberry Plaguer, Cryptonberry Harrier, Cryptonberry Stalker, Magic Millstone, Dark Elemental\010Oldton Movalpolos\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Coffer/Chest Keys Maps\010\010Ancient Bomb, Bugbear Bondman, Goblin Doorman, Goblin Shovelman, Goblin Tollman, Goblin Oilman, Moblin Ashman, Moblin Coalman, Moblin Gasman, Moblin Pikeman, Moblin Rodman, Moblin Repairman, Thunder Elemental\010Newton Movalpolos\010Goblin's Bat (Goblin Headman), Bugbear Watchman, Bugbear Deathsman, Nightmare Bats, Purgatory Bat, Goblin Junkman, Moblin Aidman\010<div class=\"toau\">",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Purchased\010\010Area  |  NPC  |  Location  |  Price\010Al Zahbi\010Riyadahf \010Aht Urhgan Whitegate (I-10)\010600 gil\010Nahmau\010Riyadahf\010Aht Urhgan Whitegate (I-10)\0103,000 gil\010Bhaflau Thickets\010Riyadahf \010Aht Urhgan Whitegate (I-10)\0103,000 gil\010Wajaom Woodlands\010Riyadahf\010Aht Urhgan Whitegate (I-10)\0103,000 gil\010Mamook\010Imperial Gate Guard\010Various\0102,000 Imperial Standing\010Arrapago Reef\010Imperial Gate Guard\010Various\0102,000 Imperial Standing\010Havlung\010Imperial Gate Guard\010Various\0102,000 Imperial Standing",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Quested\010\010Map Area  |  Quest/Mission  |  NPC  |  Location\010Aydeewa Subterrane\010What Friends are For\010Tsetseroon\010Nashmau (G-6)\010(Upper level)\010Alzadaal Ruins\010Aht Urhgan Mission 9: Undersea Scouting\010Kamih Mapokhalam\010(trade 3 Imperial Mythril Pieces)\010Bhaflau Thickets (F-6)\010Mount Zhayolm\010Rock Bottom\010Zurtermor\010Mount Zhayolm (L-7)\010Caedarva Mire\010The Prankster\010Ahaadah\010Aht Urhgan Whitegate (G-6)\010</div>\010<div class=\"wotg\">",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Shadowreign Era Maps (WotG)\010\010If you own the Key Item for a present day era region that has a Shadowreign equivalent, you will also have the map for the Shadowreign region. \010The following areas are unique to the Shadowreign era, and must be quested:\010Map Area  |  Quest  |  NPC  |  Location\010Vunkerl Inlet (S) \010The Flipside of Things\010Rarcasmeault\010Garlaige Citadel (S) (J-6)\010Grauberg (S)\010Lost in Translocation\010Thorben\010Batallia Downs (S) (J-10)\010Fort Karugo-Narugo (S)\010The Weekly Adventurer\010[[Naiko-Paneiko (S)\010Crawlers' Nest (S) (L-8)\010</div>\010<div class=\"abyssea\">",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Cruor\010\010Cruor Prospector\010Abyssea - Altepa\010Abyssea - Attohwa\010Abyssea - Grauberg\010Abyssea - Konschtat\010Abyssea - La Theine\010Abyssea - Misareaux\010Abyssea - Tahrongi\010Abyssea - Uleguerand\010Abyssea - Vunkerl\010</div>\010<div class=\"soa\">",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Bayld\010\010Sifa Alani in Eastern Adoulin (F-9)\010</div>\010<div class=\"rov\">",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Escha Silt\010\010Affi in Escha - Zi'Tah (F-10)\010Dremi in Escha - Ru'Aun (H-11)\010Shiftrix in Reisenjima (F-12)\010</div>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_52602",["title"]="New Player FAQ",["page_title"]="New Player FAQ",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/New_Player_FAQ",["revision"]="119468",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template Guide: ] \010Written by Sushomi. This is a work in progress, so I would ask that other players do not directly edit this guide. If you feel there is an inaccuracy, or a question you think should be added, please reach out to me either in the main Horizon, or the Horizon Wiki Discords.\010This page aims to answer questions that new players may have when starting this game. Please note that I'll be keeping this to gameplay questions, as technical issues are already handled elsewhere (see Tech Support).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Does Race/Job Combo Matter?\010\010To an extent, yes. The different races do have different strengths and weaknesses. For example, taru have high INT, AGI, and MP, but low HP, STR, and VIT. This makes them excel at mage jobs and are somewhat weaker at melee and tanking jobs.\010HOWEVER, that difference is not significant enough to stop you playing whatever race/job combo you prefer. You're going to be staring at your character for many, many hours, so it is definitely wise to pick whichever race you like the most. For some players, min/maxing stats will be part of the enjoyment. But for most casual players, just know that you can be proficient at any job no matter your race.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Does Starting Nation Matter?\010\010Not really, no. The main impact your nation has on your gameplay is which story missions you will play through. Each nation has their own storyline.\010The nation vendors will have some different items for sale, and it can affect which supply quests you have available to you at the start. However, these are fairly minor considerations in the long run and will only affect very early gameplay.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Do I have to pick a certain race/nation combo to get the lvl 1 city ring?\010\010No, you don't. In retail you needed to pick certain combos to get a starting ring. For example, if you chose to play an Elvaan in San d'Oria, you would start with a San d'Orian ring.\010This has been changed on Horizon. You now get the city rings on any race upon completing Mission 1-3.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is hardcore, and how do I start it?\010\010Hardcore is a gamemode where you try to get as many levels as possible without ever dying. It rewards a few cosmetic rewards upon reaching certain milestones.\010Hardcore mode can only be initiated on a fresh character, by talking to a designated npc in your starting city.\010Upon dying, the character loses hardcore status and continues as a regular character.\010For more information on the rewards and the location of the hardcore npcs, please see Hardcore.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="I've just logged in for the first time. What do I do?\010\010Firstly, you'll have an item in your inventory called the Adventurer Coupon. Check that item's page for a list of npc's you can trade it to. When you trade it to them, it gives you 50gil (not a lot, but it's a start).\010Make sure to open your equipment screen (Ctrl+E) and equip your starting weapon. And if you're a mage, go into your items (Ctrl+I) and learn your level 1 spell.\010Then I'd recommend checking out the Tutorial NPC in your starting city. They'll give a couple of quests to help get you started, including a voucher for a free exp band (increases exp earned for a short time). So the quests are well worth doing. Note: these npcs will try to give you a quest where you have to obtain level 4. This quest is not actually enabled, so once you have the free exp ring voucher, the tutorial quests have finished.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Can I change jobs?\010\010Yes, you can. You start the game with the six basic jobs (Warrior, Monk, Thief, Red Mage, White Mage, and Black Mage unlocked.\010To change jobs, open up your map and look for the Residential Area (it will be denoted by a large square icon that looks like a keychain and keys). If you head there, you'll be able to speak to a Moogle in your mog house who will let you change jobs.\010Note that you will need to acquire a weapon and possibly spells for this job, so you may need a little bit of gil before you can realistically change jobs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Can I use Trusts?\010\010No, Trusts are not enabled on this server. They were not a part of the 2006 era of FFXI, and give people incentive not to group up with other players. As such, there are no plans for them to ever be enabled on Horizon.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Why can't I do Records of Eminence?\010\010Records of Eminence came much later than the 2006 era version of the game that Horizon is currently emulating, so they are not enabled here.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is Signet?\010\010Signet is a buff that can be obtain by speaking to various guards in the main cities or outposts. It has various effects, such as speeding up HP recovery while resting, preventing TP loss while resting, and raising your defense and evasion against monsters that are lower level than you.\010It also allows you to earn Conquest Points from killing enemies, and allows enemies that grant experience to drop crystals. At level 50+, Signet also increases experience gains slightly. See the Signet page for the full details.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I get one of the EXP rings?\010\010You can obtain a free exp ring voucher by following the Tutorial NPC quests. After that, they can be purchased from a Conquest Overseer in exchange for Conquest Points. You can only purchase or recharge an exp ring once per week (this resets at JP midnight on Sundays).\010Note that the client menus are extremely hard to edit, and therefore the cost of these rings shows incorrectly in the menus. Please see this page for the correct costs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="My inventory is constantly full. What can I do about this?\010\010By default, the game does not place stackable items into stacks. You need to turn on a setting to enable this. Go to Menu > Config > Misc 2 and set the auto sort option to on.\010This will mean every time you get a new item the game will try to combine it with other partial stacks of the same item, which will save you a lot of inventory space since most items stack to either 12 or 99.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What do I do with these Beastmen's Seals I'm getting?\010\010Beastmen seals are used to gain entry to battlefields where you will fight one or more monsters for the chance at specific loot. They drop from level 2+ monsters, and can only drop at most every 5 minutes.\010They are quite valuable and worth holding onto. They do stack to 99, so providing you turn auto sort on they won't take up too much space.\010Once you reach the city of Jeuno (most players will head here around lvl 20-ish), you can head to Shami in Port Jeuno. This is the npc who will exchange your seals for various orbs, which are used to initiate the fights. If you trade Shami your beastmen seals (and later your Kindred Seals which drop from level 50+ monsters) he will store them for you until you're ready to purchase an orb.\010See BCNM for more details on the battlefields that can be entered.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is Yell? And how is it different from Shout?\010\010/shout is a chat channel that broadcasts to people in the same area as you within a certain radius.\010/yell broadcasts to all areas of the game where yelling is enabled (cities and towns only). As per Horizon rules, /yell is only to be used for looking for groups or trading/commerce.\010Before you can use the Yell command, you need to have at least level 10 or 15 [Wiki: needs verification] . You will then need to head to a town or city and type !yell which will ask you to read through some basic rules, before typing a second command to indicate you have agreed to the rules.\010Note that yell has a 5-minute cooldown, and its use is actively monitored by the GM team. Anyone found to be using /yell for reasons other than those specified in the !yell rules can be temporarily or permanently muted from being able to use it.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I get the Mog Satchel?\010\010Head over to the main Horizon Discord and look for the #discord-setup channel. Follow the instructions there to Register (you will need to be logged in game to complete the process). Once done, you will need to zone or relog for the change to take effect.\010Once done, to access your satchel you can either type /satchel in-game, or go to your Menu > View House > Mog Satchel.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I increase the space in my Mog Satchel?\010\010The Satchel starts with 30 slots once you unlock it. It does not increase in space as you increase your main inventory slots (it used to do this on retail).\010Instead, you can earn extra mog satchel space by completing certain quests in game. Note that the anniversary quests will require you to be fairly far into the game and have progressed in some of the missions. Currently, there are 15 extra slots available:\010 5 from Anniversary_Scavenger_Hunt_2023\010 5 from Anniversary Scavenger Hunt 2024\010 5 from linking your Horizon account to the Retro Achievements site.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Can I get the Satchel on my alts?\010\010Yes, you can. If you already had your alt created when registered your account, then your alts should already have it unlocked.\010If you created your alt after you registered, then you need to head back to the #discord-setup channel in the main Horizon discord, and click the button to 'Refresh My Account Link'. Once done, zone or relog and all characters on your account will have the mog satchel.\010Please note that the extra slots obtained from the anniversary quests only apply to the character the quest was completed on. You can complete these quests on multiple characters though.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is the difference between Mission and Quest?\010\010Missions progress the main story in Final Fantasy XI. You will start off with your chosen Nation's main story, which can be initiated from a gate guard. Doing these will increase your Nation Rank (not to be confused with Fame/Reputation - see next question for explanation of that).\010Each expansion then comes with its own set of story missions. From level 30 you can progress with Chains of Promathia missions. And after reaching rank 6 with your nation, you will also be able to complete the Rise of the Zilart story missions.\010Quests are essentially side content, and can serve a number of uses, such as raising your fame/reputation with various factions, getting unique gear, gil, maps, or unique perks (for example, chocobo license, airship pass, etc).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is the difference between Fame/Reputation and Rank?\010\010Your Nation Rank indicates how far through that nation's storyline you are. Your Signet buff also lasts an extra hour for each rank you have, and Conquest Overseer npcs will require you to have a certain rank to purchase different items.\010Fame (or Reputation as other games might call it) is a measure of how many side quests you've done for that faction. Note that each of the three nations also have a fame level, which is completely separate to your rank.\010Fame is used for a few different things. It slightly decreases the cost of items from npcs in that area, and slightly increases the amount that those npcs will give you for items you sell to them. Having higher level fame is also a prerequisite for certain quests.\010Generally, each faction will have at least one repeatable quest you can complete to easily level up your fame. Check out the [Guide] for more details on those, and also take a look at [Reporters] to see how you can check what level fame you have with the different factions.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How does the Auction House work?\010\010Players list an item (or a stack of items) for sale on the Auction House, and specify the minimum price they want to sell for.\010Another player then goes to the auction house to buy said item. They are unable to see what prices the items are listed for, but can see the price of the last 10 of that item that sold. They enter their bid, and as long as they have bid over or equal to the minimum being asked for, they acquire the item. If they bid under, they will be told their bid was unsuccessful and can try again with a higher amount.\010When there are multiple of an item for sale, the item that sells first will be the one with the lowest minimum bid. If there are multiple with the same minimum bid, then the one that was listed first will be sold first.\010For example: Player A lists a stack of wind crystals for 2,000 gil. Shortly after that, Player B lists a stack of wind crystals for 1,900 gil. Player C then goes to buy a stack of wind crystals. They see the last 10 sales were all for 2,000 gil, so they bid this amount. Since Player B listed their item for a lower amount, theirs will sell. Player B will now get 2,000 gil from Player C.\010Note: Even though Player B listed the item for less than what Player C bid, they obtain the full amount that Player C bid, not their original asking price.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Where do I pick up the gil from my Auction sales?\010\010You need to speak to either the moogle in your mog house, or a nomad moogle. Select 'Delivery Box' and then 'Receive' to view any gil received from auction sales.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="I sold an item on Auction House, but didn't receive the gil\010\010Chances are you haven't actually sold it yet. The game client is limited in that it can only display 6 items being sold at a time. However, you have the ability to list 21 items for sale at any one time per character.\010If you have more than 6 items for sale, when you click sales status it will show you the oldest 6 items first. To view the rest, exit out of the 'sales status' menu, then click back into it. This will now show you the next 6 items.\010Note that when viewing sales status at an auction house, it will output a message into your chat log telling you what page you are viewing. You can have a maximum of four pages (3 pages of 6, and 1 page of 3, to make up your 21 item max).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How can I make money as a new player?\010\010First off, always make sure you have Signet when fighting enemies. Just talk to the guards at your city gates (they will have R.K, W.W, or I.M after their name) to obtain the Signet buff. This allows crystals to drop, and lets you accrue Conquest Points from killing enemies. Save up your crystals, and sell any full stacks of 12 on the Auction House. Doing this can get you a little bit of gil to at least afford some of your first armor/weapon upgrades.\010A common strategy is to level the 6 starter jobs to around level 10-12, and selling all the crystals you get from doing this. While you're doing this, take a look at the price of crystals and work out which sell for more. You can then selectively target enemies based on which will give the best returns.\010The three starter cities will also have some basic repeatable quests that can often be completed using drops from monsters in the starter zones. Use these to earn a little bit of extra gil.\010Also, don't just buy gear upgrades because you can. Take a moment to ask if you actually need them. For example, if you're going to level a mage in a party, you're usually not going to be taking many hits, so buying gear with a couple of extra points in defense will provide little benefit but could still set you back a few thousand gil. Whereas a weapon or ring with a few points of extra INT or MND (depending on your job) will serve you much better.\010Besides that, check out the Crafting and HELM section of this FAQ for some other potential gil-makers.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="When should I stop soloing and start grouping up with other players?\010\010Most players will start partying up around level 12-ish.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I look for an exp party?\010\010Firstly, you should put your party flag up. This is the little green exclamation mark you may see next to some players name. The party flag allows other players to see that you're interested in teaming up. To do this, either type /inv, or go to your Menu > Party > Seek Party.\010If you need to turn the flag off, just do the same again.\010You can also add a comment that other players will be able to see when searching for party members. You can edit your search comment by going to Menu > Search > Edit Comment (the game will ask you to select a category / sub-category, but these don't really matter so just pick any).\010Generally, the kind of information you would put in is:\010 How much exp you need to level - this helps players who might be looking for an appropriate level sync. If they're forming a lvl 14 party, and you're only 100 exp from levelling, it can mean having to relocate or rearrange the party, so your exp to next level (TNL, for short) is very useful info to provide.\010 Any restrictions you have - for example, you might not be able to level sync any lower than level 12 due to not having an appropriate weapon of that level, or maybe you will only party up if your friend also gets invited, in which case you may want to put that in your comment.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I search for other players to party with?\010\010You can do this using search commands to bring up a list of players that meet certain criteria.\010As an example, if you type '/sea all inv' it will show a list of players with their party flag up. You can further narrow down this search to specific level ranges or jobs by adding additional criteria, such as '/sea all 10-20 whm inv', which will find all whms between levels 10 and 20 with their party flag up. (Note: the 'all' part of the command specifies searching in all areas of the world. If you left it out, it would only search in your current zone).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Where should I go to party?\010\010The wiki has a player maintained list of EXP Camps which you may find useful. Most low level parties will go to Ghelsba Outpost around level 10, and Valkurm Dunes around level 12.\010There are other camps at that level, but players tend to stick with what they know best, and Valkurm Dunes seem to hold a lot of nostalgia with those who played back in the 2000's. That said, some of the other camps are great and worth a try if you get the chance.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="If I join a level synced party, will my gear sync down?\010\010No, gear does not sync when in level sync parties. As such, even when you obtain a gear upgrade, you may want to hang onto the old gear for a few levels in case you join a level sync party and can't use your new gear.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Where can I find more information about exp parties?\010\010Check out the other player guides about parties here: Player Guides",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Why aren't the homepoint crystal teleports working?\010\010The ability to warp between homepoint crystals is disabled here. The reasoning for this is it makes the game feel much emptier because you no longer see players running around in various zones.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Are the Field Manual teleports enabled?\010\010No. Field Manuals were added later in the game's lifecycle and are not enabled on Horizon. They will likely be added with later expansions, though we do not currently know if there will be any changes to how they work.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What's the quickest way for me to get around the world?\010\010There are a few travel methods that players use:\010 Outpost Teleportation - This will probably be one of your main travel methods as your progress through the game. Please see the outpost page for more information on how to unlock the teleports, and their level requirements.\010 Chocobo License - After level 20 you can obtain a license to rent chocobos from major cities and a few other locations.\010 Teleport Spells - Learned by White Mage at levels 36, 38, and 42. Can be used to teleport an entire party. Requires you to have touched the Telepoint crystals and picked up their respective Key Item before you can be teleports to that location. Check the Telepoint page for a list of their locations.\010 Warp and Warp II - Learned by Black Mage at levels 17 and 40 respectively. Warp can be used on yourself to send you back to your homepoint, whereas Warp II can be used to warp another player in your party. (See also Warp Cudgel and Instant Warp)\010 Deathwarp - Also known as D3. Basically, get yourself killed for a quick warp to your homepoint. Can use a low level job (4 and under) so that you don't lose any/much experience when you die. Obviously, don't do this is you're player on hardcore.\010 Airship Pass - You can purchase a pass in Jeuno for 500,000 gil. However, you will obtain one for free when reaching rank 5 with your Nation Missions.\010 Complete the Mog House Exit Quests to be able to travel around cities faster.\010 Use warp NPCs to travel around Windurst faster.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Why can't I buy the Warp Ring?\010\010The Warp Ring was not originally added to the game until after 2008, so it is not possible to purchase it on Horizon. However, it is very difficult to edit the client's menus to remove the ring from the Conquest vendors. There is only one method to obtaining the Warp Ring currently, and that is to reach level 75 on a Hardcore Character (note: the ring will be lost if the player ever loses their hardcore status).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How can I get the chocobo whistle / raise my own chocobo?\010\010Currently, you can't. The chocobo raising system is incredibly complex, and has never been fully implemented on private servers. Due to the number of variables that can happen during the chocobo raising process, it requires a ton of retail captures to document the process correctly, not to mention the amount of work that will have to go into coding it all.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Can I summon different mounts?\010\010You can only mount up from a chocobo rental npc. While Horizon doesn't have different mounts, per se, we do have the ability to change our chocobo's skin. See Chocobo Skins for more details.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is the best crafting profession for \"insert job here\"?\010\010Long story short, there isn't a best crafting profession for any specific job. Players from other games might think that you would want leatherworking for jobs wearing light armor, or blacksmithing for heavy armor jobs, etc. However, FFXI's crafting system is quite different to a lot of other MMO's.\010Crafting in FFXI is designed to encourage trade, because you can only max one craft, and levelling them up is a HUGE gil sink. If you're looking to make your own armor, then know that it is going to cost you MANY, MANY times more gil to level your crafts and make the item, than it would to simply buy it off the auction house.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Why is crafting not recommended for new players?\010\010Due to the way crafting works, most items tend to cost less than the materials needed to craft them. This is because higher level crafters have a higher chance of creating a HQ (high quality) result, and will often craft a single item many times in the hopes of obtaining the HQ version. This often leads to an over-supply of the NQ (normal quality) version, which in turns ends up with people undercutting each other to try and recoup their gil, to the point that they take a loss on those items.\010As such, it is extremely difficult for a lower level crafter to compete with the over-abundance of higher level crafters. Since a low level is FAR less likely to create HQ results, they're forced to take a much bigger loss on most crafts.\010If, however, you are determined to level a crafting profession, you will need to find a good, steady source of income elsewhere to fund it.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Are any of the professions starter-friendly?\010\010If you're dead set of levelling a craft as a new player, then Cooking is generally considered the way to go. It is possible to level cooking up to 60 while making a small profit, but this still requires you to research the available recipes and check how much items are selling for on the auction house and at vendors, then carefully choosing what to craft.\010Woodworking and Alchemy aren't too expensive at low levels, but you may find it hard to make any profits until much higher levels.\010All of the other professions can be very expensive to level, especially Goldsmithing and Smithing.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Why can't I fish?\010\010Fishing requires that you have at least one job at level 15. This requirement was added on Horizon to deter fishing bots.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Does Horizon have fishing fatigue?\010\010No. Horizon does not have fishing fatigue.\010Instead we have a different system where each body of water has a set number of each fish that you can catch there. At certain hours of the day, that pool is restocked with a specific number of fish.\010So while you can continue to fish all day if you wish, you may find that some pools of fish may dry up if there's enough competition, meaning you will either need to wait for the next restock, or move to a different area to continue fishing.\010The restock times are 00:00 , 4:00 , 6:00 , 7:00 , 17:00 , 18:00 and 20:00 every in-game hour.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is HELM?\010\010HELM stands for Harvesting, Excavation, Logging, and Mining. It is a collective term for some of FFXI's gathering professions. Note that in addition to these, there is also Fishing, Chocobo Digging, Clamming, and Gardening.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Should I do gathering as a new player?\010\010Each gathering method has its own pros and cons. But most require some form of initial investment.\010Harvesting: Requires the Field gear set to be worthwhile. While wearing the three field gear pieces, your tools (Sickle) won't break. However, harvesting points are generally quite spaced out, and there aren't many good low level spots available.\010Excavation: Has no gear to boost its results, and has a high rate of breaking tools (Pickaxe). No good low level spots, and often doesn't have a great yield.\010Logging: Requires the Field gear set to be worthwhile. Tools (Hatchet can still break even with the field gear. Best done at Ghelsba Outpost as a new player once you're level 12+, but expect a lot of competition.\010Mining: Requires the Field gear set to be worthwhile. Tools (Pickaxe can still break even with the field gear. Best low level spot is in Zeruhn Mines where you can get Iron Ore and Darksteel Ore occasionally (can be done at level 1).\010Fishing: Requires a level 15 job to start, plus a bit of gil for a fishing rod and bait or lure. Takes a long time to level up, but after reaching level 1 fishing (or acquiring a piece of the fishing/angler's set) you can fish moat carps, which generally sell for around 2-3,000 per stack of 12.\010Chocobo Digging: Not really worthwhile for new players. Takes an insanely long time to level up, and is often very overcamped in profitable areas. Zones only have a set number of items per game day, so there is a lot of competition. Also requires level 20 for the Chocobo License, plus a lot of gil to spend of Gysahl Greens.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Should I do gathering as a new player?\010\010Clamming: I might be biased, since I wrote the clamming guides, but clamming can be a great money-maker for new players. It's non-competitive, and doesn't require a massive amount of gil to start. Does require a trek to Purgonorgo Isle in Bibiki Bay though, and a good understanding of the mini game to make it worthwhile. Yields slightly worse results if you don't have the clamming gear from the seasonal summer event (usually starts around August), but still worth a look-in if you're not into fighting over resources with other players.\010 Gardening is a good way to make some passive income as it requires very little effort to maintain. You do need to check your plants each day to stop them from wilting. And you'll need some gil to start off so you can buy flowerpots and seeds. The downside to gardening is it can take anywhere from 3 to 28 days depending on what seeds you're planting. You can only have 10 pots per character (30 per account if you're using all 3 allowed characters). So you\226\128\153re limited in how much gil you can obtain from gardening. That said, due to the fact that it requires very little time per day, it can still be worthwhile to supplement your income.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_1830",["title"]="New to XP Parties Guide",["page_title"]="New to XP Parties Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/New_to_XP_Parties_Guide",["revision"]="111092",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template Guide: ]\010This guide is a working progress but should hopefully contain enough info the get the general idea behind XP partys.\010Welcome to the guide. It's primarlily aimed at new players who are new to XP partys in FFXI.\010Generally players solo from levels 1 to 11 or 12 because quite simply it's the easiest way to gain XP at those levels. Once you hit the 11 or 12 mark, for many jobs it becomes quicker to gain XP in a group or a party.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Where to Party\010\010By far and large, the most common XP area at these levels can be found in the Valkurm Dunes. Depending on which nation you started in, you will take different paths to get there:\010 Bastok: South Gustaberg -> North Gustaberg -> Konschtat Highlands - > Valkurm Dunes\010 San d'Oria: West Ronfaure -> La Theine Plateau -> Valkurm Dunes\010 Windurst: East Sarutabaruta -> Tahrongi Canyon -> Buburimu Peninsula -> Mhaura -> Ship bound for Selbina -> Selbina -> Valkurm Dunes\010First of all you will notice that both Bastok and San d'Oria have far fewer areas to cross to reach Valkurm Dunes. Geographically Windurstrians are the furthest from the 'Dunes'. Don't be fooled though, all nations undertake a perilous journey, regardless of how far they have to travel.\010Once you journey out of the starter areas or 'zones' of Ronafaure, Gustaberg and Sarutabaruta, i would recommend NOT fighting any mobs, providing your sole purpose is to reach the Dunes. The difficulty of mobs significantly increases and they will likely kick your ass. I would give ALL mobs a wide berth as well, unless you know that they do not Aggro. Aggro is a game mechanic of FFXI where mobs begin attacking you for apparenty no reason. Usually the reason will be that they 'saw' you or 'heard' you. As long as you keep your distance, they'll have no reason to be upset. Install the distance addon if you need help staying 15m away for sight aggro, and 8m away for aggro by sound. Alternatively spells such as Sneak and Invisible or the items Silent Oil and Prism Powder will conceal you from 'aggro'ing'.\010When you finally reach the Dunes, you'll be looking for the map co-ordinate (G-9) where you will find the little town called Selbina. This will be your first place of safety and will be able to access your Mog House via a Nomad Moogle.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How to Find a Party\010\010To express the FFXI world that you wish to Party, you need to be Looking for Party. To do this, you need to go into the menus, select the party tab and then select the option 'seek party'. You should be a little green icon appear next to your name which means you've been successful in turning this on. Now any other player who is looking to form a party knows that you would like to join one.\010It may take some time for a party leader to invite you. Especially during non-NA prime time hours. DPS jobs tend to get slower invites than tanks, healers, and support jobs as well. \010You might want to consider starting your own party. Type /sea all 12-22 invite to pull up a list of players that are seeking a party between the levels of 12 and 22. You can then send them a /tell to ask if they want to join up. EX: (Hello!) (Party?) (Valkrum Dunes) (Level Sync) 15\010Check out EXP_Camps for where to party in the dunes if you find yourself starting a party.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Typical Party Set-ups\010\010So you've been invited to a party. The first thing you will notice that on the bottom right of your screen, where you normally see your HP, MP and TP, you will see these same stats for your party. If the party is full, there will be 6 names there. Your party leader will tell you where you will be 'camping' in Valkurm Dunes. If you ask nicely, someone will escort you to camp, as you all want to get there safely. Deaths en-route means less Experience Points overall after all.\010If you go back into the 'party' tab of the menus, you will now see a new option called 'party members'. In here you can find out further information about our party, mainly what job they currently are. A good party will have a variety of jobs. There are several roles within a XP party and you need to find the right balance to gain XP efficiently.\010The typical roles are:\010 Healer - (White Mage) RDM\010 Tank - (Paladin) WAR NIN \010 Support - (Bard) RDM SMN BLM/WHM or BLM/RDM\010 Damage Dealer - (Samurai) DRG NIN RNG BST DRK THF BLM MNK WAR\010 Puller (brings mobs to the camp for the party to fight)- Any Melee Damage Dealer with pebbles or a ranged attack, but typically THF or BRD\010(example jobs that fit these roles)\010A good party in the Dunes will have at least 1 healer, 1 tank, 1 support who can back-up heal and 3 damage dealers. Not all parties will fit into this criteria however as some jobs can cover more than 1 role. For example, a Black Mage is primarily a damage dealer but with white mage sub job, they can back up heal too. A Warrior is primarily a damage dealer but with a shield, can be used as a tank. Ultimately, as long as all these roles are covered, your party should be a good one.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_52608",["title"]="Ninja Equipment Guide",["page_title"]="Ninja Equipment Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Ninja_Equipment_Guide",["revision"]="120489",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template Guide: ] [Wiki: under construction]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="About this Guide\010\010This is basically the \"Essential Ninja Equipment Guide by Dribble\" (source on ffxiclopedia), adjusted for HorizonXI.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What no NIN gear guide?\010\010Yeah, that's what I thought. Instead you have a list of all the possible gear that a NIN can wear. Awesome...\010So, in the vein of this great guide, I decided to start a guide for NIN covering what special gear you really should have. By special I mean something with a worthwhile stat bonus, not just the next possible upgrade.\010The aim of the guide is to help you gear your NIN with confidence from about 15-75. If you want to get into serious endgame tanking and HNMs you will need more specialised gear than listed here.\010Likewise, \"career\" Ninjas might want to go that bit further and spend that bit more time and money. This guide isn't really aimed at you; this isn't meant to be a complete list of the very best gear. In fact, most of the very best gear isn't even mentioned. However, if you're interested in the very best gear you probably have your own opinions about what is and isn't the very best gear.\010Remember this is a guide; depending on your race or play style you might prefer different gear but this is a pretty good start!\010This guide should get you to 75 with a strong starting point. Where you go from there is up to you.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Notes\010\010<div class=\"wotg\">",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Red Hands, Blue Boots and Iron Rams\010\010If you don't have time to get the uber hands (Ochimusha Kote / Ochiudo's Kote / Horomusha Kote) and/or uber feet (Sarutobi Kyahan / Fuma Kyahan), you'll need to go with the Iron Ram Chainmail Set. The more pieces of this set you wear the bigger the bonus, so the benefits of wearing an individual piece are limited. If you do have the pimp hands and feet, skip the Iron Ram gear.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Fourth Set\010\010Fourth Haube Set could provide a very respectable, complete armour set for NIN or WS set. However, you do need to wear pretty much the whole set for the big bonuses. Also, unless you have the means to collect around 255,000 AN Allied Notes at a significant rate (i.e. a 75 melee job) it will take a long time to get the whole set!\010</div>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NQ vs. HQ\010\010I'll only list NQ versions of gear and not HQ. While HQ is almost always better, it's often amazingly rare and stupidly expensive. If you can afford it, craft it, borrow it or even steal it then by all means do. Of note, however, there are a few items mentioned that are HQ, and these items are not expensive. Additionally, the Bone/Beetle +1 items only provide their beneficial stat if they are the HQ version.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mission Rewards\010\010If your main job is Ninja, you'll know which to pick, so I left them out. Here's a list though: Suppanomimi [Horizon change] , Stealth Earring [Horizon change] , Rajas Ring (maybe Sattva Ring), Mirke Wardecors, Ethereal Earring (maybe Hollow Earring), and Ulthalam's Ring (maybe Balrahn's Ring). But, these are your choices to make, and if Ninja isn't your only 75, you'll have some decisions to make.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="The Guide\010\010Below is a list of recommended gear per slot. \010Rare/Ex gear is in italics\010Gear marked with \"Haste\", \"WS\", \"Ninjutsu\", or \"Enmity\" should generally be macroed-in as necessary.\010Gear marked \"TP/WS\" can be used to gain TP and to WS.\010Gear with *** is an item that needs a that little bit more time or money to achieve but is well worth it.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Weapons\010\010Level 15: Gassan\010Level 16: Mokuto +1\010Level 24: Hibari +1\010Level 28: Fukuro\010Level 30: Nikkariaoe\010Level 39: Ohaguro - Luxury\010Level 40: Anju / Zushio\010Level 41: Bokuto +1 \010Level 48: Sai\010Level 50: Buboso - Luxury\010Level 54: Muketsu +1 - Luxury\010Level 61: Kabutowari\010Level 66: Kororito\010Level 70: Fudo x 2\010Level 72: Senjuinrikio - *** (need to break the latent)\010Level 73: Perdu Blade - *** (off hand)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ranged/Ammo\010\010Level 15: Rogetsurin (Ranged)\010Level 40: Junior Musketeer's Chakram (Ranged) - Pulling\010Level 56: Bailathorn\010Level 63: Bomb Core\010Level 70: Ungur Boomerang - Evasion ***",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Head\010\010Level 2: Cache-nez - Enmity\010Level 14: Cotton Headband\010Level 24: Emperor Hairpin / Empress Hairpin - ***\010Level 26: Dodge Headband\010Level 43: Walkure Mask - WS\010Level 56: Ninja Hatsuburi - Ninjutsu\010Level 65: Wivre Mask \010Level 68: Fourth Haube - See notes above.\010Level 70: Optical Hat - ***\010Level 72: Panther Mask\010Level 75: Walahra Turban - Haste\010Level 75: Denali Bonnet - ***",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Neck\010\010Level 7: Wing Pendant\010Level 21: Spike Necklace - WS\010Level 33: Peacock Charm / Peacock Amulet - ***\010Level 38: Spirit Torque\010Level 59: Spectacles\010Level 60: Chivalrous Chain\010Level 65: Evasion Torque - Evasion\010Level 72: Harmonia's Torque - Enmity\010Level 72: Elemental Gorgets - WS ***\010Level 73: Hope Torque ***",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Earrings\010\010Level 10: Optical Earring\010Level 21: Beetle Earring\010Level 29: Dodge Earring\010Level 50: Velocity Earring\010Level 52: Melody Earring - Evasion Skill +5 when under Song or Roll.\010Level 54: Eris' Earring - Enmity\010Level 55: Fang Earring\010Level 61: Minuet Earring\010Level 70: Stealth Earring [Horizon change] \010Level 75: Brutal Earring - ***",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Body\010\010Level 13: Power Gi\010Level 16: Bone Harness +1\010Level 20: Mercenary's Gi\010Level 25: Windurstian Gi\010Level 40: Jujitsu Gi\010Level 45: Iron Ram Chainmail / Brigandine\010Level 50: Nokizaru Gi - Luxury\010Level 55: Royal Knight's Chainmail/Jaridah Peti\010Level 57: Scorpion Harness\010Level 58: Ninja Chainmail \010Level 59: Haubergeon - WS\010Level 68: Fourth Division Brunne - See notes above.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Hands\010\010Level 14: Guerilla Gloves - TP/WS\010Level 21: Beetle Mittens +1\010Level 25: Windurstian Tekko\010Level 34: Ochiudo's Kote / Ochimusha Kote - See notes above.\010Level 34: Horomusha Kote - See notes above.\010Level 45: Iron Ram Mufflers - See notes above.\010Level 55: Jaridah Bazubands\010Level 68: Fourth Hentzes - See notes above.\010Level 72: Dusk Gloves\010Level 72: Rasetsu Tekko - Evasion\010Level 72: Enkidu's Mittens - Luxury - TP/WS\010Level 73: Hanzo Tekko\010Level 74: Ninja Tekko +1 - Career goal - WS\010Level 75: Seiryu's Kote - Luxury\010Level 75: Denali Wristbands - Luxury",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rings\010\010Level 14: Reflex Ring\010Level 14: Courage Ring - WS\010Level 30: Rajas Ring - ***\010Level 34: Venerer Ring - TP/WS\010Level 35: Jaeger Ring - TP/WS\010Level 40: Sniper's Ring / Woodsman Ring - ***\010Level 43: Mermaid's Ring - Enmity ***\010Level 49: Ecphoria Ring - TP/WS\010Level 50: Garrulous Ring\010Level 50: Shinobi Ring - Haste\010Level 54: Sun Ring/Grace Ring - WS\010Level 72: Ruby Ring/Spinel Ring - WS\010Level 74: Flame Ring/Thunder Ring - ***\010Level 75: Iota Ring - TP/WS",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Back\010\010Level 12: Traveler's Mantle\010Level 21: Night Cape\010Level 24: Nomad's Mantle\010Level 30: Mercenary Mantle - Enmity\010Level 36: High Breath Mantle - Recommended to carry 2 at all times.\010Level 61: Amemet Mantle\010Level 70: Boxer's Mantle - ***\010Level 71: Corse Cape\010Level 74: Cuchulain's Mantle - Luxury - TP/WS\010Level 75: Cerberus Mantle",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Waist\010\010Level 15: Warrior's Belt +1\010Level 38: Survival Belt \010Level 41: Quick Belt - Haste\010Level 43: Swordbelt +1 - WS\010Level 44: Headlong Belt\010Level 48: Life Belt\010Level 50: Swift Belt - ***\010Level 51: Astral Rope - Enmity\010Level 54: Virtuoso Belt - TP/WS ***\010Level 60: Potent Belt - Luxury - WS\010Level 70: Koga Sarashi\010Level 71: Warwolf Belt - Enmity\010Level 73: Scouter's Rope",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Legs\010\010Level 16: Bone Subligar +1\010Level 25: Federation Sitabaki\010Level 25: San d'Orian Trousers / Bastokan Subligar\010Level 45: Iron Ram Breeches - See notes above.\010Level 52: Ninja Hakama\010Level 55: Royal Knight's Breeches - WS\010Level 64: Arhat's Hakama\010Level 68: Fourth Schoss - See notes above.\010Level 71: Koga Hakama\010Level 73: Shura Haidate - TP/WS ***\010Level 75: Byakko's Haidate - ***",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Feet\010\010Level 7: Leaping Boots / Bounding Boots - ***\010Level 16: Bone Leggings +1\010Level 25: Windurstian Kyahan\010Level 39: Fuma Kyahan / Sarutobi Kyahan - See notes above.\010Level 45: Iron Ram Sollerets - See notes above.\010Level 55: Jaridah Nails\010Level 63: Arhat's Sune-Ate - Enmity\010Level 68: Fourth Schuhs - See notes above.\010Level 72: Fuma Sune-Ate - Haste\010Level 72: Rasetsu Sune-Ate\010Level 75: Hachiryu Sune-Ate - ***",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_5081",["title"]="Notorious Monsters: Level Guide",["page_title"]="Notorious Monsters: Level Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Notorious_Monsters%3A_Level_Guide",["revision"]="103369",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010Notorious Monsters, or rarely known as Named Monsters (which is not true for all NMs), are enemies that check as Impossible to Gauge and drop a variety of useful items. Please double check that any NM listed on this page is implemented on HorizonXI.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Levels 1 \226\128\147 9\010\010colspan=\"5\" align=\"center\" |Levels 1 - 9\010width=\"24%\" |Name\010width=\"14%\" |Level\010width=\"24%\" |Zone\010width=\"14%\" |Family\010width=\"24%\" |Notable Drop(s)\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bigmouth Billy\010East Ronfaure\010Worm\010Colored Rocks\010rowspan=\"1\" |Jaggedy-Eared Jack\010West Ronfaure\010Rabbit\010Rabbit Charm\010rowspan=\"1\" |Stinging Sophie\010North Gustaberg\010Bee\010Beestinger\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bubbly Bernie\010South Gustaberg\010Crab\010Steam Clock\010rowspan=\"1\" |Spiny Spipi\010East Sarutabaruta\010Crawler\010Mist Silk Cape\010rowspan=\"1\" |Tom Tit Tat\010West Sarutabaruta\010Mandragora\010Fruit Punches",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Levels 10 \226\128\147 19\010\010colspan=\"5\" align=\"center\" |Levels 10 - 19\010width=\"24%\" |Name\010width=\"14%\" |Level\010width=\"24%\" |Zone\010width=\"14%\" |Family\010width=\"24%\" |Notable Drop(s)\010rowspan=\"1\" |Leaping Lizzy\010South Gustaberg\010Lizard\010Leaping Boots [Horizon change] \010rowspan=\"1\" |Fungus Beetle\010West Ronfaure\010Beetle\010Clipeus\010rowspan=\"1\" |Maighdean Uaine\010North Gustaberg\010Sapling\010Optical Earring\010rowspan=\"1\" |Carnero\010South Gustaberg\010Sheep\010Katayama\010rowspan=\"1\" |Sharp-Eared Ropipi\010East Sarutabaruta\010Rabbit\010Entrancing Ribbon\010rowspan=\"1\" |Thousandarm Deshglesh\010Ghelsba Outpost\010Orc\010Obelisk, Plate Belt\010rowspan=\"1\" |Hundredscar Hajwaj\010Fort Ghelsba\010Orc\010Coarse Leggings, Wild Cudgel\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bu'Ghi Howlblade\010Palborough Mines\010Quadav\010Marine Shield\010rowspan=\"1\" |Nunyenunc\010West Sarutabaruta\010Bird\010Pilgrim's Wand\010rowspan=\"1\" |Swamfisk\010East Ronfaure\010Pugil\010Gelong Staff\010rowspan=\"1\" |Juu Duzu the Whirlwind\010Giddeus\010Yagudo\010Hunter's Longbow, Light Soleas\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bloody Vrukwuk\010Ghelsba Outpost\010Orc\010Nothing. Strange mystery of Vana'diel.\010rowspan=\"1\" |Fogweaver Mozzfuzz\010Ghelsba Outpost\010Orc\010Nothing. Strange mystery of Vana'diel.\010rowspan=\"1\" |Haty\010Konschtat Highlands\010Hound\010Rogetsurin\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bendigeit Vran\010Konschtat Highlands\010Shadow\010Rogetsurin\010rowspan=\"1\" |Nihniknoovi\010La Theine Plateau\010Bird\010Van Pendant\010rowspan=\"1\" |Yara Ma Yha Who\010Tahrongi Canyon\010Sapling\010Fasting Ring\010rowspan=\"1\" |Orcish Wallbreacher\010Ghelsb