ttohwa Chasm\010Wyrm\010Adaman Ingot, Behemoth Hide, Damascus Ingot, Damascene Cloth, Beetle Blood, Divine Log, Dragon Blood, Dragon Heart, Dragon Talon, Malboro Fiber, Oxblood, Siren's Hair, Wyrm Horn, Herald's Gaiters, Noritsune Kote\010rowspan=\"1\" |Jormungand\010Uleguerand Range\010Wyrm\010Dragon Blood, Dragon Meat, Dragon Talon, Molybdenum Ore, Damascene Cloth, Divine Log, Oxblood, Mercurial Pole, Wyrm Horn",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NMs of Unknown Level\010\010colspan=\"5\" align=\"center\" |Level: Unknown\010width=\"24%\" |Name\010width=\"14%\" |Level\010width=\"24%\" |Zone\010width=\"14%\" |Family\010width=\"24%\" |Notable Drop(s)\010rowspan=\"1\" | Arioch\010Bostaunieux Oubliette\010Giant Bat\010Arioch Fang\010rowspan=\"1\" |Manes\010Bostaunieux Oubliette\010Ghost\010Taffeta Cloth\010rowspan=\"1\" |Dame Blanche\010Korroloka Tunnel\010Ghost\010Sarcenet Cloth\010rowspan=\"1\" |Falcatus Aranei\010Korroloka Tunnel\010Spider\010Webcutter\010rowspan=\"1\" |Enkelados\010Upper Delkfutt's Tower\010Gigas\010Enkelados's Bracelets\010rowspan=\"1\" |Sabotender Bailarin\010Quicksand Caves\010Sabotender\010Bailathorn\010rowspan=\"1\" |Brigandish Blade\010Ve'Lugannon Palace\010Evil Weapon\010Gem of the South, Libation Abjuration\010rowspan=\"1\" |Mother Globe\010Shrine of Ru'Avitau\010Spheroid\010Libation Abjuration, Oblation Abjuration, Shiranui, Springstone\010rowspan=\"1\" |Ogygos\010Middle Delkfutt's Tower\010Giga\010Ogygos's Bracelets\010rowspan=\"1\" |Ophion\010Middle Delkfutt's Tower\010Giga\010rowspan=\"1\" |Polybotes\010Middle Delkfutt's Tower\010Gigas\010Gigas Bracelets\010rowspan=\"1\" |Rhoikos\010Middle Delkfutt's Tower\010Gigas\010rowspan=\"1\" |Rhoitos\010Middle Delkfutt's Tower\010Gigas\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bistre-hearted Malberry\010Den of Rancor\010Tonberry\010Skirnir's Wand\010rowspan=\"1\" |Celeste-eyed Tozberry\010Den of Rancor\010Tonberry\010Kitsutsuki\010rowspan=\"1\" |Tawny-fingered Mugberry\010Den of Rancor\010Tonberry\010Uggalepih Necklace\010rowspan=\"1\" |Ogama\010Den of Rancor\010Doomed\010Amanojaku\010rowspan=\"1\" |Beryl-footed Molberry\010Temple of Uggalepih\010Tonberry\010Hototogisu\010rowspan=\"1\" |Swashstox Beadblinker\010Newton Movalpolos\010Goblin\010Darksteel Codpiece, Pachamac's Collar\010rowspan=\"1\" |Aipaloovik\010Phanauet Channel\010Pugils\010rowspan=\"1\" |Aiatar\010Riverne - Site A01\010Wyvern\010Falconer's Hose, Sable Cuisses\010rowspan=\"1\" |Imdugud\010Riverne - Site B01\010Hippogryph\010Heavy Lance, Knightly Earring\010rowspan=\"1\" |Tres Duendes\010Phomiuna Aqueducts\010Bat Trio\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NMs of Unknown Level\010\010Chiroptera Dagger, Niokiyotsuna, Vampiric Claws\010rowspan=\"1\" |Boroka\010Riverne - Site B01\010Hippogryph\010Auditory Torque, Boroka Earring, Horomusha Kote, Skulker's Cape\010rowspan=\"1\" |Carmine Dobsonfly\010Riverne - Site A01\010Fly\010Dobson Bandanna, Jaeger Ring, Voyager Sallet\010rowspan=\"1\" |Upyri\010Misareaux Coast\010Giant Bat\010Vampire Boots, Vampire Cloak, Vampire Earring, Vampire Mask\010rowspan=\"1\" |Jailer of Prudence\010Al'Taieu\010Hpemde\010Prudence Rod, Prudence Torque, Sixth Virtue\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bo'Who Warmonger\010Pashhow Marshlands\010Quadav\010Tortoise Shield\010rowspan=\"1\" |Alastor Antlion\010Attohwa Chasm\010Antlion\010Rostrum Pumps\010rowspan=\"1\" |Ambusher Antlion\010Attohwa Chasm\010Antlion\010Archer's Jupon\010rowspan=\"1\" |Chariotbuster Byakzak\010Fort Ghelsba\010Orc\010Auriga Xiphos\010rowspan=\"1\" |Dosetsu Tree\010Qufim Island\010Treant\010Raikiri\010rowspan=\"1\" |Nihniknoovi\010La Theine Plateau\010Bird\010Van Pendant\010rowspan=\"1\" |Serra\010Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle\010Pugil\010Volans Greaves\010rowspan=\"1\" |King Vinegarroon\010Western Altepa Desert\010Scorpion\010Venomous Claw, Ace's Helm, Heavy Shell\010rowspan=\"1\" |Rose Garden\010Yuhtunga Jungle\010Morbol\010Vilma's Ring\010rowspan=\"1\" |Heliodromos\010Riverne - Site A01\010Hippogryph\010Mountain Gaiters\010rowspan=\"1\" |Mahisha\010Phomiuna Aqueducts\010Tauri\010Sinister Mask\010rowspan=\"1\" |Goblin Collector\010Newton Movalpolos\010Moblin\010Barbarian Mittens\010rowspan=\"1\" |Haty\010Konschtat Highlands\010Hound\010Rogetsurin\010rowspan=\"1\" |Ereshkigal\010Xarcabard\010Ghost\010Lucent Cape\010rowspan=\"1\" |Shieldtrap\010Riverne - Site A01\010Flytrap\010Sennight Bangles\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bugbear Matman\010Newton Movalpolos\010Bugbear\010Rutter Sabatons, Sylvan Stone\010rowspan=\"1\" |Defoliate Leshy\010Lufaise Meadows\010Treant\010rowspan=\"1\" |Proteus\010Ferry - Nashmau/Al Zahbi\010Sea Monk\010Phantom Fleuret, Shark Necklace\010rowspan=\"1\" |Zoraal Ja's Pkuucha\010Wajaom Woodlands\010Colibri\010Zoraal Ja's Axe, Fowling Earring\010rowspan=\"1\" |Garfurlar the Rabid\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NMs of Unknown Level\010\010Mount Zhayolm\010Troll\010rowspan=\"1\" |Garhorlur the Brutal\010Mount Zhayolm\010Troll\010rowspan=\"1\" |Kirlirger the Abhorrent\010Halvung\010Troll\010Mercenary's Boots\010rowspan=\"1\" |Farlarder the Shrewd\010Halvung\010Troll\010Mercenary's Subligar\010rowspan=\"1\" |Gurfurlur the Menacing\010Halvung\010Troll\010Gurfurlur's Helmet, Mercenary's Ring\010rowspan=\"1\" |Medusa\010Arrapago Reef\010Lamiae\010Medusa's Armlet, Mercenary's Dastanas\010rowspan=\"1\" |Bloody Bones\010Arrapago Reef\010Skeleton\010Blackjack, Ace's Locket\010rowspan=\"1\" |Southern Piranu\010Ferry - Mhaura/Al Zahbi\010Orobon\010Spagyric Nails, Kugui\010rowspan=\"1\" |Almighty Apkallu\010Ferry - Nashmau/Al Zahbi\010Apkallu\010Sanguine Sword\010rowspan=\"1\" |Crystal Eater\010Aydeewa Subterrane\010Funguar\010Rapture Earring, Brigand's Mask\010rowspan=\"1\" |Zizzy Zillah\010Mamook\010Cockatrice\010Fencing Bracers, Namikirimaru\010rowspan=\"1\" |Dragonscaled Bugaal Ja\010Mamook\010Mamool Ja\010Beast Bazubands\010rowspan=\"1\" |Gulool Ja Ja\010Mamook\010Mamool Ja\010Volunteer's Ring, Ja Ja's Chestplate\010rowspan=\"1\" |Peallaidh\010Caedarva Mire\010Sheep\010Nightmare Gloves\010rowspan=\"1\" |Ravin Raven\010Caedarva Mire\010Bird\010Elm Log\010rowspan=\"1\" |Cyclopean Conch (Fishing)\010Manaclipper\010Uragnites\010Uragnite Shell\010rowspan=\"1\" |Harajnite (Fishing)\010Manaclipper\010Uragnites\010Harajnite Shell",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_55755",["title"]="Parry skillup guide",["page_title"]="Parry skillup guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Parry_skillup_guide",["revision"]="117426",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template Guide: ]\010Welcome to a guide on how to skill up parry, or as I call it: \"How I learned to stop caring and getting all blue for combat skills.\"\010This guide seeks to help others in skilling up Parry in the least painful way. To start with, lets review when parry kicks in:\010 Evasion > Parry > Shadows > Counter > Block\010As you can see, it extends out shadows and can diminish taking damage. NIN, SAM and THF (and COR) have , which caps at 269. It then drops to 225-200 for everyone else.\010To parry, your weapon must be drawn while facing the enemy (within a ~60 degree angle).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Powerleveling Parry\010\010Most people would tradionally advise going and soloing mobs to a point, but you'll have to rest at some point. This guide seeks to eliminate that and take advantage of some mechanics to actively speed up the process.\010In theory almost any 75 job can do this method, although BRD, PLD and RDM have bonuses for them. However, there are some caveats to this method:\010A 75 job with a RDM subjob to 37, capped at 109 , and a method of MP/HP restoration (Weaponskill/Auto Refresh/Ballad/Etcetera) will have a much easier time powerleveling Parry.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Physical Damage Taken (PDT) reduced to zero\010\010To start with, damage calculation goes:\010 Defense\195\183Crit > Physical Damage Taken (PDT) > Phalanx\010This may not mean much at first glance, but PDT is a percent reduction and stacking it will cause damage taken to drop fast. Example: An Earth Staff -20% PDT reduces a 100 damage hit to 80. So if we get high Defense and enough PDT we can lower damage a lot. \010Using common PDT gear, such as the Darksteel Harness +1 Set (-13% PDT), Earth Staff (-20% PDT) and Jelly Ring (-5% PDT), Svatta Ring (-5% PDT) and Cheviot Cape (-5% PDT)), you can attain -48% PDT, just two percentage points short of the cap.\010The closer you can stack PDT up to the -50% PDT cap, the better. Even only the set and staff together already adds up to -33% PDT.\010For a full list of equipment that affects Physical Damage Taken (PDT), see here.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Phalanx\010\010Then we add Phalanx. The short versiom is \"Damage reduction = Floor(Enhancing Magic Skill/10) - 2\". Base ---/RDM (75/37) Enhancing Magic is capped at 109. Meanining that Phalanx results in a flat -8 Damage Reduction. If you equip an Enhancing Torque its -9.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Optimal Route\010\010To maximIze skill-ups, as well as to reduce damage, we want to target MNK-type mobs. Which leaves us with two choices: Mandys or Beastmen.\010This is the approximate map I used:\010 Bubu Mandy - 15\010 Beastmen MNK - 21 (Yagudo Votary at Giddeus/Castle Oztroja)\010 Jungle Mandy - 35\010 Purongo isle mandy - 37\010 Beastmen MNK - 42 (Yagudo Zealot at Castle Oztroja)\010 Beastmen MNK - 47 (Yagudo Zealot / Orcish Gladiator / Spinel Quadav at Castle Zvahl)\010 Sea Serpent Grotto - 52 (Marsh Sahagin at Sea Serpent Grotto (Mythril door))\010 Tree- mandy lower level - 62\010 Beastman MNK - 64/66 \010 Tree- mandy upper level - 72 through 75",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Tips & Tricks\010\010So the trick to fast skill ups is to ideally get the mobs to hit 0 damage which up to level 37 is very easy. At 47 I could mostly produce 0s using DEF food and -38% PDT and -10 from Phalanx as a BRD, while facing off against 7 MNK Beastmen.\010I'd first test incoming damage by pulling one mob to see what the damage is like, then incrase the number of monsters to as many as you can engage and fit together, keeping the 60 degrees parrying arc in mind. We just turn it into a numbers game of them throwing out weak hits, waiting for skill-ups to roll in. Super easy up until 50s to refresh Phalanx before it wears off without interruption, so you just have to worry about how you will regain MP.\010At 50s against 62 mandys I started to take hits for about 8 - 12 damage each. Which made pulling multiple mobs riskier and harder to time Phalanx recasts as a BRD/RDM. I stuck to two mobs at a time from then on and if worst happened: Lullaby to recast Phalanx, use Paeon to regen HP back and equip Spirit Taker to restore MP. RDM or PLD should be able to push this to -22 dmg phalanx which at this level range should reduce incoming damag to almost 0 damage. \010The whole experience can be summerized as such: Get net damage to 0 (using DEF food, PDT, Phalanx and HP Regen) and have a way to restore MP for Phalanx.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_3120",["title"]="Party",["page_title"]="Party",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Party",["revision"]="111190",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Introduction\010\010In the Final Fantasy XI that we have all come to know and love, there is a very complex and intricate system of grouping with other players in order to gain Experience Points. As we all know, this system of partying occupies an extremely large portion of a character's advancement, and it becomes a very necessary activity early on in the game. In this guide we will attempt to deliver everything one should know about partying, from how to party, where to party, and who to party with. This guide is a work in progress, so please bear with us as we get going.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Overview\010\010Partying refers to the activity of teaming up with two or more players to form a team and work together to gain Experience Points or Merit Points. The two-six people in the group work together to defeat enemies that would otherwise be impossible to take down alone. The formation of the party involves a party leader who invites other members into their party and is expected to make all of the important decisions of a party.\010Note: One thing that can easily get missed is miscommunication of who is getting a Rep (replacement) it will usually be the Party Leader but to avoid issues sometimes of disruption people will quietly get their own replacement. Either way it is good to have communication between players if you are needing to leave to tell the Party Leader you need a Rep or that you have one coming as to help the party as a whole.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Party Leader\010\010The party leader is the person who invites the other players to become members of the party, and has control of Loot Distribution and party makeup. The party leader can kick and replace members at his/her discretion, and is in charge of the welfare of the entire party. While technically all a party leader has to do is to click the invite button on five other players and let things move along, a good party leader is expected to have a certain amount of leadership qualities:\010valign=\"top\" width=50%\"|\010A good party leader...\010Is familiar with party strategies, knows what jobs work well with others, and creates a party setup with a good balance of damage, tanking, and support.\010Is familiar with the skillchain chart and invites damage dealers that chain well together.\010Knows exactly where to camp and what to fight beforehand, and invites jobs that perform well in the selected area.\010Keeps tabs on how much longer each party member can play, and when one party member has to leave the leader can then make a decision on whether or not to get a replacement based on how much longer everyone else can play.\010Knows a proper use of power. This can typically be seen with suggestions on more efficient partying that are allowed to be debated, removing party members with justifiable causes; such as extremely detrimental effects to efficiency, failure to perform a required job, or does his job in such a way that it endangers the rest of the party, and potentially arbitrating conflicts that arise such as the availability of multiple skillchain possibilities.\010Keeps the levels of the party members close enough such that everyone gets the best possible EXP.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"|\010A bad party leader...\010Invites no tank, too little support, or too much tanking and support while not enough damage\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Party Leader\010\010Is unfamiliar with the skillchain chart and invites a seemingly random selection of damage dealers.\010Invites everyone without knowing where to camp and then asks: \"Where does everyone want to go?\"\010Sends tells asking if another player would like to replace a member of his/her party without knowledge of how much longer the party is going to last. This often results in the replacement player making all necessary preparations to arrive at camp only to find that the party is going to disband shortly.\010Will either exhibit minimal, erratic, or extreme control over the party. Minimal leadership is least likely to cause problems, but creates issues when someone is not performing in a correct manner and no one will rise to correct it. Erratic control is usually exhibited in actions such as attempting to correct something that is actually a correct method. Extreme control normally manifests in a leader that will remove members who disagree with his belief, cause conflicts with other members, or demand a certain method of battling even if it is less efficient.\010As you can see, being a good party lead er is not a simple task, but hopefully this guide will be able to provide you with everything you need to know in order to become a great party leader.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Partying Goals\010\010Strategy is one of the most flexible concepts of a party; but to begin with, we must define the goals of partying. The goals of partying are:\010To defeat enemies that provide a substantial amount of Experience Points quickly.\010'Note:Points Addon works great for showing exp/hour or skillchain # and time remaining to keep it going.To ensure that the members of the party do not get KO'd.Note: Not just the Healers job, everyone can help in their own position (i.e. Tank placement of mobs, DD's not doing unnecessary damage before tank gets enmity built a little, or support helping with healing when curve balls happen. Things will happen and can get unpredictable at times so it's up to everyone)\010To keep support characters' downtime to a minimum.\010To gain Experience Points at a satisfactory rate.\010A bad party can be defined as a party that does not meet the goals of the party, which is to gain Experience Points at a satisfactory rate. Other than gaining Experience Points at an unsatisfactory rate, bad parties often result in death and premature disbanding. Causes of a bad party include a number of factors, most of which can be traced back to poor leadership. Poor choice of job combinations and camp location can quickly lead a party to the dark side. Other causes of a bad party can be the presence of leechers, or players who do not contribute anything of value to a party and simply join to leech the experience points gained through the hard work of others.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Partying Goals\010\010A good party can be easily defined as a party that gains Experience Points at a satisfactory rate. All members of the party are doing their jobs well, and enemies are dying quickly while downtime is minimized. To describe how a good party works, we find it easiest to say that a good party consists of six people who are honestly trying their best to work together and achieve all of the party's goals.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Basic Strategy\010\010It's important to keep in mind that while most jobs can be modified to have multiple functions, some will always have their strong points in certain roles, such as a PLD and NIN being able to break even 1,000 damage with a weaponskill, a DNC being able to produce fairly good damage numbers, or a PLD being able to main heal. Keep an open mind while forming a party, but also ensure each member has both the modifications and the skill needed to perform the task at hand.\010That being said; The most commonly used successful party setups are as follows:\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Tank\010Quantity: 1\010Jobs: WAR, PLD, NIN, DNC\010valign=\"top\" width=\"75%\"|\010The member responsible for taking the hits from the monster. By usage of various abilities that produce enmity, the tank must work hard to constantly direct the enemy's attention away from other players (this involves keeping a mob being fought pulled to a spot away from any other mobs to aggro as to avoid fighting multiple at the same time or even where you are standing facing the mob away from other party members, some mobs have front cone attacks or even rarely rear cone attacks). A tank must also be able to survive all attacks directed against him/her and not place a heavy burden on the Healer at the same time. Being the tank in a party often entails placing the lives of your party members above your own, and while good parties usually do not run into extremely perilous situations, accidents can happen at anytime - in which the tank of the party should be able to quickly respond by allowing the rest of the party to retreat to safety even at the cost of his own Experience Points.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Healer\010Quantity: 1\010Jobs: WHM, RDM, SMN[Conditional wiki content: toau \226\128\148 , BLU], DNC, SCH\010valign=\"top\" width=\"80%\"|\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Basic Strategy\010\010The member responsible for keeping the entire party alive through the use of curative magic and beneficial status effects. While most of the healer's attention should be directed toward the tank of the party, they must also tend to the needs of all of the members as well. Playing the healer almost entirely revolves around watching the party's HP bars and one's own MP bar. A good healer will actively seek to do his/her job while expending the least amount of MP possible in order to reduce downtime. The relationship between the healer and the tank is always very direct - they must work together closely in order to ensure that neither player's role is too difficult or impossible.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Damage Dealer\010(DD)\010Quantity: 3\010Jobs: All except\010PLD, NIN, WHM, RDM, BRD[Conditional wiki content: wotg \226\128\148 , DNC]\010valign=\"top\" width=\"75%\"|\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Basic Strategy\010\010Members of the party responsible for dealing the lion's share of damage and defeating enemies. Members of this category must maintain high damage output and be able to consistently land blows on the enemy. The Damage Dealers are also responsible for setting up skillchains to lay the ground work for magic bursts for additional damage (Basic Chain: Open Weaponskill>Closing Weaponskill>Magic Burst) While all other players in a party work with the sole intent of keeping the party from losing Experience Points, the damage dealers are given the important task of driving the party forward - a task that is often underestimated by other players. Damage dealers focus on directly overcoming the defenses of the enemy and finding the most efficient way of defeating them in chains. Part of this task is to also establish a good understanding of the abilities and limits of the party's tank - as a damage dealer that attacks without restraint may very well attract the enemy's attention away from the tank and towards themselves. The result of this is most often additional difficulty for the healer, and sometimes fatal for the damage dealer.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Support\010Quantity: 1\010Jobs: RDM, BRD, SMN[Conditional wiki content: toau \226\128\148 , COR][Conditional wiki content: wotg \226\128\148 , DNC]\010valign=\"top\" width=\"75%\"|\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Basic Strategy\010\010Member of the party that facilitates the functions of a party. Able to increase the effectiveness of all other members of the party by enhancing the ability to deal damage, tank, and/or directly reduce downtime through usage of abilities that restore MP. The role of a support character is quite simply to support the other members of the party. While not having a direct impact on the goals of the party, a support character is just as important as the rest of the party. Having a support character around makes everybody's job easier - as one can easily enfeeble an enemy to deal less or take more damage, directly enhance a damage dealer's damage potential, or provide a safety cushion for healing when the healer has exhausted his/her resources.\010The above setup is what most experienced party leaders have in mind when forming a party. As you can see from the quantity ranges of each role, the setup of a party is never set in stone and can be manipulated within reason as long as the goals of partying are met. The roles are spread so that both damage and downtime can be balanced and met.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Additional Roles\010\010In addition to the roles detailed above, there are a few more roles that are commonly encountered during partying. Although important, these tasks can be considered minor, as they are secondary and must be performed without causing a player to come short in his/her primary role(s).\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Puller\010valign=\"top\" width=\"75%\"| \010The member responsible for seeking out and luring enemies to camp to defeat. The puller must always use discretion when performing this task, as they are constantly in almost direct control of the rate at which experience points are attained. A player assigned to this task must keep a watchful eye on the party's resources, and pull only when the party is ready to engage the next enemy. A skilled puller will be able to judge their party's preparedness accurately and push each player to their respective limits. This task is commonly assigned to a damage dealer with a ranged attack, although pretty much anyone can do it. Because of this, no character should ever be played to be a puller exclusively, as they will always best be replaced by someone who can pull as well as contribute to the party in another role. A puller must also know when enough is enough. If a puller pulls too fast for a certain party type, the party's performance can fall into that of a Bad Party. Pullers may also disengage from the current battle slightly before it finishes in order to ready themselves to pull the next target.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Additional Roles\010\010Puller duties tend to fall on THF first and foremost, and with the addition of Bully it's even more helpful as it guarantees the next status bolt effect to hit. (If you're very lucky a RNG is also an amazing puller but they have to stand at a specific distance from the mob the entire fight to do the most damage so they will probably not want to pull).\010While any attack of any kind can be used to pull, the ideal attack is a ranged weapon to save both Job Abilities and Magic Points so they can be used at the appropriate times. In Burn parties a SMN will \"pet pull\" with the avatar they're going to open the skillchain with, since Burn parties are reliant on a skillchain every pull 2 SMN use BP's(blood Pacts) to close for mages to magic burst. A good puller can also judge maximum pulling distance when using their ranged attack in order to put a good distance between them and the monster so they can safely make it back to camp. Note: that the ranged weapon does not need to hit the target to be effective. The ranged weapon is ideal because there are zones where mobs detect on magic (i.e. arcana), and a handful of mobs in ToAU zones that detect the use of job abilities (i.e. flans).\010Due to Japanese influence, who use the verb fishing for the act of bringing an enemy to camp, the puller is also referred as a fisherman in the party. It should be noted, when using the Auto-Translator function in a party with Japanese players, that fishing is the verb and fish is a noun referring to the aquatic creature.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Hate Control\010valign=\"top\" width=\"75%\"|\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Additional Roles\010\010A task that is made objective to someone besides the tank with the ability Trick Attack. This strategy simply involves a damage dealer with the Trick Attack ability calling upon the Tank and sometimes another party member to cooperate in order to give the Tank some additional enmity. THF is often invited into parties for this role purpose. See SATA for more details. With the addition of Accomplice and Collaborator for THF Hate can be controlled more easily when something goes wrong since things can and will happen while leveling. Accomplice steals 50% of a party/alliance member's enmity on to themselves, then can use Collaborator to direct that 50% enmity onto another party/alliance member which is usually the Tank.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"25%\"|\010Backup Healer\010valign=\"top\" width=\"75%\"| \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Additional Roles\010\010A backup healer is a good thing to have, but not always necessary in a party. This role usually falls on a job that has another main role, but has the capacity to heal (aka RDMs, mages that sub WHM, other jobs that sub WHM, and to a lesser extent PLDs). Summoners were usually also called upon to main heal or backup heal while they throw in some avatar bloodpacts, and past lvl 70 they are called upon to be intense damage dealing. With the increase to pet damage and job stability SMNs will usually only backup heal and not main heal. This QoL change pushed them into the support role sooner than it use to be, even the damage output is too nice to be pushed into just healing for a party now. It is this person's role to help the healer in dire situations, either when an unexpected attack drops a party member's HPs drastically, multiple party members need status effects removed simultaneously, or the main healer has run low on MP (or has otherwise become unable to heal) and needs assistance keeping the tank cured. These are most useful in parties that involve fighting monsters with AOE damaging attacks (when the use of Curaga isn't prudent), or fighting monsters with a variety of AOE status effects (ex. Coeurls). SMNs Whispering Wind [Horizon change] - Garuda 38+ is amazing for this in parties as a Horizon change added an erasega effect with the already potent Curaga 2 effect it has, and the best part, it generates NO enmity for Garuda or casting SMN. Amazing in a pinch",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level Difference\010\010Horizon uses the level sync system. All party member's need to be within 10 levels of the level sync to not loose a % of Experience Points being too high level. Only players that are above 10 levels of the sync will lose a % of exp. The main website at the top showing player currently online highlighted with a mouse (not on mobile) will show the current exp % drop starting at 10 levels above the sync. https://horizonxi.com/\010When recruiting members for a party, its a good idea to keep the levels of the members close. This suggestion made by Square-Enix is in fact quite vague in describing how \"close\" the members' levels should be. Suffice it to say that in a basic everyday party the member with the highest level should be at most two to three levels higher than the member with the lowest level. Ideally though, everyone in a party would be of the same level. The primary reason for keeping party members' levels close is simply to ensure that each member receives nearly the same amount of Experience Points per fight. \010A very important point for party leaders to recognize is the fact that the levels of the party's melee characters matter a great deal more than the party's mages. That is to say, Tanks' and Damage Dealers' ability to do their job is much more dependent on their character's level than Healers and Support characters. For tanks, character level has a very profound impact on his/her ability to mitigate damage and maintain hate. For melee damage dealers, character level has an extremely direct effect on their primary combat skill(s), and thus largely governs their ability to land blows and deal damage to enemies. \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level Difference\010\010Mages' performance, on the other hand, is not very related to their character's level at all. There is no difference between a Refresh spell cast by a level 41 Red Mage and one cast by a level 75. However, this does not mean mages' performance is completely unaffected by their level. Spells such as Elemental Magic or Enfeebling Magic resistance by an enemy is primarily determined by the skill level associated with the magic. A severely lower level mage will be attempting to land enfeebling spells or elemental magic with little accuracy. This will cause a noticable decrease in efficiency, since a Black Mage will have to cast more spells in order to inflict a full amount of damage. A Red Mage will have to cast an enfeebling spell multiple times to get its effect to \"stick\" to an enemy, or the effect of the enfeeble will wear off in half the time as it normally would. Both these circumstances mean that unweakened monster will exist longer, and add to the difficulty of the battle.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level Difference\010\010Even so, it becomes important when creating a party to devote some attention into making sure that the party's melee members are at the higher end of the party's level range, while it is acceptable to invite mages that are at a considerably lower level than the melee characters. While some players will outright refuse to party with a level difference of four or greater, knowledgeable players will accept the fact that the experience difference is based on the consensus reached by the party or party leader. As partying reaches the highest levels of experience, it is not uncommon to see mages in their high 60's partying with characters who are already level 75.Summary'Try to keep the levels of your party members as close as possible for a within 10 level, level sync(unless the person higher level out of range doesn't mind knowing the % drop they will receive).\010It is important to make sure that the melee/tank members of the party are at the higher end of the party's level range.\010It is considered acceptable to invite mages who are of a lower level than the level sync of the party.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level Difference\010\010Aside from difference in levels between party members, it is also important to pay attention to the difference in levels between the members of the party and the enemies the party is facing. This information can be ascertained by each member using the check command, and is very useful in discovering the limits of each party. In general, a basic successful party should be aiming to fight enemies that are 7 to 9 levels above its melee members. Its usually not a good idea to fight enemies that are higher than that, as each fight may take too many resources to be efficient and cause too much downtime. In some advanced strategies, however, the difference between party and enemy can be manipulated to meet the party's objectives, listed below.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Advanced Strategy\010\010Once the basics of partying are completely mastered, a player can begin to start using out of the box strategies that completely bend the rules of convention. Again, the basics of partying and cooperation must be very familiar to any player before attempting to try any of the below listed strategies.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Dual Tanking\010\010This is a technique often used by a pair of players with the ability to use both Provoke and Utsusemi: Ichi, more specifically WAR/NINs and NIN/WARs who have not yet acquired Utsusemi: Ni. The general idea is as follows: \010One player attracts the attention of the enemy for as long as his Utsusemi can last.\010The second player then uses Provoke and tanks for as long as his shadows last. \010While the enemy is attacking the second player, the first player then has the opportunity to recast his Utsusemi. \010Once recast, the first player will then pay close attention to the status of the second player's shadows, and as soon as the shadows are gone, they will then use Provoke to regain the attention of the enemy. \010This cycle repeats itself until the enemy is defeated.\010While Ninjas quickly outgrow this technique at the early age of level 37, WAR/NINs may continue to utilize it throughout most of their career. This is an extremely effective technique in the sense that it allows both Warriors to fully exercise their potential as damage dealers, and eliminates the party's need for an exclusive tank who does not contribute as much damage to the enemy. However, in addition to the skill necessary to master the timing of Provokes and the casting of Utsusemi, this technique also requires that both players maintain very close enmity levels via damage. If one player is doing a lot more damage than the second, it will be very difficult for the second player to attract the enemy's attention while the first player is out of shadows. Therefore, this strategy is not recommended to players who have not yet obtained a good grasp of the basic mechanics of the game, and can really only be utilized by two players who have a good amount of experience, skill, and equipment.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Combo Support\010\010Just as Dual Tanking removes the party's need for an exclusive tank, the Combo Support tactic eliminates the party's need for a designated healer. The basic concept is simple: include both a Red Mage and a Bard in a level 41+ party. Refresh and Ballad songs together provide up to 6 MP every 3 seconds of constant MP recovery, and nearly eliminate all downtime caused by healing for MP. The Red Mage handles the main healer role, while the party gains all the regular Bard party buffs and Red Mage enemy debuffs for easier battles.\010Another, similar tactic is the use of two SMNs, who trade off casting Defensive Blood Pacts, usually Aerial Armor, Ecliptic Growl, Ecliptic Howl, and Earthen Ward while simultaneously curing the party and debuffing the target. This strategy requires careful management of MP and a talented tank, however.\010The downside compared to a White Mage plus support job is the lack of Raise II/Raise III, the wide range of status ailment removal spells, and the MP efficient curatives like Cure V, and Regen II/Regen III.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Speedkill (aka \"Burns\")\010\010This tactic is an extremely popular method to gain Experience Points for players at lv75, but it most certainly is not limited to level 75 players. The simple idea behind Speedkill is to target enemies that are only four to seven levels above the party's members, and to kill them extremely fast, hence the name. This method is known for its ability to produce seemingly impossible numbers of Experience Chains, as well as its almost complete disregard for enmity control.\010Speedkill starts with a team of highly skilled and powerful damage dealers who can not only deal damage, but can also survive a decent amount of hits. This usually means melee jobs such as Monk, Warrior, and Samurai, although other jobs can certainly participate and perform well. Dual Tanking is a tactic very commonly used in conjunction with speedkill. Put them together with a small amount of mage support - usually two mages for Combo Support, although other combinations work as well depending on player skill, and you've got yourself a speedkill party.\010In this kind of situation, there need not be a single designated tank, puller, nor camp. Enmity is bounced around between the damage dealers of the party constantly, and enemies always die before they are given the opportunity to inflict severe damage on anyone. After each fight, each member of the group spreads out to an extent in order to locate the next target instead of having one person pull, and the party itself will move as a whole. A classic example of Speedkill are the Monk-based parties in King Ranperre's Tomb, where skeletons are quickly turned into bone chips one after another at an alarming rate.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Speedkill (aka \"Burns\")\010\010When performed skillfully, the rate of Experience Points in a Speedkill party can far outdo that of a traditional party. However, in addition to the heavy skill requirement necessary for success, finding good areas to use this method is also not an easy task. Due to the extremely fast enemy after enemy nature of this method, it is very important that enough enemies are available to kill. Running out of enemies to defeat is a very large problem that will hamper such a party's success.\010See Partying: \"Burn\" Parties for examples of commonly used speedkill or \"burn\" parties.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_20131",["title"]="Picking your Coffers and Chests",["page_title"]="Picking your Coffers and Chests",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Picking_your_Coffers_and_Chests",["revision"]="50993",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]\010Picking your own coffer or chest can (obviously) save you a lot of time farming for a key! But it comes with other hassles and worries...There are dangers to consider when picking your coffers and chests.\010First off, only a thief can pick coffers/chests. Not sub-thief, but main thief. And you need to get some tools to pick your coffers/chests. Not all picking-tools are created equally, so you may want to experiment...",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="The Tools\010\010Thief's Tools: These are the cheapest tools. These can be crafted with Goldsmithing, Smithing, and Woodworking or purchased from a vendor when your nation is in 2nd or 3rd place in Conquest.\010Living Key: These cost more than Thief's Tools. Crafted with Alchemy skill or purchased from a vendor only when your nation is in last place in Conquest. Alchemy skill is not required to use them.\010Skeleton Key: These can be quite expensive, as they cannot be purchased from vendors. Crafted with Bonecraft and Alchemy.\010A thief can also get 5 free Skeleton Keys (one every 30 minutes) from the quested item: Key Ring Belt. This is a possible reward for a quest in Bastok called Bait and Switch. Choose \"Lucky Charm\" at the beginning, and you will be able to get the Key Ring Belt. Quest can be repeated once a week; you do not need to drop the Key Ring Belt to repeat or finish the quest, but you can only ever receive one belt a week. If you have a Belt upon completing the quest, it will remain at the ??? until you drop the belt you currently possess, and you cannot flag the quest again until picking it up.\010Doesn't seem to be any correlation between any of the key's working better than the others.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="The Picking\010\010The basics of it are that you go to the zone, find the coffer/chest, trade your tool-of-choice to the coffer/chest, and hopefully it works! If it doesn't work, one of 3 things can happen:\010Nothing, it just says you failed to open the coffer/chest and it continues to sit there waiting for you to try again!\010You receive Weakness, which can last anywhere from five minutes to three hours. During this time you can not try to pick the coffer/chest again, you have to wait for it to wear. In order to get rid of it more quickly, you can allow yourself to be KO'd -- returning to your home point will remove the weakness entirely, or getting a Raise will give you a 5-minute weakness as normal.\010A Mimic spawns |  This is not good when you are alone and you probably want to get out of there as fast as you can, or get KO'd. More on Mimics can be found below.\010In any of these cases, the lockpicking tool used is consumed.\010Note: Since the Dec. '08 update, the chest may also vanish with a message about it being \"only an illusion.\" In this case the lockpicking tool is NOT consumed and the chest will respawn elsewhere in the zone after a short delay. This will only occur if someone has recently opened a chest in the zone that wasn't for Artifact armor or a key item/quest, and you are attempting to do the same. Opening a chest for AF or a key item will never cause the chest to be an illusion.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Failure?\010\010There seems to be a strong correlation between the success of a pick and your level to that of the mobs in the zone. Basically, to have a good success rate, you want to be near the level of the mobs in the zone. You will probably fail over and over again if you were a 20 thief picking coffers in Castle Oztroja. But as a 58 thief you should have a much better success rate. (See talk page for discussion)\010Once you've failed a picking, you will lose the tool that you used. So it's possible to go through a stack of tools and not succeed.\010Chests as a general rule of thumb tend to be a bit easier to pick than coffers.\010Mimics only spawn from failed coffer picks. They can last in a zone for quite a bit of time.\010Popping a mimic does NOT start your cooldown, so you may go pop again.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Additional Notes\010\010After a coffer/chest has been picked, it can take for 15-45 minutes to respawn. They have also been reported to respawn almost instantly, although this is a rare occurrence.\010If you get a critical failure (i.e. Mimics from failed coffer picks & trap), the coffer/chest can respawn immediately (usually between 5 to 15 minutes).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="More on Mimics\010\010They will instantly do an AoE attack that does Poison and/or Stun. This attack is called Death Trap, and Mimics will also use it when they have TP. The poison effect is pretty strong and lasts for a long time.\010You can't run from them - they have an ability that draws you in. It basically transports you from your running-away to right in front of it again. Even Flee can't stop it.\010Recommended Solo Tactics: Only recommended if Evasion, Marksmanship skills are capped. If the level difference is too great, use the recommended escape as described below. Hit Perfect Dodge and use an antidote (Even better if its macro'd). Pop accuracy food such as sushi, and reposition yourself. Use Perfect Dodge duration to apply blind bolt and acid bolt. Mimics have high evasion and good accuracy so defeating it quickly is necessary, as nearby mobs may join in the fight. Blind bolts have a noticeable effect of lowered accuracy and are critical as Mimic attacks are fast. Apply sleep bolts when needing healing or for Utsusemi recast. \010Recommended escape: Hit Perfect Dodge and use a Scroll of Instant Warp (or some other Warp item). Run as fast as you can from your Home Point to your Mog House to get rid of lingering poison effects.\010A party of level 60's should be able to kill it. It is possible for a 75 THF/NIN with antidotes and status bolts to solo mimics in most zones, depending on where that mimic is located.\010If it's killed, a coffer key of the current area will drop. This is one guaranteed method of acquiring coffer keys.\010When it is killed, a coffer will spawn immediately somewhere else in the area (or possibly, at the same spot).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_10430",["title"]="Promyvion Guide",["page_title"]="Promyvion Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Promyvion_Guide",["revision"]="120763",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]\010This page has some Out of Era information on it so use this guide with caution.\010\226\154\160\239\184\143(All 3 starter Promyvions MUST be completed before you can repeat one you have beaten already)\226\154\160\239\184\143",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Short form of the Emptiness access quests:\010\0101) Visit Lower Delkfutt's Tower. Cutscene as you zone in. \0102) Visit Upper Jeuno. Cutscene as you zone in. \0103) Go to the Infirmary (Upper Jeuno (G-9)), find and talk to Monberaux, the doctor. Cutscene. \0104) Go to Ru'Lude Gardens and head into the palace. Proceed left around the main stairwell and enter the door to the guard post. Talk to Pherimociel for the last cutscene you need. \0105) Go visit a Shattered Telepoint. Each of the three main crags has one. If this is your first visit, or if your memory is erased, you'll be prompted twice before entry. \0106) You'll spawn in the Hall of Transference. If this is your first time inside, talk to the Large Apparatus. If you have been in this Promyvion before, you can walk up to the Cermet Door and talk to it for entry.\0107) After the first time you enter a Promyvion you can talk with Harith in Ru'Lude Gardens (H-5), Archduke's House to make Animas. See the quest Empty Memories for further details.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="General details about the Emptiness:\010\010You're going through this stuff because it's the only way to access the Tavnazian Archipelago. If you ever want to get to that part of the world, this is the way. It's CoP's version of the Kazham Key quest. \010 Experience will be earned here. If you are at or below level 30, you will earn experience as you normally would. If you are above level 30, your experience is reduced. All experience earned is applied to your main job. All deaths received carry a Lv30 penalty (456 experience after returning to your Home Point). The EXP penalty does not apply while inside the BC.\010 Promyvion zones are keyed upon entry; if you've gone into Dem, you can skip the entry cutscenes by talking to the Cermet Door in Dem. Conversely, you can't warp directly to Mea and enter the crystal there. You will not receive a message explaining your inability to enter. If you want to leave the Promyvion you're working on and go elsewhere, use the Large Apparatus on your way out. The Apparatus will erase your memory, allowing you to enter any Promyvion zone.\010 After a recent update, it is no longer necessary to erase the memory of a prior Promyvion at the specific crag. Instead, you will get a brief message when attempting to enter another Shattered Telepoint, asking if you wish to seal the memory of the other Promyvion. From there, you can enter as normal.\010 After defeating a Promyvion area, you can freely enter that area through the Cermet Door at any time, whether or not you have erased your memories from another Promyvion area.\010 Erasing your memory does not remove drops acquired, experience earned, or key items obtained. It is only there to untie you from a given Promy zone. \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="General details about the Emptiness:\010\010 Contrary to popular belief, you do not go \"up\" or \"down\" in Promyvion; you travel across, anyone with a map of any Promyvion can see this.\010 Enemies in Promyvion are called 'the Empty'. There are several classes, detailed below. \010 Each Promyvion has four floors of enemies. \0101st: Easy Prey to Tough \0102nd: Decent Challenge to Very Tough \0103rd: Tough to Incredibly Tough \0104th: Tough to Incredibly Tough\010 To go across a floor, you must find and defeat a Memory Receptacle. MRs show up on Wide Scan. MRs always appear on platforms, and the platform locations are fixed (thus memorizable/mappable). When you beat an MR, if you're fortunate, a portal to the next level will appear. Some MRs will not generate portals. More MR details are found below. \010 MR Portals last for three minutes. Do not panic and rush the portal, lest you leave someone behind. Make sure you take the time needed to clear any residual Strays before crossing over into to the next level. \010 If you need to go back across a level, search for a platform that has a live portal but no MR sitting on it. That will be the portal back across. Try not to get separated from your group! \010 The fourth floor does not have an MR; instead, you must find and enter the Spire. The Spire is a tower-like structure, and has an archway that you zone through. It looks like a castle (sorta) and it fades into the background, making it unobvious that it is a zone. But once you realize what it looks like, it is easier to find.\010 The Spire is an empty room with a door at the end. The door acts like a Burning Circle; talking to it lets your party enter for a BCNM battle against the Promyvion's mega-boss. The level cap is reapplied when the battle begins and all non-food buffs will be removed. \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="General details about the Emptiness:\010\010Enemies drop several types of items, but the most important are the Recollections. These items can be synthed into special animas by Harith (found in the Ru'Lude Gardens in the Duke's house past Maat). Farm recollections and make the special animas before the boss fight. Farming these should be mandatory! See the appropriate section below for more information. \010 Boss fights are single-party only. If you went up with a partial alliance, someone is going to sit out during the fight. This can be good or bad... \010 If you are defeated by the boss, you are ejected back into the Spire's lobby after three minutes have passed. If someone is there, they can Raise you. If you win, your party is teleported back outside the Shattered Telepoint (or to Lufaise Meadows after you've completed your third Promyvion). If someone is left behind in the Spire, the only way to retrieve them is to go back across the four floors. (It pays to bring an Instant Warp scroll.) \010 The Memory Flux gates are not used during the original Promyvion runs. Don't worry about them, as they involve the quest \"Shadows of the Departed\".",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What enemies will I encounter?\010\010Each Promyvion zone has three regular enemies, plus an additional crag-specific beast. Do not bother with Sneak or Invisible, as all of the mobs are active on True Sight. It is possible, therefore, to walk behind them on the lower levels. The mobs on the higher levels are also active on True Sound. The best bet is to give them their right of way.\010Empty monsters do link when pulled, but only with other members of the same type. For example, if your puller shoots into a crowd of two Wanderers and two Weepers, and hits one of the Wanderers, only the other Wanderer will link in. It is unclear whether sound or some other Empty-specific detection mode causes the link, but it does have a fairly small radius.\010The crag-specific enemies are important, as the NM in each crag is based off that class, as is the boss waiting in the Spire. If you want to learn how to fight the boss, spend some time fighting the crag-specific enemy - the boss typically gets one extra special attack. Not that it matters; you should be slamming it with Hysteroanima instead of eating its special attacks.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Normal Enemies:\010\010Wanderer: Floating wisps of memory; these are the most common creature and are sometimes found in packs. \010Special Attacks\010Aura of Persistance - Defense boost. \010Mirage - Evasion boost. \010Vanity Drive - Single target damage attack.\010Empty Beleaguer - AOE damage attack.\010Weeper: These look like dinosaurs; specifically, like a tiny stegosaurus. Quite common and often found near Wanderers. \010Special Attacks\010Hexagon Belt - Defense boost. \010Empty Cutter - Single target damage attack. \010Vacuous Oscillation - Damage attack + Poison + Plague, one target. \010Auroral Drape - AOE Silence + Blind. \010Empty Kiss - Damage (target unknown), Poison + Disease. \010Memory of <Element> - AOE Elemental damage based on the Weeper's core; be careful fighting a green Weeper on Windsday, etc. I've seen this spammed fairly hard before. \010Seether: Walking eyeballs. Less common but still appear from time to time. Most parties refuse to fight these. \010Special Attacks\010Occultation - Blink-like skill, unsure of total 7 (at least or 9, need confirmation) of shadows. \010Vanity Strike - Single target damage attack. \010Empty Crush - Single target high damage attack. \010Lamentation - AOE damage attack.\010Wanion - AOE Transferrance; any debuffs stacked on the Seether are pushed to your party.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Crag-specific enemies:\010\010(4th floor \"Spire\" Boss of eacah area. Although there are smaller weaker versions on lower floors)\010Gorger (Dem): Large ticks, fat and lumbering. Possibly badly made copies of Konschat's sheep.\010Special Attacks\010Stygian Flatus - AOE Paralyze. \010Quadratic Continuum - Single target damage attack. \010Promyvion Barrier - Defense boost. \010Craver (Mea): Sort of an obscene Daddy Longlegs, looks kind of like a skull-less, rib-less dhalmel skeleton. They stand straight and tall; the central leg is a whip-like tail. These can be a pain to fight. \010Special Attacks\010Material Fend - Evasion boost. \010Brain Spike - Single target damage attack.\010Empty Thrash - Single target high damage attack.\010Murk - AOE Slow + Gravity. \010Promyvion Brume - AOE damage + poison + hate reset. \010Thinker (Holla): Sort of reminds me of the living trees in Jugner, but more squat in appearance. Two clearly defined eyes. \010Special Attacks\010Winds of Promyvion - Erase. \010Stygian Vapor - AOE Plague (sets TP to 0, stops TP growth). \010Trinary Absorption - Drain, one target.\010 Uses Binary tap - It can absorb 2 of your buffs, like protect II and shell.\010Shadow Spread - AOE Sleep + Blind + Curse",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Notorious Monsters:\010\010On 12/9/2004, NMs were added to the Promyvion zones. The NMs always appear on the third floor of the zone. The NMs can range, and have no predefined area or movement pattern.\010The NMs drop a rare/ex Remnant keyed to their type, and may also drop Memospheres for that crag. See the relevant sections below. \010The NMs are hard, but can be beaten by a party of six; alliances should worry only about the AOE skills. Animas cannot be used against the NMs. My Promy strike team was able to take the Coveter and the Cerebrator in the same evening. The fights are hard; there isn't much difference between the NM and the boss, save for a single skill and the boss having a few more HP. NMs are significantly harder than their crag-specific counterparts. \010Satiator (Dem) (Gorger type) \010Drops: Remnant of a Satiator, Beryl Memosphere \010Special Attacks\010Promyvion Barrier - Defense boost. \010Vanity Drive - Single target damage attack.\010Quadratic Continuum - Single target damage attack.\010Coveter (Mea) (Craver type) \010HP: ~3,600 (thanks, Prim) \010Drops: Remnant of a Coveter, Indigo Memosphere \010Special Attacks\010Material Fend - Evasion boost. \010Brain Spike - Single target damage attack.\010Empty Thrash - Single target high damage attack.\010Murk - AOE Slow + Gravity. \010Promyvion Brume - AOE damage + poison + hate reset. \010Impalement - Single target huge damage attack. Damage can vary. I've seen it hit for 100, I've also seen 500. Throat-stab type? \010Cerebrator (Holla) (Thinker type) \010Drops: Remnant of a Cerebrator, Teal Memosphere\010Special Attacks\010Spirit Tap - Steals 1 status effect from one target. \010Binary Tap - Steals 2 status effects from one target. \010Trinary Tap - Steals 3 status effects from one target. \010Spirit Absorption - Single target Drain.\010Binary Absorption - Single target Drain.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Notorious Monsters:\010\010Empty Cutter - Single target damage attack.\010Negative Whirl - AOE damage attack.\010Stygian Vapor - AOE Plague + hate reset. \010Winds of Promyvion - Erase. \010 Utsusemi doesn't do a thing versus these attacks, as the enemy steals your shadows for itself.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Enemy core-types:\010\010Pay attention to the core of the Empty; by noting the color, you can tell what its elemental properties are\226\128\148and from that, you'll know what weakness it has. The excellent photos below are from Prim:",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I reach the Spire?\010\010To reach the Spire you must locate and fight Memory Receptacles (or \"MRs\"). MRs are large glowing balls that sit on the various platforms found in Promyvion. An MR will have a number of Strays surrounding it. Strays look like Wanderers, and appear as a clustered group. The Strays around the MR will also be a bit weaker than the ones you'll find adrift.\010When you arrive at an MR, it will not attack, though its Strays will aggro. The MR remains inactive until engaged. Clear the Strays first, then set a camp to do battle from. \010MRs fight using two techniques. First, they'll spawn Strays throughout the fight. You can see this happen when the MR lifts itself up into the air. Second, the MR has an attack called Empty Seed. This is a high range AOE damage skill with an incredible knockback effect. This attack is preceded by a bright glow. It can be dangerous to get slapped by if there are enemies nearby. This can be prevented by fighting in front of the 4 pillars. The mages are advised to be in a safe position (e.g. near a border where the knockback effect won't push them around).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Best practices\010\010If you are a damage dealer, get on the platform with the MR. Set your back to one of the four pillars on the platform; this will keep you in melee range and prevent the knockback effect from sending you flying backwards. It will also keep you conveniently close to one another (for Curaga spells). Empty Seed can also be blocked with Utsusemi, though rarely (Empty Seed can take off between 1 and 3 shadows or strip them all as well as cause damage, it can also be completely evaded). \010A Red Mage is completely capable of soloing every Stray created during the battle</s>.\010A Red Mage will die quickly if you let him solo every stay, at 3rd flood a pld can die in few seconds if every stray are on him.\010For every MR fight select one player, who can kill a stray in 1-2 attacks and don't die so easily, to handle these strays. At 2nd level, a single dd can handle them, but be sure that a 2nd one is ready in case of emergency. For 3rd level, 2players killing stray are better, and a 3rd ready in case of emergency. For example, very low hp or strays on mage.\010If you are careless about healing, you can fall quickly because of the Strays, so make sure each person knows their job.\010Keep up the damage and the MR will fall. As soon as it dies, the main combat team must engage and defeat any remaining Strays. Once the platform area is clear, a portal may or may not appear to carry you across. \010If you are in an overstrength alliance, it pays to know that you don't have to call for help on a Receptacle fight. All available players may engage and kill the MR together. \010If you use a Reraise item, the effects will be wiped as soon as you zone from the 4th Floor. Furthermore, if you use Reraise again, once inside the BC, the effects will be wiped when you leave after the fight, so plan accordingly.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Best practices\010\010\"Fake\" Receptacles \010Sometimes you'll kill an MR and it will not give you a portal. You will have to kill a new MR to continue across. Sometimes they spawn on other platforms; sometimes one will respawn on the same platform you just fought. The rate of portal spawns is unknown, but is keyed only to beating the MR - not to a particular platform.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What kinds of drops can I expect, and what do they do?\010\010There are four types of drops that occur in Promyvion. Note that Rangers cannot Scavenge here.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Remnant of a Memory\010\010There are eight types, one for each element in the game. Alchemists are able to synth these into Elemental Animas, which are used in a variety of other tasks. The recipes for Elemental Anima are Alchemy Lv75~ and require the guild Key Item Anima Synthesis. Here's the breakdown for which types synth to which elements: \010Remnant of a Profane Memory\010Earth\010Remnant of a Fleeting Memory\010Wind\010Remnant of a Bitter Memory\010Ice\010Remnant of a Burning Memory\010Fire\010Remnant of a Somber Memory\010Water\010Remnant of a Startling Memory\010Lightning\010Remnant of a Radiant Memory\010Light\010Remnant of a Malevolent Memory\010Dark",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Memospheres\010\010Memospheres are what give you the map to a given Promyvion. To get the map, you must locate the ??? point inside the Promyvion zone. This point will be inside a blue structure (called a Memory Flux); one of Promyvion's \"streetlights\". It is surrounded by enemies. \010Memospheres may fall very rarely from the crag-specific enemies; Gorgers in Dem, Cravers in Mea, Thinkers in Holla. They also drop (100%) from the Notorious Monsters. \010There is a different color Memosphere for each Promyvion. The colors and associated crags are listed: \010Promyvion - Dem: Beryl Memosphere \010Promyvion - Mea: Indigo Memosphere \010Promyvion - Holla: Teal Memosphere \010The ???-point for the map is not in a fixed location. It is most always one of the heavily guarded lamp-like objects out in the zone, but which one gets the ???-point is a matter of chance.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Recollection of Guilt, Fear, and Pain\010\010These are the most important of the four drops! These are what you'll want to go into Promy for to farm up before taking a shot at the bosses. Recollections are memories that can be exchanged for special animas. The special animas act like anti-boss weapons. \010It seems that each Promyvion has a higer drop rate for a given Recollection.\010The special animas can be used like any item or food would be; the target will be the Boss instead of yourself.\010Macro syntax: /item \"Psychoanima\" <t> \010There are three types of recollections, and three types of animas to be made: \010Recollection of Guilt -=> Terroanima \010The enemy will become afraid and run around. Good as a defense if you draw unfortunate aggro. You can also try to ElemSeal+Bind and pound on the enemy; he will not fight back while he's trying to flee. \010Recollection of Fear -=> Psychoanima\010The enemy will be intimidated for the duration. This will pre-empt any normal attacks. Special attacks may still occur while Psychoanima is in effect. If you are able to use Psychoanima before the NM triggers a physical attack skill (such as Carousel), the attack will be negated, despite the animation that still occurs.\010Recollection of Pain -=> Hysteroanima \010The enemy will attempt to use special attacks, but they will not execute. This is a fantastic anima, as it prevents the boss from swarming you under with their most powerful attacks. Sadly there is no message to let you know that Hysteroanima is working, nor any to let you know when it stops. \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Recollection of Guilt, Fear, and Pain\010\010These effects of these special animas stack. If you farm up Psychoanima to go with your Hysteroanima you can shut down Physical and Special attacks for 25-30 seconds at a stretch. As long as you can do sufficient damage to the boss and kill it before you're out of animas, you will do well. However, if you wish to stack either one of the three and you throw either one first, the next one thrown will negate the first anima.\010Some Recollections are easier to farm in certain crags due to the way they drop. Wanderers will drop one Recollection, Weepers drop another, Seethers drop the third. Seethers can be found only on the second floor and higher and are much more difficult to fight; it may be to your advantage to change to an easier crag to farm a particular Recollection. \010Promyvion - Dem \010Wanderers: Recollection of Pain (Hysteroanima) \010Weepers: Recollection of Guilt (Terroanima) \010Seethers: Recollection of Fear (Psychoanima) \010Promyvion - Mea\010Wanderers: Recollection of Guilt (Terroanima) \010Weepers: Recollection of Fear (Psychoanima) \010Seethers: Recollection of Pain (Hysteroanima) \010Promyvion - Holla\010Wanderers: Recollection of Fear (Psychoanima) \010Weepers: Recollection of Pain (Hysteroanima) \010Seethers: Recollection of Guilt (Terroanima) \010A party preparing for the BC fight of an \"early\" Promyvion (that is, Holla, Dem, or Mea) should get at least enough Recollections of Pain for everyone to synth one Hysteroanima. A full set of Hysteroanima and Psychoanima is usually enough to shut down a boss for the entire fight. If you are farming Recollections, either as a group or by yourself, it's easiest to go to Holla to farm these, since the two weakest monsters (Weepers and Wanderers) will give you the Recollections you need most.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Recollection of Guilt, Fear, and Pain\010\010Terroanima are not particularly useful in the early fights, as it's difficult to continue combat while the boss is running around the BC. However, Terroanima can be a critical element in your strategy for Promyvion - Vahzl, as you'll be fighting all three of the early Promyvion bosses in a row, with some overlap. You can use Terroanima to \"kite\" the next boss while you finish up the one you're working on. Refer to the strategic notes on Desires of Emptiness for more details.\010Take these back to Harith in Ru'Lude Gardens (the Duke's house behind where Maat is). Trade him the Recollection + 2,000g to get your special anima back. There is a minor catch to this. \010The animas are Rare, so you cannot hold two of the same name. However, the Recollections are non-Rare, so you can carry as many of those as you want, and synth them as needed.\010If you synth up your Hysteroanima then give him a Recollection of Pain, Harith will take it. He'll even make the Hysteroanima for you. He just won't give it to you! He'll also refuse to do any other conversions while he's holding your Hysteroanima. However this can be bypassed by storing your extra animas in your delivery box (send it to yourself).\010You must have memories of being in a Promyvion area in order for Harith to perform these synths for you. This means you must either have beaten at least one Promyvion area or not erased your memories when leaving a Promyvion area.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Remnant of a Notorious Monster\010\010Each of the NMs drops a rare/ex Remnant; the Coveter drops a Coveter Remnant, for example. \010These are used to spawn a certain NM in Promyvion - Vahzl. You find a ??? and examine it, the text it gives clues you in to which Remnant to use.\010The Coveter Remnant will spawn the NM that can drop the Recollection of Animosity. Take that Recollection to Harith and trade with 2000 gil and you will get a Dia Wand.\010The Satiator Remnant will spawn the NM that can drop the Recollection of Anxiety. Trade this one to Harith and you'll get the Stone Gorget.\010The Cerebrator Remnant will spawn the NM that can drop the Recollection of Suffering. Trade this one to Harith and you'll get the Hamayumi.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What's waiting for me in the Spire?\010\010Boss fights, of course. The Spire is a long room where your level cap is turned off. At the end is a door that you can talk to; doing so will start a BCNM-style fight called Ancient Flames Beckon. Your party is teleported into the arena and the battle begins. Buffs wear on entry. You will have time to buff and med on the inside of the door, so don't panic - just don't run forward after you zone in. \010All bosses are a bigger form of the crag-specific enemy.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spire of Dem\010\010Boss: Progenerator (Derived from Gorger)\010Specials \010Fission - Spawns an Offspring\010Spirit Absorption - 200 HP drain attack \010Promyvion Barrier (Offspring only) - Defense Boost \010Quadratic Continuum - Damage attack, one target \010Vanity Drive - Damage attack, one target \010Stygian Flatus - Paralyze, Area of Effect.\010The Offspring are smaller than normal Gorgers, and are weak. The Progenerator is immune to Sleep and Bind; the Offspring are not. The maximum number of Offspring in play is 6. Offspring will continue to spawn if some are killed off. Fission can be blocked by Hysteroanima or by kiting the Progenerator around.\010This NM does have an innate HP regen ability. To an unprepared party, this NM can cause the offspring to regenerate faster than some can handle.\010Kiting is possible, and is fun; it's especially doable with a blink tank and Elemental Seal+Gravity. If you get an offspring and have no way to handle it, consider kiting the boss or using a Terroanima; that should give you plenty of time to handle the add.\010Defeat the Progenerator successfully (you do not need to kill all of the Offspring before you kill the mega-boss), and you will receive the Key Item Light of Dem.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spire of Mea\010\010Boss: Delver (Derived from Craver) \010Specials \010Brain Spike - Damage attack, one target. \010Empty Thrash - High damage attack, one target. \010Impalement - High damage attack, one target. Damage varies oddly. \010Murk - AOE Slow + Gravity. \010Promyvion Brume - AOE damage + poison + hate reset. \010Carousel - Higher AOE damage. \010Carousel is a bothersome attack, as healing your damage dealers often requires Curaga, which can shift hate in unpleasant ways. Utsusemi can block the Carousel. Utsusemi can't block the AOE poison effect from Promyvion Brume. This is an incredibly nasty skill to face; Hysteroanima will be your friend. \010Make sure you bring Antidote potions to self-cure Poison; your healer will not have time to give Poisona to people. The hate reset also makes doing so a dangerous proposition. \010This boss is incredibly hard to fight if it's allowed to spam its skills. Try triggering Hysteroanima every 20 seconds (or six weapon attacks) to keep him from ever getting one off. Bring Antidote potions - Brume resets hate; if your WHM is doing Poisona repeatedly, he may find himself being chased. Running away with Gravity on is ... tricky. \010Defeat the Delver successfully and you will receive the Key Item Light of Mea.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spire of Holla\010\010Boss: Wreaker (Derived from Thinker) \010Specials \010Trinary Absorption - Drain, one target. \010Trinary Tap - Steals 3 status effects from whomever is targeted. \010Empty Cutter - Damage attack, one target. \010Negative Whirl - AOE damage attack plus Slow. \010Stygian Vapor - Short range AOE Plague. \010Shadow Spread - AOE Curse + Sleep. This is what kills teams. \010Bring Poison Potions (something slow) to make sure you don't sleep; bring Holy Water to fix the Curse status. \010Hysteroanimas are your friend. Every 30 seconds will do it for you. My team fought this boss with six Hysteroanimas on board; we used four by the time he died. We were not affected by a single skill, so I can't fully update his list. In truth, I never want to go up there to let him slap me around. Would you? ^^\010Defeat the Wreaker successfully and you will receive the Key Item Light of Holla.\010(Note by a half Noob who did this in the fantastic time of 17.54 minutes. What I write about is how not to go into the Spire. Just a couple of pieces of advice that would have avoided us a whole lot of troubles.\010First, bring at least 4 Poison Potions each, in case something goes wrong.\010Second, Holy Water is important, but not fundamental if you are tanking with a WAR/NIN\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spire of Holla\010\010What I write about is mostly how to Astral Flow, because you will bring as many SMN as possible, won't you? (That includes bringing a WHM to heal). I really think the best way to use Astral Flow is this one: the SMNs use their Yagudo Drinks, someone throws his Hysteroanima, then the tank 'vokes while the SMNs cast their Avatar. Then they use Astral Flow and the 2hr Pact. Every time you should repeat this, because what went wrong in my run was that 2 or 3 of our Blood Pacts didn't go off, one because the Wreaker started to run around, another because the Avatar was asleep. Casting the Avatar right before using the 2hr ability makes sure it is not asleep; using Provoke will assure that the mob will stay in place, and using the Hysteroanima will assure that the Boss won't Sleep your avatar while charging the Blood Pact.\010To conclude, just don't go in and spam your Blood Pacts, have some kind of strategy to make sure they all hit their target. Another thing, because it is likely that one won't hit the Boss, bring many, many potions and Hi-Ethers for the SMNs; it will help staying awake and being able to deal some damage in the end when the boss spams the AoE Sleep.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Working Strategy and party setup for all these 3 Promyvions\010\010How?\010You don't need SMN\010It is highly recommended to bring a whm for main healing to this fight\010You need DDs\010You need skilled players, or at least a skilled leader and members who can obey without thinking\010Remain calm and keep focused on your role in the fight\010You need animas, of course. Buy these, or buy recollections, or farm recollections.\010Each Players must have a least Psychoanima and Hysteroanima.\010Set an order to use, healer, then 2nd 3rd 4th 5th 6th players.\010Items\010You don't really need meds for Dem\010You don't really need meds for Mea, you can fight even with poison on you. But bring antidote if you want to. \010You will need Eye Drops and Poison Potion for Holla. Holy Water not required, but better.\010Of course any players can bring potion, food, drink, ether...\010You should know...\010If it's your first time fighting a promyvion boss you will have the feeling that you can't beat it, because it has high HP, and it deals high damage to melee.\010Don't panic! Just control hate and try to keep everyone alive.\010These is no \"perfect\" setup for this fight, but some are better then others\010Even with 1 or 2 death, you can beat it\010Boss strategy\010Enter and use food, wait for everyone and buff.\010Melee who have JA's with long recast like SAM Medidate, use it immediately.\010Full rest, it's always better to have full MP\010The tank should enter first to set hate and melee follow after\010If you have a dd with /nin let them enter first, use shadows, then the tank can provoke \010Fight normally, WHM heal melee, and the tank provoke the boss\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Working Strategy and party setup for all these 3 Promyvions\010\010Now, my dear skilled leader friend who is reading this, YOU have to decide when to use the anima. Not too late, don't let one die, not too earlier, you need to keep the boss under effect of animna thoughout the fight. Check how the WHM can handle healing, and HP-damages of the boss.\010But anyway, don't panic, if you use it at 70-80% of boss life, you will even keep some animas.\010Remember the order of anima use, and ask them to use BOTH of animas, first psycho, then hystero.\010When the boss attack again (it's why i said first psycho), use it again, then hystero.\010If everyone does their job, and regarding of which jobs you have, you will win easily and keep 1-3 or more animas left over\010(I have to go, work in progress.)\010(i have wrote the minimum of killing boss, animas use)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Helping Others\010\010People who have completed all three BCNM fights are able to enter to help others, so long as someone who needs the battle triggers combat first. In all respects, it's just like the 2-3 Dragon fight, the 5-1 Archlich Taber'quon fight, the 5-2 Shadow Lord fight, and many others.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Why do I want to do the Promy missions?\010\010When you finish your third Promyvion BCNM, you will be automatically warped to Lufaise Meadows. This gets you a cutscene and starts Chapter 2 of Chains of Promathia. \010After you have started Chapter 2, you will be able to use the Swirling Vortexes in Valkurm Dunes and Qufim Island. \010Valkurm Dunes -=> Lufaise Meadows\010Qufim Island -=> Misareaux Coast\010You will then be able to walk to the Tavnazian Safehold, where the next CoP missions await. \010If you complete your last Promy run with a Level 30 character, it's important to know that there are aggressive enemies between you and the Safehold. Either wait for friends, recruit some on-site help, or back up 'til you find the Portal and jump over to Valkurm. Come back with Prism Powders (Orcs, Giants) and Silent Oils (Bugards).\010You will enjoy next missions and quests involved in Chain of Promathia..!",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Credits, etc\010\010See Source\010Author/Editor: Wingchild \010Started: September 24th, 2004 \010Rewrite: December 13th, 2004 \010Last edit: 01/21/2005 - It's been a month since the last update and the information here is now static; I expect no further major revisions for this guide. You may consider it to be a complete work - all the information inside is tested and verified. Added some notes about the Memory Flux gates. Still no data on NM Remnants. \010 Update : May 17th, 2010\010See also The Mothercrystals",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_3115",["title"]="Promyvion Map Guide",["page_title"]="Promyvion Map Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Promyvion_Map_Guide",["revision"]="49983",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Walkthrough\010\010The Promyvion maps are obtained by trading the correct Memosphere item to a ??? on the final floors. This is not the same ??? as the one used for the Tactics Pearl quest.\010The Memospheres in Dem, Mea and Holla drop from the Crag-specific monsters and their NM counterparts; the Memosphere in Vahzl drops from those same three mob types as well as the three NMs. \010Once each person who wants the map has a Memosphere, it's time to locate the ???.\010The ??? for each zone spawns in a fixed location on the map\010Promyvion - Holla (M-8), Fourth Floor\010Promyvion - Dem (J-9), Fourth Floor\010Promyvion - Mea (H-4), Fourth Floor\010Promyvion - Vahzl (D-8), Fifth Floor\010To quickly trade an item you may create a macro:\010/item \"Indigo Memosphere\" <t>\010The ??? locations are marked on the maps below.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Maps\010\010Zone  |  Memosphere  |  Dropped by  |  ??? Location\010Promyvion - Dem  |  Beryl Memosphere  | \010Gorger (29-40)\010Satiator (38)\010Promyvion - Dem (J-9)\010Promyvion - Holla  |  Teal Memosphere  | \010Thinker (29-40)\010Cerebrator (38)\010Promyvion - Holla (M-8)\010Promyvion - Mea  |  Indigo Memosphere  | \010Craver (29-40)\010Coveter (38)\010Promyvion - Mea (H-4)\010Promyvion - Vahzl  |  White Memosphere  | \010Gorger\010Thinker\010Craver\010Deviator\010Provoker\010Wailer\010Promyvion - Vahzl (D-8)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_57769",["title"]="Puppetmaster Attachment Library",["page_title"]="Puppetmaster Attachment Library",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Puppetmaster_Attachment_Library",["revision"]="123891",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Puppetmaster Attachment Library\010\010Wassup mfs. Welcome to library. This place is currently being worked on, and will eventually contain information on all the automaton attachments. Listing out their tier, mechanics, and overall use. Many changes have came to attachments on horizon, in addition, it can be hard for puppetmasters to know exactly what to get while leveling or at 75. We created this library to help with that. And at any point, feel free to add something, or correct wrong information.\010List of contributors here (feel free to add your name):",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Auto-repair kits\010\010Cost: 2 Light // 3 Light\010Functionality: Increases max hp, and gives a regen effect while you have light manuevers up.\010According to the FF11 wiki; these are the max hp bonus values.\010Frame\010Auto-Repair Kit I\010Auto-Repair Kit II\010Harlequin Frame\010+5%\010+10%\010Valoredge Frame\010+4.2%\010+8.3%\010Sharpshot Frame\010+5.6%\010+11.1%\010Stormwaker Frame\010+6.2%\010+12.4%\010And these are the values you get for regen;\010Light Maneuvers\010Auto-Repair Kit I\010Auto-Repair Kit II\0100\010No Regen Effect\010No Regen Effect\0101\0101\0103\0102\0102\0106\0103\0103\0109\010This is really about the only set of attachments in the game that I truly believe to be F tier. I mean, simply by looking at them you could understand that maybe these could be decent. A source of regen would be nice. Well... No. On paper having more max hp and a source of regen sounds good, especally for a melee auto like VE or Harly.\010\"But Cryo, don't the attachments do a percentage of max hp in their regen, too?\" Sadly, no. That was changed on Horizon.\010In practice, these attachments do next to nothing. The max hp generally will not be useful. It may be nice in certain situations, but 99% of the time you would rather have any other attachment than this one.\010An arguement can be made that these attachments are good at lower levels, and to that, I'd say sure. Yes, they can contribute something at lower levels assuming you have not many good attachments. But... According to the current AH as I am writing this, the repair kits cost 60k and 300k gil each respectively. This. Is. Not worth it, in any way. You are better off buying really anything else with that gil.\010Here is some example math.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Auto-repair kits\010\010Assuming you have three light manuevers up, with both kits, you would be generating 12 health every tick. Sounds decent, right? Yeah, no. A single cannibal blade from the ranges of 66-75 can hit from anywhere to 300-600 damage. This only requires a single dark manuever, and can keep the auto up on its own.\010300(cannibal blade health) * 12(3 light maneuver, double repair kits, regen) = 25\010You need 25 ticks of regen to equal as much health as cannibal blade would give you. Which would take 100 seconds.\010This is very bad. Triple light is completely awful too, as very few light attachments are even worth using a manuever for. A single dark maneuver would let your auto use cannibal blade often, which not only heals it for more than THREE LIGHT MANUEVERS would, it also doesn't cost a billion overload (as optimal setups need optic fiber, and optic fiber causes light manuevers to be a lot more expensive burden wise).\010In conclusion, I hate this attachment, it is very bad at anything other than extremely low levels. Use only if you have completely free extra slots. I do not recommend anyone buying it.\010Hi guys my i love cinnamonrolls and elvaan mommies with silver hair. Now none of this is relevant to anything but let me also tell you why tarus are inferior to galkan race. Bomb toss and nin explosions. Nuff said.. So long lamers",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_15902",["title"]="Reputation-guide",["page_title"]="Reputation-guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Reputation-guide",["revision"]="121200",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Only the Best\010\010Highly Recommended:\010Bastok, San d'Oria, Jeuno, Selbina, Rabao\010Only the Best is a repeatable Selbina quest that will simultaneously raise reputation for Bastok, San d'Oria and Jeuno. Because of this, it is the best quest to complete for Bastok and San d'Oria.\010It requires La Theine Cabbage, Boyahda Moss or Millioncorn. Each item rewards a different amount and raises a certain amount of Reputation.\010For a complete walkthrough, trade breakdown, and cost analysis, refer to the quest page.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Windurst\010\010[Template Icon: Windurst / extra large]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mihgo's Amigo\010\010Otherwise known as \"Farming Yagudo Necklace for Norg fame.\" Harvest Yagudo Necklace by defeating Yagudo in Giddeus. Trade four at a time to Nanaa Mihgo for Norg reputation. You are not required to zone. 250-300 Yagudo Necklace [Wiki: needs verification] will give you enough fame to start the quest(s). \010Note Best to go as a combo such as BLM30/THF15+ or THF35/BLM17+ for Treasure Hunter and the ability to Warp back to Windurst. \010This Quest and Shady Business are highly recommended.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mandragora-Mad\010\010Yoran-Oran seems to have a thing for mandies. He wants any and all Mandragora-related items. All items can be acquired from mandies all over the world. Cornette, Four-Leaf Mandragora Bud, Snobby Letter, Three-Leaf Mandragora Bud, and Yuhtunga Sulfur are the items that he wants. This quest is your best bet for improving your Windurst reputation. When Bastok is 2nd or 1st in the Conquest, you can purchase Cornettes from Harmodios in Bastok Markets (K-10, Harmodios's Music Shop) for 219\226\128\147258 gil. You get 200 gil back for trading the Cornette, making it a minimal loss. You can trade them one after the other, as zoning is not required. It is suggested that you have a friend or a mule send you the Cornettes, as traveling between Windy and Bastok multiple times can be a pain. It takes 270-300 cornettes to max Windy reputation. With a friend, second character, or second account in Bastok you can go from level 1 reputation to level 9 in just under 2 hours. [Wiki: needs verification] \010This is not a complete list, and may not represent the minimum. The list was made with Fame checked roughly every 20 Cornettes so it may be possible for the amount to be less than what is listed.\010 I received the final message at 245 turnins as a Bastokan citizen.\010align=\"center\" width=\"30%\"|Cornettes\010align=\"center\" width=\"35%\"|Reputation level\010 Refer to the quest page for additional information and testimonials.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Creepy Crawlies\010\010This NPC wants three Silk Threads or three Crawler Calculi. This can be tied in with farming Silk Threads for profit. Don't give the Silk to the NPC, sell that for money. Just give her the Calculi. Gil + Reputation = Happy!",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Something Fishy\010\010Tokaka will pay you 70 gil for each Bastore Sardine you bring her. Zoning between trades is required so use the Teleport Taru at the start of the dock. Bastore Sardines can be purchased beside her at the Fishing Guild. They can also be fished up with minimal fishing skill. But also remember, the Fishing Guild carries a limited supply of Bastore Sardine, so plan carefully. It's very time consuming, yet cheap in gilspending. Be sure to check the Auction House prices, they may be cheaper than buying the Bastore Sardine from the Fishing Guild.\010It takes roughly 3-4 stacks of Bastore Sardines to gain a rank in fame, this is a rough estimate of course.\010Not wanting to alter anything above, I did Rank 1-9 Fame Windurst using this tedious method of only trading in 1 Bastore Sardine at a time then zone.\01056 sardines = Rank 6 and 214 Sardines = Rank 9 that is what it took me to complete the Bastore Sardines straight up to Rank 9 Fame Windurst.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Bastok\010\010[Template Icon: Bastok / extra large]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Minesweeper\010\010This requires that you turn in Zeruhn Soot obtained in Zeruhn Mines. Zeruhn Soot is difficult to get at times, even with Treasure Hunter. The biggest problem is that Zeruhn is not a very big zone, so there aren't many monsters around to drop it. The soot can also be purchased through the auction house, though it is rather pricey for such a low level item. This quest can be repeated without changing areas between.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Shady Business\010\010Same idea as Mihgo's Amigo, but you are harvesting Zinc Ore from Worms instead of Yagudo Necklace. You can also get lots of Zinc Ore while mining in certain areas, such as Oldton Movalpolos. Give them to Talib, four at a time. \010Note There is a pre-quest where you have to give him one ore, then he will start taking four at a time. \010This Quest and Mihgo's Amigo are highly recommended.\010It takes 109 Zinc Ore (27 trades of 4 plus the 1 from the pre-quest) in order to go from 0 Norg fame to level 4 and be able to get the Trial by Water, Utsusemi: Ichi and Tonko: Ichi quests. [Wiki: needs verification] \010This quest does not give Bastok Fame, only Norg.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="San d'Oria\010\010[Template Icon: san d'oria / extra large]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Fear of the Dark\010\010Pretty simple. Give 2 bat wings to Secodiand - Northern San d'Oria (F-6). Bat Wings can be farmed in nearby King Ranperre's Tomb or even Ronfaure at night time. The only thing that may make you want to stray away from this quest is that bat wings sell really well at the auction house. Then again, it is because of this quest.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Thick Shells\010\010This quest is good if you just came back from EXPing on Beetles. Trade Vounebariont - Port San d'Oria (I-10) five Beetle Shells.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Starting a Flame\010\010This is like the A Flash in the Pan quest in Bastok. Four Flint Stones to Legata - Southern San d'Oria (K-6). There are no time or zoning restrictions, so just keep trading 4 at a time to build your reputation. Flint Stones are easily obtained from killing worms in Ronfaure.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="The Merchant's Bidding\010\010Yet another \"bring me items for a reward\" quest. This time it's three Rabbit Hides. Kill bunnies in Ronfaure. Then Trade them to Parvipon - Southern San d'Oria (E-8).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="The Sweetest Things\010\010Kill a bunch of bees for Honey, give them to Raimbroy- Southern San d'Oria (F-8) five at a time. Result... reputation.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="The Competition\010\010This is one of the easier ways to get reputation in San d'Oria. Start the quest by talking to one of the brothers, Joulet or Gallijaux, at the end of the pier in Port San d'Oria and agree to help them. Then just procure some Moat Carp however you please, they can be bought on the Auction House, or they can be fished up using a Halcyon Rod and Insect Paste. Note that this can be somewhat expensive, but if you trade the carp in one at a time, each time it counts as a quest. I got to level 10 reputation in a little under an hour.\010Note: You can set up a macro to trade single Moat Carp to the brother of choice: /item \"Moat Carp\" <t> then select the brother and spam.\010 It requires 252 Moat Carps to go from Zero fame to Hero Fame. Trade Moat Carps one by one.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Tiger's Teeth\010\010Trading 3 Black Tiger Fangs to Taumila in Southern San D'Oria at (E-9) can be a good way to increase fame and make money at the same time. Higher level (50+) characters can farm the tigers in Batallia Downs and Jugner Forest with ease and each set of 3 fangs will net you 2100 gil. \010Requires Sandy Fame 3 to utilize.*",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Jeuno\010\010[Template Icon: Jeuno / extra large]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Community Service\010\010This is a repeatable quest, but can only be done by one person per server per Vana'diel day. It's a fun little distraction that includes walking up and down Lower Jeuno lighting lamps at night. It can be activated at 18:00.\010 Talk to Zauko - Lower Jeuno (I-6) between 18:00 and 1:00.\010 Zauko needs you to light all the lamps before 1:00.\010 There are 12 \"Street Lamp\" objects along the entire length of Lower Jeuno, on the seaward side of the street.\010 Click on each one to light it, then return to Zauko after you've lit all twelve.\010If you do this quest several times, Zauko gives you a key item. Thereafter, every time you zone into Lower Jeuno between 18:00 and 01:00, if this quest is unclaimed for the day, you will get a system message, \"Zauko is recruiting an adventurer to light the lamps.\"\010Zauko will sometimes wait until as late as 18:10 Vana'diel time before opening the quest. If you talk to him exactly at 18:00, he might just give you a short speech and turn away. If this happens, just try him again.\010In addition to boosting your reputation among NPCs, it can also get your name out to other players. If someone who has not yet done this quest talks to Zauko at night after the quest is completed, he tells them the name of the character who lit the lamps. This effect does nothing significant, but is similar to the title-changing NPCs who sing the name and title of their previous customers before offering their services to you.\010The Goblin Tailor, Gobbiebag, Artifact Armor, Bard Flag Quest, and Beastmaster Flag Quest also give Jeuno reputation.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_20150",["title"]="SATA",["page_title"]="SATA",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/SATA",["revision"]="51036",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]\010SATA is the acronym for a widely used Thief attack. The attack consists of two thief job abilities: Sneak Attack and Trick Attack.\010When both these abilities are activated the thief can deal critical damage from behind the mob and standing directly behind another player. Bear in mind that Trick Attack will transfer all the enmity of your damage onto the player you're behind, also known as your trick partner, so it is advisable to only SATA onto someone who can take the hits if the mob turns on them.\010SATA will only connect on the first attack of an attack round. If you have a multi-hit effect active from Dual Wield, Double Attack, Triple Attack, or an Occasionally Attacks X Times weapon SATA will not connect on the current attack round if used after the first attack of the round and before the last attack of the round. SATA will only connect on the first attack of the next round.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Basics\010\010It can be hard being a thief because many other players aren't aware of the very specific positioning requirements that we have. Basically, we need the trick partner (usually the tank) behind the mob, and the thief behind the tank. Easy as that. But nonetheless, we don't get every SATA off. Here is a little diagram:\010valign=\"top\"|\010THF---> TANK---> MOB---> <---DD or THF--->DD--->MOB---><---TANK\010You need one person holding the hate for long enough that the thief can do their thing from behind the mob and the tank. All other party members can basically be wherever, as long as they're not prone to pulling hate. If they are prone to pulling hate, then they might also want to line up in front of the mob with the DD (damage dealer). A good thief should take very little time to line up and get a SATA off most of the time.\010NEVER SATA ON A MAGE JOB! THIS IS THE BEST WAY FOR THEM TO GET KILLED.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Advanced\010\010You can also take advantage of the ability Hide in a battle when you want to SATA without considering which direction the mob faces. This may become useful when the mob is often turning. Only usable on mobs that Detect and Track by Sight only.\010Get into position behind the tank.\010Disengage.\010Activate SATA.\010Use Hide.\010Engage the mob.\010You will SATA and the direction the mob faces doesn't matter.\010Example of a successful SATA with active Hide:\010valign=\"top\"|THF---> TANK---> <---MOB <---DD\010Also with the help of a Sneak effect: the spell Sneak, a Silent oil, or the ninjutsu Monomi: Ichi will all work, you can SATA Hide mobs that Detect by Sound. I believe the same is true for a Deodorize effect with mobs that Track by Scent. How ever in these cases you must sneak or deodorize before you use Hide or you will be spotted by the mob and will not lose your enmity.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="SATA Macros\010\010valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"|\010/ja \"Trick Attack\" <me> <wait 1>\010/ja \"Sneak Attack\" <me>\010This one is good for just dealing a critical hit. Many thieves like to activate SATA when they see that a mob has been pulled so that they can be ready before the mob gets back to camp. Once they get their hit on the mob their SATA will be ready a few seconds later because the timers were already ticking down as the mob was coming to camp.\010valign=\"top\" width=\"50%\"|\010/ja \"Trick Attack\" <me> <wait 1>\010/ja \"Sneak Attack\" <me> <wait 1>\010/ws \"Viper Bite\" <t>\010This one is good for making sure that your weapon skill (Viper Bite or another) goes off right after SATA. This is useful because sometimes if you're not fast enough at spamming your weapon skill macro you will swing and hit the mob, using up your SATA, and then your Viper Bite goes off, effectively wasting your TP.\010However, there are many thieves who prefer to keep their SATA macro separate from their weapon skill macro. This way they can still set up SATA as the mob is pulled, position themselves behind the mob and trick partner, use their WS macro as they engage the mob, and a few seconds later SATA is ready for use again.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="SATA plus Weapon Skills\010\010SATA can make a lot of weapon skills do a lot more damage than they normally would. But it won't stack (this means that the 2 or more abilities/spells/etc can be active at the same time, or used to enhance each other) with every WS. If the WS does purely elemental damage then your SATA is useless with it. SATA can only stack with WS's that are purely physical, or just have an elemental effect that they do. It's kinda hard to tell by looking which ones do and don't stack, but if the WS icon (when you go to your WS list in game, the icon next to the WS name) is white, then it's pure damage. If it's another color, that means it is either purely that element, or has an effect of that element. Experimentation is the best way to figure out which ones work and don't!",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Dagger WS's\010\010The following Dagger Weapon Skills will work for full effect with SA+TA.\010Wasp Sting\010Shadowstitch \010Viper Bite (most common after lvl33)\010Dancing Edge \010Shark Bite \010Evisceration",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Skillchains\010\010One of the best uses for SATA is with a weapon skill. Thieves are notorious for closing Distortion skillchains. It can be tricky to close a weapon skill as a thief because you have to time everything just right, as well as be positioned perfectly, and hope that the mob doesn't turn around prematurely. On top of that, you have to make sure that your SATA is actually ready to be used again before you do the Skillchain.\010So line yourself up, tell your buddy to go, and practice with your own personal timing from when to activate SATA and do your WS. You typically want to activate your weapon skill as you see the effects of the previous one fading away. So you need to activate SATA at about the middle to end of the previous weapon skill. I don't have a very good explanation, but practice and experimenting is what really will make you understand. A good idea is to always coordinate the skillchain before you begin fighting. You and your partner should show each other your TP and weapon skill macros so you know what to look for in the log.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Assassin\010\010Once a Thief attains level 60, they will get a new job trait, Assassin, which gives Trick Attack the same guarantee of a critical hit which cannot miss that Sneak Attack always has. Before you get Assassin, Trick Attack can miss and does not generally do critical damage. The reason it is stacked with Sneak Attack is to get this guarantee of a strong hit on the attack which is meant to transfer hate.\010Once you have Assassin, you should generally not SATA anymore and instead split the two abilities. SATA will do more damage than either ability alone, but generally separating the two abilities will do slightly more damage overall. It also removes the difficulty of lining up the enemy and risking a missed SATA.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Unexpected situations of SATA\010\010Many times during the leveling career of a Thief, you will experience a situation that was not expected. Some of the common ones are listed below.\010Low damage, hate did not seem to change: This means that your SATA may have failed. This can be caused by the tank moving during the attack, being in the incorrect position for SA, or not standing directly behind the trick partner.\010Your intended trick partner did not get hate, however another player did: This means that for some reason, the other person likely received the effect of SATA. This seems to most commonly occur if someone is very close to or behind your trick partner. If someone to either the side of the enemy got hate, they probably pulled it immediately before SATA went off.\010You strike the mob in between SA and TA or TA and SA depending on the ordering of your macro. Even with the minimal /wait 1, this occasionally happens. Try to keep an eye on your swings and hit your macro after your first swing of an attack round.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_5078",["title"]="Smithing Guide 0-60 by Pendulum",["page_title"]="Smithing Guide 0-60 by Pendulum",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Smithing_Guide_0-60_by_Pendulum",["revision"]="61446",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template UserGuide: ]\010I originally wrote this guide whilst playing on the Eden server but I thought I would port it over ot HorizonXI's wiki. Hopefully it can prove useful to someone who plays on the server. I have not proof read this guide so there may be mentions of Eden or things not available on Horizon so beware and DYOR if unsure.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 1-10\010\010(4) Bronze Sheet: A simple start, buy your ingots from the guild if possible. Save the sheets for the next recipe. Stop this synth at level 2.\010Fire Crystal\010Bronze Ingot\010(10) Bronze Scales: You can now turn your sheets into scales. Make sure to use advanced synth support until at least level 4. Buy/make more sheets if you don't manage to hit level 6, at which point you can move onto the next recipe. Dump them with a vendor when you're done as you're unlikely to sell them on the AH and will have no further need of them.\010Wind Crystal\010Bronze Sheet",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Test Item\010\010Xiphos: You can take the time to craft one yourself but it'll be easier to just grab one off the AH.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 11-20\010\010(14) Bronze Bolt Heads: Again, a simple recipe. Ingots from guild vendor if possible. Keep those costs low. You can stop this recipe at 12 or continue with it as depending on AH prices/if you're a miner, it may or may not be cheaper to do so. You will have no further need of the bolt heads so NPC/AH them as you wish.\010Wind Crystal\010Bronze Ingot\010(18) Aspis: You might find that skillups on Bronze Bolt Heads start to become a little slow around level 10. Aspis can be a fairly cheap bridge synth. All materials should be purchased from guild vendors and at base price (the ash from the woodworking guild) to keep those costs right down. As with the boltheads, you may switch to Iron Ingots from level 12. If you decide to stick this recipe out, I recommend desynthing them from level 13. Save an aspis for your next test item.\010Fire Crystal\010Ash Lumber\010Bronze Sheet x 2\010(20) Iron Ingot: The first expensive recipe you will encounter. I recommend buying your ores from the AH unless the price goes over 8.1k/stack, at which point the base guild price will be cheaper. Keep ahold of your ingots as they will be re-used. I would craft 4 and a half stacks of these in total and keep advanced guild support on at all times to reduce breakages. Stop when you have the afore mentioned ingots.\010Fire Crystal\010Iron Ore x 4",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Test Item\010\010Aspis: Hopefully you saved one earlier on but if not, these will usually be stocked on the AH.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 21-30\010\010(22) Iron Sheet: Immediately synth your iron ingots into iron sheets. If for some reason you skipped ingots and need some, they can be bought from the guild vendor at 32.4k/stack or the AH if cheaper. You're looking for a total of 4 stacks of iron sheets minimum. Stop this recipe when you have the afore mentioned sheets.\010Fire Crystal\010Iron Ingot\010(26) Iron Scales: ...and once again, your sheets will now turn into scales. You're looking to end up with 4 stacks of scales by the time you're done here. These will then be used a little later on down the line, and again, stop this recipe when you have the 4 stacks.\010Wind Crystal\010Iron Sheet\010(29) Iron Mask: This is a bridge synth and only to be done under 2 cicrumstances: you didn't manage to hit 23 with the previous recipes or there's a shortage of Rusty Greatswords on the AH, which are used in the next recipe. Level 23 is the point to move onto the next recipe here. For anyone with deeper pockets, you may choose to desynth these from level 24 onwards.\010Earth Crystal\010Iron Sheet\010Brass Sheet",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Test Item\010\010Bilbo: This is an easy synth but you will need the Goldsmithing sub-craft. Alternatively, I would expect the AH to have some stocked.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 31-40\010\010(31) Two-Handed Sword: This recipe can be a breakeven synth, if the AH price is right. Once crafted, these will sell to NPC vendors for 2,367 gil each. Stick them out to level 27 at a minimum but as long as they're the more cost-effective option. They can be desynthed from 26 for extra skill-ups, if you wish. \010-- This synth results in the Plain Sword which only NPCs for 1 gil. It's a fairly cheap synth, but do not expect to make your gil back. Bigakuga 9/7/24--\010Light Crystal\010Rusty Greatsword\010(35) Iron Cuisses: Remember those Iron Scales we crafted earlier on? Here you finally get to make use of them. The Leather Trousers can be bought from many vendors and the Cotton Thread from Clothcraft guild vendors, if you wish to save a little on the typically more expensive AH price. The cuisses themselves sell back to vendors for 4,015 gil so although they aren't likely to break-even, they're not bad. You want to use this recipe to hit level 30. Desynth from 30 at your gil peril.\010Earth Crystal\010Iron Scales x 2\010Leather Trousers\010Cotton Thread\010(38) War Pick: Again, you're unlikely to break-even here. Try to snag some ingots from the AH as cheaply as possible. At smithing skill 28 and if you have the stomach for it, with guild support you will be T1 HQ rate on Iron Ingots. The HQ being Steel Ingots of course. It would be a lot of crafting, but it's an option. Ash Lumber can be bought from the Woodworking vendor, typically cheaper than the AH. The picks when made can be sold back to vendors of for the stinking rich amongst us, desynthed from 33. Make sure to save a pick for the test item.\010Fire Crystal\010Steel Ingot\010Ash Lumber",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Test Item\010\010War Pick: As luck would have it, we've just been crafting these.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 41-50\010\010(41 (Leathercraft 10)) Iron Mittens: You will need to level your Leathercraft for this recipe but it's worth it. Cheap recipes are few and far between around these levels. Sheets we've made previously and Lizard Skins can be bought from the AH or farmed if that's your preferred method. The aim here is to reach 39 so we can move on to Mythril Picks however, you likely need to use the next bridge recipe to achieve this as skill-ups will slow right down at level 38. As has been mentioned earlier, desynth is possible from 5 levels below cap on all the suggested recipes 40-50.\010Earth Crystal\010Iron Sheet\010Lizard Skin\010(45 (Leathercraft 12)) Iron Subligar: Very similar to the last recipe but with Cotton Cloth thrown in as well. Again, we only need to hit level 39 on this recipe. Both mittens and subligars can all be sold to vendors.\010Earth Crystal\010Iron Sheet\010Lizard Skin\010Cotton Cloth\010(47) Mythril Pick: I won't lie, picks are a considerable investment, but, they resell to vendors highly as well. For some context, I spent 360k on materials but sold 300k worth if picks. The kicker to this recipe is the Mythril Ingots. If you can make your own, you can do so cheaper than their AH price (as crafters naturally want a commision) and save a little bit more. Regardless, you'll struggle to find a better recipe. Craft these until at least level 44 at which point it's time to move on. Make sure to save a pick for the test item.\010Fire Crystal\010Elm Lumber\010Mythril Ingot",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Test Item\010\010Mythril Pick: Again, as luck would have it, we've just been crafting these as well.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 51-60\010\010(52) Darksteel Ingot: Similarly to Mythril Picks, the investment here will be substantial. In fact, from here on out, you can expect the cost of smithing to remain high. Make liberal use of advanced guild support to reduce the amount of Darksteel Ore (and gil) lost. I recommend saving all the ingots you make for later on. I also recommend only using this recipe until level 46.\010Fire Crystal\010Iron Ore x 3\010Darksteel Ore\010(54) Steel Ingot: A great recipe for the level we are at. It depends on AH prices but I was able to break-even here, even with breaks factored in. Ride this recipe as long as possible and use that advanced guild support all the while. Iron Sand is usually cheaper on the AH as the NPC price is really high. Bomb Ash can be purchased from the alchemy guild vendors but only in small supply. I would stick this out until at least 51, maybe even 52 if you're happy to wait on your ingots to sell on the AH.\010Fire Crystal\010Bomb Ash x 4\010Iron Sand x 4\010(57) Gauntlets: ...and back to throwing our gil away sadly. Sheets we've already made previously and Leather Gloves can be bought from many city merchants. Sell the gauntlets you make to vendors for ~3,000g or alternatively, you can desynth these for the extra chance of a skill up. I personally recommend desynthing these from 52 as alternate recipes will cost far more. \010Fire Crystal\010Iron Sheet x 2\010Leather Gloves x 2",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Test Item\010\010Nothing!: We don't want to uncap our smithing sub-craft, unless you plan on leveling Smithing as a main craft in which case you will need a Darksteel Falchion.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 61-70\010\010Alright, you're ready for the final push. I will warn you now, no matter what route you take from here on out, it will cost you a large chunk of gil. The best advice I can give here - make Darksteel Chains and find someone to buy them from you directly. They sell pretty slowly on the AH but you might be able to cut a deal with someone, you make a good portion of your investment back and they get some cheap chains. Win win. Whatever you recipe you choose, the aim is to hit58.\010(62) Darksteel Bolt Heads: One of the simplest recipes available at these levels which requires no sub-crafts. If your plan is to power through these last few levels of smithing, this is definitely a recipe for you.\010Wind Crystal\010Darksteel Ingot\010(62 (Clothcraft [Template Question: ]) Haidate: These will vendor for around 4,000g so they could be considered a cheap recipe, comparitively to others around these levels. The downside is you will need the clothcraft and unfortunately, the required level is currenntly unknown. However, the fact that this recipe uses silk would lead me to believe that it would need to be as high as in the 50's, the level one can craft the silk itself. You can check out my clothcraft sub-craft guide if you wish to level it also.\010Earth Crystal\010Iron Sheet x 2\010Sheep Leather\010Silk Cloth\010Silk Thread\010(63) Darksteel Baselard: There's no denying that this recipe is really expensive. I have included it as the baselards can be desynth'ed, for at least double the attempts at skill-ups.\010Fire Crystal\010Darksteel Ingot\010Mythril Ingot\010(64 (Clothcraft [Template Question: ]) Sune-Ate: Basically the same recipe as Haidate as described above but with a fire crystal instead and caps 2 levels higher.\010Fire Crystal\010Iron Sheet x 2\010Sheep Leather\010Silk Cloth\010Silk Thread\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 61-70\010\010(66) Nodowa: This recipe will easily take you to the 60 cap and finish your smithing sub-craft. A cheap skill-up recipe for the level we are at so it's a real shame that the earliest you can realistically attempt it is around the level 57/58 mark. Expect lots of breaks at 57 but compared to the alternatives, it might be the cheapest route anyway. Nodowa's sell to vendors for around 2,700g. Congratulations, you've finished your smithing sub-craft!\010Earth Crystal\010Iron Sheet\010Silk Thread",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_16013",["title"]="Smithing guide by Ctownwood and comments",["page_title"]="Smithing guide by Ctownwood and comments",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Smithing_guide_by_Ctownwood_and_comments",["revision"]="118281",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template UserGuide: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Horizon version\010\010This is one of my favorite guides of ctownwoody and felt it applicable for the guide section. Remember Ctownwoody played on Asura(I played on Odin) but his philosophies and observations continue to shine in the game today. I will also add notes about alchemy along side this guide. --Pyrra0 (talk) 22:06, 8 March 2024 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Original guide\010\010Smithing Guide 1.0\0100-70\010by Ctownwoody of Asura\010Sons of Valkurm Linkshell\010Disclaimer: This worked for me and is based on my research and efforts on Asura. Prices are increasingly normalized, but I still suggest going to ffxiah.com to research your particular server. I would use particular care in this craft because it has changed greatly since I started down this road.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctownwood's Smithing philosophy\010\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/b/b7/MiningIcon.png Smithing Tips \010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/b/b7/MiningIcon.png\0101. This is the least profitable craft to take to 60. Goldsmithing is the most expensive and Cooking is the cheapest, but both offer good shots at profit. In Smithing, you'll be attempting a lot of times to simply break even. \010Goldsmithing https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/7/79/GoldsmithingIcon.png \010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/e/ea/CookingIcon.png Cooking\0102. The Myth of Mining: Mining does not save you gil when crafting. Mining makes you gil by selling what you mine; crafting makes you gil by increasing the value of the ingredients. If you were to mine up 10k worth of ingredients and sell them for 6k, you did NOT save 6k by mining; you lost 4k by crafting what you could have sold.\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png\0103. Smithing as a skill isn't profitable, per se, but it is used very often as a sub-craft in every craft except Cooking. You'll want to level this out of a sense of obligation, if nothing else.\010 What ctownwoody means by this, for example, Clothcraft and Alchemy both are profitable as you rank up. Real quick this is due to the inner-connectivity of certain materials like thread, cloth, (chemistry) mercury, fibers, sands, animas. etc --Pyrra0 (talk) 01:52, 10 March 2024 (UTC)\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png\0104. There are actually plenty of profitable synths in Smithing but they sell slowly and/or are prone to flooding, and thus I do not recommend making them for skill. If you are truly taking your time or incredibly stubborn, you can make these for skill. At most, I would only recommend making 12 for a few points of skill and then selling them at your leisure.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctownwood's Smithing philosophy\010\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png\0105. As noted in #3, there are a lot of items that require /Smithing to make and some of them are used in this Guide. Typically, they tend to be easier to make, sell faster, stack better, and are more profitable than anything that uses Smithing as the main-craft.\010 I believe ctownwoody is referencing making ammo and status bolt heads --Pyrra0 (talk) 01:52, 10 March 2024 (UTC)\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png\0106. Basic Pattern is 4 Ores -> Ingot -> Sheet -> Attach to Weapon/Armor. Sometimes, there is a Sheet -> Scale step in there. Unlike when I use this in Goldsmithing, you'll use this formula, because the resulting armor will often sell close to break-even, if not at a profit to Guildshops. However, you might end up skipping the Sheet -> Scale step and finding the scales (Iron and Steel especially) on the AH, where they will be cheaper.\010[Template Synthesis Recipe: nc\010 / craft = Smithing\010 / cap = 20\010 / result = Iron Ingot\010 / hq result = Steel Ingot\010 / crystal = Fire\010 / ingredient = Iron Ore\010 / ingredient quantity = 4]\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png\0107. https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/6/69/Junction.pngOne method, widely recommended, for getting cheap synthesis \010materials if not outright sellable items, is Desynthesis. \010Goblin Helms and Mail and their \010Moblin equivalents are \010standard low-level ones. Antican and Quadav Armors are \010more used by top-tier Smiths for gil. \010Please, be aware that Desynthesis is tricky, \010with a high failure rate, so assume at least a 50% failure \010rate on attempts and maybe a 33-40% rate at breaks.https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/6/69/Junction.png\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctownwood's Smithing philosophy\010\010If you take anything from this guide, I hope it is number 7 --Pyrra0 (talk) 01:52, 10 March 2024 (UTC)8. Camp the AH! Especially Jeuno. Some crafters, for some bizarre reason, will make the scales for skill and then dump them on the AH for cheap. Their idiocy is your gain.\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png9. As you will no doubt conclude after a careful reading of this guide, you will need an absolute ton of Iron Sheets, more than any other item except crystals. I would recommend finding, making, or identifying a friend with Sheeting, a Key Item that is used in both Smithing and Goldsmithing. This method can make you tons of Sheets faster and cheaper than you could yourself. Also, if you plan to take Smithing beyond 60 (see this guide for advice), you will want the Key Item for yourself because you won't stop using massive amounts of sheets at 60, or even 66.\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png10'. Bastok, Sandy, and Whitegate (for those that have access) all have Guilds with Guildshops (plus a small, under-stocked shop in Mhaura). They offer the same ores, generally, but Bastok and Sandy buy different sets of items, including ones that you will make for skill. As a rule of thumb, Sheet-based items sell in Bastok, and Scale-based items sell in Sandy.\010https://horizonffxi.wiki/w/images/a/ab/Charm_%28Status_Effect%29.png",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="0-10\010\010Bronze Ingot (2)-Fire Crystal, Copper Ore x3, Tin Ore--Cheap and easy to do. Now, pretty cheap to get on AH and from Guilds as well. Save them if you make them, but you can skip .\010Bronze Sheet (4)-Fire Crystal, Bronze Ingot--Easy, cheap, shouldn't take more than 2-3 stacks from 2 or 3-4 stacks from 0 skill. You can make these or buy these. Pretty cheap at this point.\010Hatchet (8)-Fire Crystal, Maple Lumber, Bronze Ingot x2--Woodworking sub of 2, which isn't a problem. At lowest prices from Guilds, these are cheaper to make, before HQs, than they are to buy from NPCs. Make a couple of stacks to sell on AH for various quests/missions.\010Bronze Scales (10)-Wind Crystal, Bronze Sheet--Make to cap, sell them at a loss to an NPC, and get over it. There isn't a lot here but the good news is that this is cheap as anything you'll see, despite the increase in Wind Crystal prices.\010TEST ITEM: Xiphos(8): Fire Crystal, Giant Femur, Bronze Ingot x2--Get one somehow. Bonecraft 2 as a sub might make you fail but only maybe.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="11-20\010\010Boltheads (14)-Wind Crystal, Bronze Ingot, Animal Glue, [additive]--All of these aside from Bronze Bolt Heads require some level of Alchemy to make the [additive] and then to make the boltheads. This is the principle reason why my Alchemy-mule got his Smithing skill to 15. The list is Blind_Bolt_Heads (31), Acid_Bolt_Heads (45), Holy_Bolt_Heads (53), Sleep_Bolt_Heads (67), Venom_Bolt_Heads (76), and Bloody_Bolt_Heads (81). Make to cap, one or multiple types.\010Note: You can try to make the plain Bronze_Bolt_Heads from just Bronze Ingots, but they don't sell well. However, it's easier that way, if you don't have the Alchemy.\010 15-19 lightning crystal + Goblin Mail to get closer to Iron Ingot, imo --Pyrra0 (talk) 01:52, 10 March 2024 (UTC)\010 This is a stopping point to level alchemy to 40 and goldsmithing to 24 if you plan to make bullets. If you plan to make bolts, i'm not sure tbh --Pyrra0 (talk) 05:46, 9 March 2024 (UTC)\010Iron Ingot (20)-Fire Crystal, Iron Ore x4--This will be the only time that you make these. Ever. The ore costs 675 at the guild, check the AH for cheaper samples, and the Ingots cost 2700 each (675 x4 = 2700), so buying the ingots is cheaper but you need the skill for now and it only costs a crystal per attempt. Never, ever ever buy either for more than the listed prices.\010Paktong Ingot (20)-Fire Crystal, Zinc Ore, Copper Ore x2, Kopparnickel Ore--These are a good example of that \"profitable but slow\" synth. It's used almost exclusively for Gobbiebag quest and the ore is a drop from Bugbears in Movalpolos, so don't count on large supplies or high demand, but consider it if you see a chance.\010TEST ITEM: Aspis: Fire Crystal, Ash Lumber, Bronze Sheet x2--Easy enough to make if you are in Sandy or buy from the Guild if you are in Bastok.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="21-30\010\010Iron Sheet (22)-Fire Crystal, Iron Ingot--Time was, that this up-coming series was extremely profitable. Now, not as profitable, but it is the best way to get rid of your newly-made Iron Ingots. Don't sell the Sheets yet, though. Just cap on these before moving onto Scales/Makibishi.\010Update: After passing 26 in skill, you can turn Iron Ingots into Sheets at a profit. People use a ton of Iron Sheets from mid 30s to 45 and then from mid 50s to 66.\010Makibishi (23)-Wind Crystal, Iron Scales--Ironically, this is the third of the three synths for disposing of your Iron Ingots but second in terms of skill. Turn one Sheet into a Scale then into a Makibishi in order to attempt to maximize your skill-ups. A good item for Ninjas spamming the wheel.\010 This is a stopping point to get past Iron Bullet in alchemy(45) then Steel Bullet(50). You will now start making more Steel Ingot from this point forward than Iron Ingot given the skill difference. Plan accordingly. Protip: iron bullets are easier to make but sells faster because its lower lvl while steel bullets is used in 75/endgame, harder to acquire in theory and sells slower. --Pyrra0 (talk) 05:46, 9 March 2024 (UTC)\010Iron Scales (26)-Wind Crystal, Iron Sheet--Second in the Iron Ingot Sequence, but the top in terms of skill. Make one, then turn it into Makibishi. Once you hit cap on Makibishi, just make these until you cap, then make them into Makibishi.\010Steel Bullet (30)-Fire Crystal, Steel Ingot, Firesand--Alchemy 49 as the main-craft and you can't make Steel Ingots yet but look for them on the AH. This isn't cheap, around 50-60k per stack made, but profitable. These are the premiere bullets for non-Rangers. NOT AVAILABLE ON Horizon\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="21-30\010\010TEST ITEM: Bilbo: Fire Crystal, Silver Ingot, Iron Ingot: Goldsmithing 7 as a sub-craft, but you might be able to buy one on the AH for cheaper than it takes to make this.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="31-40\010\010Iron Cuisses (35)-Earth Crystal, Iron Scales x2, Cotton Thread, Leather Trousers--Camp the AH for Iron Scales, at 2k each or less. Make these in Sandy and beware of the sub-craft of Leathercraft 5. If you do all that, these NPC to the Guildshop at break-even or better. Even if you have to make your own scales, cap on these.\010Iron Greaves (36)-Earth Crystal, Iron Scales x2, Cotton Thread, Leather Highboots--Same pattern you'll use again with Steel Scales, same sub-craft of Leathercraft 5, same principles of camping the AH, etc. However, just use left-over Iron Scales from Iron Cuisses because the Greaves cost almost as much to make as the Cuisses but NPC back for less.\010War Pick (38)-Fire Crystal, Ash Lumber, Steel Ingot--Get the Steel Ingot for around 3.5k or so and this will NPC at Sandy guild for a profit, in batches. Woodworking 6, which can Ash Lumber, is required as a sub-craft. Easier than finding cheap Iron Scales, but not sure on the Guilds' tolerance for a lot of these at once.\010TEST ITEM: War Pick: Fire Crystal, Steel Ingot, Ash Lumber: Beware the Woodworking 6 sub-craft; it's just enough to cause issues. Make or buy it, whichever is cheaper.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="41-50\010\010Iron Mittens (41)-Earth Crystal, Iron Sheet, Lizard Skin--Leathercraft 10 as a sub-craft. A cause for concern if your skill is only at 5, but not that big a worry otherwise. This is the first Iron Sheet armor you'll be making and begins your need for large amounts of Iron Sheets. The Iron Mittens sell to NPCs at the Bastok Guild this time, for almost the cost of an Iron Ingot. Make these until 39-40 tops.\010Iron Subligar (45)-Earth Crystal, Iron Sheet, Lizard Skin, Cotton Cloth--Leathercraft 12 this time, so make sure you have turned in a Rabbit Mantle to the Leathercraft Guild in Sandy. These sell for so much more than the Iron Mittens, but barely break-even at best. Make these to cap for the simple reason that it's better than the alternatives. \010Mythril Pick (47)-Fire Crystal, Mythril Ingot, Elm Lumber--TEST ITEM. These sell for between 7400-8300 (depending on recent sales to the Guildshop) and cost 7k-ish to make, depending on how cheaply you can get the lumber and the ingots. Woodworking 8 as a subcraft. Frankly, again, if you can NPC these at a profit while getting skill, that's the best bet by far. Also, sell decently considering their use in getting Map of Mount Zhayolm.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="51-60\010\010Darksteel Ingot (52)-Fire Crystal, Darksteel Ore, Iron Ore x3--Back in the profits again. A commonly used craft item that sells well in singles and stacks. Used in Gobbiebag quests as well. The only thing to beware is that these are a common product of Mine-bots and Gil-Farmers that use Mining. Remember to never buy Iron Ore above 675, factor that into what you pay for the Darksteel Ore, and craft like a maniac. Cap on these and enjoy the best way to regenerate your gil that Smithing can offer.\010Gorget (53)-Fire Crystal, Iron Sheet, Sheep Leather--A good level of skill for almost break-even. This was recommended to me by Danallen for those too cheap or too poor for a ton of Darksteel Ores. Also, a good extra level. The ingots are 2.7k, the gorgets sell for 3060 at the Bastok Guild, so 360 gil for a Fire Crystal and Sheep Leather.\010Darksteel Sheet (55)-Fire Crystal, Darksteel Ingot--Adding these into the mix kinda late, but a bad sequence of Steel Cuisses synths wasted a ton of carefully-hoarded Steel Scales, despite being a Full Moon + Darksday, so I need the extra little oomph provided.\010Steel Cuisses (55)-Earth Crystal, Steel Scales x2, Cotton Thread, Leather Trousers--Camp the AH for Steel Scales, at 2k each or less. Make these in Sandy and be aware of the sub-craft of Leathercraft 5. However, you do all that, these NPC to the Guildshop at break-even or better. Cap on these. (Seems familiar? I copy-and-pasted the Iron Cuisses text here and changed Iron to Steel; it really was that simple).\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="51-60\010\010Steel Greaves (56)-Earth Crystal, Steel Scales x2, Cotton Thread, Leather Highboots--Same pattern you used with Iron Scales, same sub-craft of Leathercraft 5, same principles of camping the AH, etc. The difference between this and Iron Greaves is that this extra level isn't optional this time as the next craft I recommend is a bit more advanced. Quite a bit more...\010TEST ITEM: Darksteel Falchion--Fire Crystal, Steel Ingot, Darksteel Ingot x3: Make or buy as you can. Last one before 70.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="61-70\010\010Nodowa (66)-Earth Crystal, Silk Thread, Iron Sheet--Yes, you are reading this right. A 10-level jump. And boy do these eat your gil. But they are decently close to break-even if you sell them to Silver Owl of the Tenshodo in Port Bastok (you are a member, right?). The only good news I can offer is that the sheer scale of the jump offers a better chance at .2 skill-ups than previously. \010Dweomer Steel (67)-Fire Crystal, Swamp Ore, Iron Ore x3--The Swamp Ore is from Unions in Campaign Battle, fairly common and cheap on the AH. While it costs less than Nodowa to make, it sells for less as well and space can become an issue if you are trying to do a ton at once. Still, 60+ is kinda barren for Smithing at this stretch. Technically used for Succor to the Sidhe items, 8~9 of them at 67 or less, that you can sell to friends.\010Darksteel Cap (72)-Fire Crystal, Darksteel Sheet, Tiger Leather x2--These are used as the base item in about 3-4 high level Bonecraft synths, and thus sell decently at a profit. Subcraft of Leather is in the mid/upper-30s, but this synth only makes sense if you can make your own leather (right now, that's 1/3~1/4 the price of buying on AH). Only make to AH, and a better use of Darksteel Sheets than I've found for skilling up.\010Darksteel Pick (73)-Fire Crystal, Elm Lumber, Darksteel Ingot--These NPC for over 10k to the right guild NPC and the ingots are cheaper than ever. Great way to get to 70+.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mythril Picks\010\010Mythril pick is a great item to skillup on as it sells to guild npc in al zahabi for 8.3k to 9.3k\010Looked into these and agreed. I've added them to my guide as another example of skilling while NPCing the product for profit. Smithing has some good ones. Thanks! --Ctownwoody 16:35, 25 April 2009 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Repeating Xbows\010\010if you have woodworking 88+ bone 46+ then making repeating crossbows is a nice way to get smithing to 59, its what i did and it made me a profit plus even made a machine crossbow ---Dclinc\010There are a few reasons for not including this. First, I want the guide to function equally well for all characters, including crafting mules, so there goes the Woodworking 88. Second, these typically sell okay, maybe 8 or so a week on Asura, which is far too slow for doing these for skill. --Ctownwoody 14:50, September 5, 2009 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="War Pick\010\010--Baracca 15:35, September 11, 2009 (UTC)I used War Picks to get to lvl 38.\194\160 The steel ignots are easier to obtain than Iron Scales and they sell for just under 4K to even a normal NPC.\194\160 When I worked with these, I think I even made a small profit on the synths.\194\160 Also the sub-craft of Woodworking offers a different option to Leathercrafting. As a side note, I sold around 16 each \"day\" to the guild. Over the course of 3 days, I dont think that there was a drop of more than a couple hundred gil. Im not sure how fast they go back up though.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Darksteel Ingots / Sheets\010\010As noted by the OP, darksteel ingots can be the profits of mine-bots and gilfarmers. However, they are also produced in large quantities by higher-level smiths as the product of desynthesis. Although darksteel sheets sell more slowly, they often return more on the investment in darksteel ore and may be more profitable for someone who is only taking the craft to 60. There are generally less people producing darksteel sheets than darksteel ingots, so you'll find the market less crowded (albeit slower). If Darksteel Ingots are selling for break even or worse (i.e. crowded), look into making your money back with sheets. --Ezz 18:47, October 3, 2009 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Cheaper Bridge from 56~60?\010\010Dweomer Steel (Smithing 67)\010Fire Crystal\0103 Iron Ore\0101 Swamp Ore\010Either recipe you use, you're basically converting gil to skillups. Since the introduction of the Campaign Union system, I found that I could get my Swamp ores cheaply, in most cases less than the price of Iron Ore from the guild. If you make Nodowa, that's 4 Iron Ores for the Ingot (assuming you produce the sheets yourself) plus 2 Fire Crystals (1 to make the ingot, 1 to make the sheet) plus a Silk Thread and an Earth Crystal. When the Kupower \"Artisan's Advantage\" was in effect I could break even on that synth because I wasn't losing too many mats, but I found the Dweomer ingots to be much more cost-efficient and inventory-friendly means to getting 60.\010(I feel really funny even posting this, Ctown... your guides are Holy Writ to me. Thanks for helping make Asura and FFXI great! ^^) Aeryck's Obnoxious Sig 22:33, November 26, 2009 (UTC)\010Good idea, I am adding this to the guide. However, Nodowa sell for 2.7k~ish and Dweomer Steel for 1k~ish, so the Nodowa make up for the cost of Silk Thread + Earth Crystal extra. And you should be buying the Iron Ingots from the Guild directly, once you finish making them. --Ctownwoody 23:29, November 26, 2009 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NPCing to guild\010\010the guild vendor in whitegate will buy the items from both bastok and sandy guilds. make sure to check there to make sure you get the best prices for your items. --Yjhuoh 02:29, April 22, 2010 (UTC)\010Good idea, added WG to the guide as a possible selling spot. I tend to not invoke it so that mules have access. --Ctownwoody 03:49, April 22, 2010 (UTC)\010Another quick update, I managed to sell Mythril Picks to the smithing vendor in WG for 11k. Smithing vendor in sandy paid me 9.9k. So definitely make these to cap. Also, that last response was like instant!--Yjhuoh 00:02, April 25, 2010 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Bronze Sheets till 4?\010\010I used the Bronze Ingots that I made getting to level 2 and turned them into Bronze Sheets until 4. This way, the gap between Ingots and Scales was a tad smaller, and I was going to use Sheets to make Scales anyway. Should we add this? --squingynaut 15:27, May 11, 2010 (UTC)\010Let me research it a bit but when I wrote this, Bronze Ingots are much more valuable than Bronze Sheets or Bronze Scales. That's why I proposed saving/selling the ingots as opposed to turning them into sheets. But I should re-check it. --Ctownwoody 02:43, May 12, 2010 (UTC)\010 Buying anything from the guild (especially in singles) is usually a huge pain due to the forced waiting between purchases. I found it easier to just buy three stacks of ingots and convert them to sheets to get close to 4, and then just buy sheets from the guild shop (almost always minimum price, something like 84 gil each) and then just making scales to level 10 (which I believe is what the guide suggests). It turns out that this is much cheaper than buying the ores and making ingots yourself, and then making sheets out of the ingots (two crystals per sheet, and factoring in the potential breaks when synthing). --Yjhuoh 08:16, May 12, 2010 (UTC)\010Okay, prices at AH and availability at guild now mean that saving Bronze Ingots isn't as imperative as it once was. Changing guide. --Ctownwoody 15:45, May 15, 2010 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Musketoon?\010\010Have you considered Musketoons at 43 (Bronze Ingotx2, Brass Ingot, Willow Lumber). They go for a loss, but I got 5 levels of skill for just over 90k, which is very cheap compared to the cost of steel. They also easily put you in range for Mythril Picks, which is a bonus. Just curious because it worked for me. InfamousDS 05:56, May 13, 2010 (UTC)\010Yeah, I checked this out. The upfront costs are a lot less but the net cost is actually higher. --Ctownwoody 15:36, May 15, 2010 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Tin ore as a bridge to 15?\010\010When crafting, I try to avoid more than 5 skill level gaps because you get no skillup on breaks.\010In the early stages of smithing, I think the cheap tin ores that can be gotten for 30 gil from the guild deserve a mention.\010They're ideal to go from boltheads to 15 before starting on iron ingots, so that when you do start on the more expensive irons, you can at least get skillups as a consolation prize.\010Thoughts? --Findanniin 09:59, May 4, 2011 (UTC)\010Bolthead should cap at 15, most types anyway. Still, if you are left a few skill short of 15, then, yes Tin Ingots are a way to get to cap. That said, Tin Ingots don't make anything useful, so it's mostly going to sell for nil. Keep your options open, though.--Ctownwoody 14:24, May 4, 2011 (UTC)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_7679",["title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2023/Guide",["page_title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2023/Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Sunbreeze_Festival_2023%2FGuide",["revision"]="112232",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="HorizonXI Wiki - Sunbreeze Festival 2023 Guide\010\010Data collection for the Sunbreeze Festival 2023. The event concluded on September 1st 2023.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Whack-a-mandie\010\010Purgonorgo West Beach\010 Invasive Mandie mobs spawns along the shore\010 Strike them with a club hammer (obtained for free from Info Moogle on the North beach) to kill and receive points\010 Mandragoras without flowers are worth 1 point\010 Mandragoras with flowers are worth 2 points\010 Points can be redeemed at the Reward Moogle on the North Beach\010 Mandies can currently be killed with DRG Jump and MNK Chi Blast. This is considered an exploit, and GMs are punishing people for it.\010Rewards\010 Swimming Top: 20 points\010Hume Top (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet (Hume M)\010Mithra Top (Mithra)\010Elvaan Top (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet (Galka)\010Tarutaru Top (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot (Tarutaru M)\010 Swimming Bottom: 20 points (requires Swimming Top purchase)\010Hume Shorts (Hume F)\010Hume Trunks (Hume M)\010Elvaan Shorts (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Trunks (Elvaan M)\010Mithra Shorts (Mithra)\010Galka Trunks (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Trunks (Tarutaru M)\010 Festive Yukata: 25 points\010 Omina Yukata (F)\010 Onoko Yukata (M)\010 Upgrade Yukata: 35 points (requires Festive Yukata purchase)\010 Lady's Yukata (F)\010 Lord's Yukata (M)\010 Goshikitenge: 5 points\010 Marine Bliss: 5 points\010 Muteppo: 15 points",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NM Midgardsormr [Horizon change]\010\010Spawns around H-10 in the center of the map\010 Timed spawn every 6 hours\010 Unclaimable\010 Frequently uses Poisonga II, Sleepga, Graviga, and Breakga. Poison potions are recommended.\010 Occasionally uses Death.\010 Can be Aspired.\010 Midgardsormr can be stunned, and it may be prudent to save TP for stunning Weapon Skills in order to prevent Breakga and Death\010 Speak to Rewards Moogle on the North Beach upon defeat to receive: \010 Custom Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010 Custom Top +1 (Hume F)\010 Savage Top +1 (Mithra)\010 Magna Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010 Magna Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010 Elder Gilet +1 (Galka)\010 Wonder Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010 Wonder Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010 Subsequent kills do not seem to yield any additional reward from the moogle\010 You do not need the NQ top to obtain the +1 as a reward.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Racetrack Betting\010\010North Beach\010 Speak to Racing Moogle during the betting period to bet on one of the four NPC racers\010 Betting times start at 5:00 , 11:00 , 17:00 , 23:00 (In-game clock times)\010 Races start 1 in game hour after betting starts -- 6:00 , 12:00 , 18:00 , 0:00\010 When placing a bet with Racing Moogle , you know it was successful if you get the dialogue: \"So be it! Come and see me after the race. Good luck!\" If you did not get that message, then you need to talk to Racing Moogle again to lock in your bet (probably due to there being a huge influx of players placing bets at the same time).\010 On a win, you can double your bet (this is not necessary to receive a HQ swim suit.)\010 If you have the NQ swim suit pieces from Whack-a-Mandie, you will additionally receive the HQ version on a successful bet\010Hume Top +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Shorts +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010Hume Trunks +1 (Hume M)\010Mithra Shorts +1 (Mithra)\010Mithra Top +1 (Mithra)\010Elvaan Shorts +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010Elvaan Trunks +1 (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet +1 (Galka)\010Galka Trunks +1 (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010Tarutaru Trunks +1 (Tarutaru M)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Vendors\010\010Marcella - sells different Snow Cones",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Clamming\010\010Clamming - Note: Clamming is not specifically part of the Sunbreeze event, and will still be available after the event ends.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Goldfish Scooping\010\010Goldfish Scooping is a mini-game that can be played during Sunbreeze Festival. It is a special type of fishing where your objective is to accumulate Goldfish Points by catching and trading these golfish to Special Event NPCs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Locations\010\010There are four locations where you can participate in the event. At each location is an NPC who sells event-related items and hands out rewards. Each NPC is sitting near the edge of a small body of water in which the goldfish may be caught.\010Location\010Event NPC\010Body of Water\010West Ronfaure (G-10)\010Saradorial\010Knightswell\010South Gustaberg (E-8)\010Fish Eyes\010The Fumaroles\010East Sarutabaruta (H-8)\010Kesha Shopehllok\010Nompipi River\010Rabao (F-7)\010Mei\010Rabao Spring",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Equipment\010\010In order to start with the mini-game you need to get a Goldfish Basket and several super scoops from the NPCs listed above.\010The Goldfish Basket is obtained for free by choosing the \"I'd like a bowl\" option.\010Super Scoops can be purchased for 100 gil each by choosing the \"I want to buy a scoop\" option, or in exchange for points obtained from trading fish (see below).\010Equip the Goldfish Basket in your Range slot, and Super Scoops in your Ammo slot.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Points\010\010Trade goldfish to any of the goldfish scooping NPCs to earn points. Points may be redeemed at the same NPCs.\010 Tiny Goldfish = 1\010 Black Bubble-Eye = 2\010 Lionhead = 10\010 Pearlscale = 30\010 Calico Comet = 30",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="West Ronfaure Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: White Butterfly",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="East Sarutabaruta Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: Glowfly",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="South Gustaberg Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: Bell Cricket",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rabao Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: White Butterfly, Glowfly, or Bell Cricket. It is unclear if this is intended behavior.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_23678",["title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2024/Guide",["page_title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2024/Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Sunbreeze_Festival_2024%2FGuide",["revision"]="112233",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="HorizonXI Wiki - Sunbreeze Festival 2024 Guide\010\010Data collection for the Sunbreeze Festival 2024. The event will conclude on September 1st 2024.\010Date is not confirmed but based off 2023's end date\010Unknown if any changes have been made from last year",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Whack-a-mandie\010\010Purgonorgo West Beach\010 Invasive Mandie mobs spawns along the shore\010 Strike them with a club hammer (obtained for free from Info Moogle on the North beach) to kill and receive points\010 Mandragoras without flowers are worth 1 point\010 Mandragoras with flowers are worth 2 points\010 Points can be redeemed at the Rewards Moogle on the North Beach\010 Mandies can currently be killed with DRG Jump and MNK Chi Blast. This is considered an exploit, and GMs are punishing people for it.\010Rewards\010 Swimming Top: 20 points\010Hume Top (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet (Hume M)\010Mithra Top (Mithra)\010Elvaan Top (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet (Galka)\010Tarutaru Top (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot (Tarutaru M)\010 Swimming Bottom: 20 points (requires Swimming Top purchase)\010Hume Shorts (Hume F)\010Hume Trunks (Hume M)\010Elvaan Shorts (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Trunks (Elvaan M)\010Mithra Shorts (Mithra)\010Galka Trunks (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Trunks (Tarutaru M)\010 Festive Yukata: 25 points\010 Omina Yukata (F)\010 Onoko Yukata (M)\010 Upgrade Yukata: 35 points (requires Festive Yukata purchase)\010 Lady's Yukata (F)\010 Lord's Yukata (M)\010 Goshikitenge: 5 points\010 Marine Bliss: 5 points\010 Muteppo: 15 points",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NM Midgardsormr [Horizon change]\010\010Spawns around Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle (H-10) in the center of the map\010 Timed spawn every 6 hours\010 Unclaimable\010 Frequently uses Poisonga II, Sleepga, Graviga, and Breakga. Poison potions are recommended.\010 Occasionally uses Death.\010 Can be Aspired.\010 Midgardsormr can be stunned, and it may be prudent to save TP for stunning Weapon Skills in order to prevent Breakga and Death\010 Speak to Rewards Moogle on the North Beach upon defeat to receive: \010 Custom Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010 Custom Top +1 (Hume F)\010 Savage Top +1 (Mithra)\010 Magna Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010 Magna Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010 Elder Gilet +1 (Galka)\010 Wonder Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010 Wonder Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010 Subsequent kills do not seem to yield any additional reward from the moogle\010 You do not need the NQ top to obtain the +1 as a reward.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Racetrack Betting\010\010North Beach\010Races last about 4 minutes\010Depends when the final runner finishes\010Betting times start at 5:00 , 11:00 , 17:00 , 23:00 (In-game clock times)\010Races start 1 in game hour after betting starts -- 6:00 , 12:00 , 18:00 , 0:00\010 Speak to Racing Moogle during the betting period to bet on one of the four NPC racers\010When placing a bet with Racing Moogle , you know it was successful if you get the dialogue: \"So be it! Come and see me after the race. Good luck!\" If you did not get that message, then you need to talk to Racing Moogle again to lock in your bet (probably due to there being a huge influx of players placing bets at the same time).\010 On a win, you can double your bet (this is not nessecary to receive a HQ swim suit.)\010 If you have the NQ swim suit pieces from Whack-a-Mandie, you will additionally receive the HQ version on a successful bet\010Hume Top +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Shorts +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010Hume Trunks +1 (Hume M)\010Mithra Shorts +1 (Mithra)\010Mithra Top +1 (Mithra)\010Elvaan Shorts +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010Elvaan Trunks +1 (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet +1 (Galka)\010Galka Trunks +1 (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010Tarutaru Trunks +1 (Tarutaru M)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Vendors\010\010Marcella - sells different Snow Cones",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Clamming\010\010Clamming - Note: Clamming is not specifically part of the Sunbreeze event, and will still be available after the event ends.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Goldfish Scooping\010\010Goldfish Scooping is a mini-game that can be played during Sunbreeze Festival. It is a special type of fishing where your objective is to accumulate Goldfish Points by catching and trading these golfish to Special Event NPCs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Locations\010\010There are four locations where you can participate in the event. At each location is an NPC who sells event-related items and hands out rewards. Each NPC is sitting near the edge of a small body of water in which the goldfish may be caught.\010Location\010Event NPC\010Body of Water\010West Ronfaure (G-10)\010Saradorial\010Knightswell\010South Gustaberg (E-8)\010Fish Eyes\010The Fumaroles\010East Sarutabaruta (H-8)\010Kesha Shopehllok\010Nompipi River\010Rabao (F-7)\010Mei\010Rabao Spring",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Equipment\010\010In order to start with the mini-game you need to get a Goldfish Basket and several super scoops from the NPCs listed above.\010The Goldfish Basket is obtained for free by choosing the \"I'd like a bowl\" option.\010Super Scoops can be purchased for 100 gil each by choosing the \"I want to buy a scoop\" option, or in exchange for points obtained from trading fish (see below).\010Equip the Goldfish Basket in your Range slot, and Super Scoops in your Ammo slot.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Points\010\010Trade goldfish to any of the goldfish scooping NPCs to earn points. Points may be redeemed at the same NPCs.\010 Tiny Goldfish = 1\010 Black Bubble-Eye = 2\010 Lionhead = 10\010 Pearlscale = 30\010 Calico Comet = 30",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rewards\010\010the 80 point box will not be obtainable at this time, it is a side effect of the menu and is not easy to edit out",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="West Ronfaure Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: White Butterfly",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="East Sarutabaruta Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: Glowfly",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="South Gustaberg Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: Bell Cricket\010 (80) Mystery box part four: Iratsume Happi [Wiki: needs verification] \010 unknown if a real option, currently unable to purchase the box.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rabao Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: White Butterfly, Glowfly, or Bell Cricket. It is unclear if this is intended behavior.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_53769",["title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2025/Guide",["page_title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2025/Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Sunbreeze_Festival_2025%2FGuide",["revision"]="122715",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="HorizonXI Wiki - Sunbreeze Festival 2025 Guide\010\010Data collection for the Sunbreeze Festival 2025. The event started August 1st 2025 and will conclude on September 1st 2025.\010Unknown if any changes have been made from last year",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle\010\010Info to be added",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Whack-a-mandie\010\010Purgonorgo West Beach\010 Invasive Mandie mobs spawns along the shore\010 Strike them with a club hammer (obtained for free from Info Moogle on the North beach) to kill and receive points\010 Mandragoras without flowers are worth 1 point\010 Mandragoras with flowers are worth 2 points\010 Points can be redeemed at the Rewards Moogle on the North Beach\010 Mandies can currently be killed with DRG Jump and MNK Chi Blast. This is considered an exploit, and GMs are punishing people for it.\010Rewards\010 Swimming Top: 20 points\010Hume Top (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet (Hume M)\010Mithra Top (Mithra)\010Elvaan Top (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet (Galka)\010Tarutaru Top (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot (Tarutaru M)\010 Swimming Bottom: 20 points (requires Swimming Top purchase)\010Hume Shorts (Hume F)\010Hume Trunks (Hume M)\010Elvaan Shorts (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Trunks (Elvaan M)\010Mithra Shorts (Mithra)\010Galka Trunks (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Trunks (Tarutaru M)\010 Festive Yukata: 25 points\010 Omina Yukata (F)\010 Onoko Yukata (M)\010 Upgrade Yukata: 35 points (requires Festive Yukata purchase)\010 Lady's Yukata (F)\010 Lord's Yukata (M)\010 Goshikitenge: 5 points\010 Marine Bliss: 5 points\010 Muteppo: 15 points",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NM Midgardsormr [Horizon change]\010\010Spawns around Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle (H-10) in the center of the map\010 Timed spawn every 6 hours [Wiki: needs verification] \010 Unclaimable\010 Frequently uses Poisonga II, Sleepga, Graviga, and Breakga. Poison potions are recommended.\010 Occasionally uses Death.\010 Can be Aspired.\010 Midgardsormr can be stunned, and it may be prudent to save TP for stunning Weapon Skills in order to prevent Breakga and Death\010 Speak to Rewards Moogle on the North Beach upon defeat to receive: \010 Custom Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010 Custom Top +1 (Hume F)\010 Savage Top +1 (Mithra)\010 Magna Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010 Magna Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010 Elder Gilet +1 (Galka)\010 Wonder Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010 Wonder Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010 Subsequent kills do not seem to yield any additional reward from the moogle\010 You do not need the NQ top to obtain the +1 as a reward.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Racetrack Betting\010\010North Beach\010Races last about 4 minutes\010Depends when the final runner finishes\010Betting times start at 5:00 , 11:00 , 17:00 , 23:00 (In-game clock times)\010Races start 1 in game hour after betting starts -- 6:00 , 12:00 , 18:00 , 0:00\010 Speak to Racing Moogle during the betting period to bet on one of the four NPC racers\010When placing a bet with Racing Moogle , you know it was successful if you get the dialogue: \"So be it! Come and see me after the race. Good luck!\" If you did not get that message, then you need to talk to Racing Moogle again to lock in your bet (probably due to there being a huge influx of players placing bets at the same time).\010 On a win, you can double your bet (this is not nessecary to receive a HQ swim suit.)\010 If you have the NQ swim suit pieces from Whack-a-Mandie, you will additionally receive the HQ version on a successful bet\010Hume Top +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Shorts +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010Hume Trunks +1 (Hume M)\010Mithra Shorts +1 (Mithra)\010Mithra Top +1 (Mithra)\010Elvaan Shorts +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010Elvaan Trunks +1 (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet +1 (Galka)\010Galka Trunks +1 (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010Tarutaru Trunks +1 (Tarutaru M)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Vendors\010\010Marcella - sells different Snow Cones",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Clamming\010\010Clamming - Note: Clamming is not specifically part of the Sunbreeze event, and will still be available after the event ends.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Goldfish Scooping\010\010Goldfish Scooping is a mini-game that can be played during Sunbreeze Festival. It is a special type of fishing where your objective is to accumulate Goldfish Points by catching and trading these golfish to Special Event NPCs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Locations\010\010There are four locations where you can participate in the event. At each location is an NPC who sells event-related items and hands out rewards. Each NPC is sitting near the edge of a small body of water in which the goldfish may be caught.\010Location\010Event NPC\010Body of Water\010West Ronfaure (G-10)\010Saradorial\010Knightswell\010South Gustaberg (E-8)\010Fish Eyes\010The Fumaroles\010East Sarutabaruta (H-8)\010Kesha Shopehllok\010Nompipi River\010Rabao (F-7)\010Mei\010Rabao Spring",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Equipment\010\010In order to start with the mini-game you need to get a Goldfish Basket and several super scoops from the NPCs listed above.\010The Goldfish Basket is obtained for free by choosing the \"I'd like a bowl\" option.\010Super Scoops can be purchased for 100 gil each by choosing the \"I want to buy a scoop\" option, or in exchange for points obtained from trading fish (see below).\010Equip the Goldfish Basket in your Range slot, and Super Scoops in your Ammo slot.\010/fish in or next to water to begin.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Points\010\010Trade goldfish to any of the goldfish scooping NPCs to earn points. Points may be redeemed at the same NPCs.\010 Tiny Goldfish = 1\010 Black Bubble-Eye = 2\010 Lionhead = 10\010 Pearlscale = 30\010 Calico Comet = 30",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rewards [Wiki: needs verification]\010\010NOTE: The RSE swimsuit bottoms are not the same as the bottoms you get from Whack-a-Mandie! You cannot upgrade them to the +1 version that improves clamming results. The swimsuit bottoms from Goldfish Scooping are purely cosmetic.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="West Ronfaure Goldfishing Rewards\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: White Butterfly\010 (80) Mystery box part four: Ominaeshi Yukata (or possibly male equivalent)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="East Sarutabaruta Goldfishing Rewards\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: Glowfly",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="South Gustaberg Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: Bell Cricket\010 (80) Mystery box part four: Iratsume Happi [Wiki: needs verification] \010 unknown if a real option, currently unable to purchase the box.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rabao Goldfishing Rewards\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE swimsuit bottoms\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: White Butterfly, Glowfly, or Bell Cricket. It is unclear if this is intended behavior.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_57689",["title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2026/Guide",["page_title"]="Sunbreeze Festival 2026/Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Sunbreeze_Festival_2026%2FGuide",["revision"]="123020",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="HorizonXI Wiki - Sunbreeze Festival 2026 Guide\010\010Data collection for the Sunbreeze Festival 2026. The event started August 28th 2026.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle\010\010Info to be added",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Whack-a-mandie\010\010Purgonorgo East, West and Southern beaches.\010 Invasive Mandie mobs spawn near these shores.\010 Strike them with a club hammer (obtained for free from Info Moogle on the North beach) to kill and receive points\010 Mandragoras without flowers are worth 1 point\010 Mandragoras with flowers are worth 2 points\010 Korrigans (black) are worth 5 points.\010 Adeniums (larger and pink colored) are worth 7 points.\010 Korrigans with a gold flower are worth 15 points!\010 Points can be redeemed at the Rewards Moogle on the North Beach\010 Mandies can currently be killed with DRG Jump and MNK Chi Blast. This is considered an exploit, and GMs are punishing people for it. (This text in the guide is a holdover from previous events. Don't verify if it still works, just use the hammer.)\010Rewards\010 Swimming Top: 20 points\010Hume Top (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet (Hume M)\010Mithra Top (Mithra)\010Elvaan Top (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet (Galka)\010Tarutaru Top (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot (Tarutaru M)\010 Swimming Bottom: 20 points (requires Swimming Top purchase)\010Hume Shorts (Hume F)\010Hume Trunks (Hume M)\010Elvaan Shorts (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Trunks (Elvaan M)\010Mithra Shorts (Mithra)\010Galka Trunks (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Trunks (Tarutaru M)\010 Festive Yukata: 25 points\010 Omina Yukata (F)\010 Onoko Yukata (M)\010 Upgrade Yukata: 35 points (requires Festive Yukata purchase. Does not replace said yukata.)\010 Lady's Yukata (F)\010 Lord's Yukata (M)\010 Goshikitenge: 5 points\010 Marine Bliss: 5 points\010 Muteppo: 15 points",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="NM Midgardsormr [Horizon change]\010\010Spawns around Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle (H-10) in the center of the map\010 Timed spawn every 6 hours [Wiki: needs verification] \010 Unclaimable\010 Frequently uses Poisonga II, Sleepga, Graviga, and Breakga. Poison potions are recommended.\010 Occasionally uses Death.\010 Can be Aspired.\010 Midgardsormr can be stunned, and it may be prudent to save TP for stunning Weapon Skills in order to prevent Breakga and Death\010If you received credit, flavor text will print in your chat log describing what you take from Midgardsormr's body, and that you should bring it to the moogles on the isle.\010 Speak to Rewards Moogle on the North Beach upon defeat to receive: \010 Custom Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010 Custom Top +1 (Hume F)\010 Savage Top +1 (Mithra)\010 Magna Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010 Magna Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010 Elder Gilet +1 (Galka)\010 Wonder Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010 Wonder Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010 Subsequent kills do not seem to yield any additional reward from the moogle\010 You do not need the NQ top to obtain the +1 as a reward.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Racetrack Betting\010\010North Beach\010Races last about 4 minutes\010Depends when the final runner finishes\010Betting times start at 5:00 , 11:00 , 17:00 , 23:00 (In-game clock times)\010Races start 1 in game hour after betting starts -- 6:00 , 12:00 , 18:00 , 0:00\010 Speak to Racing Moogle during the betting period to bet on one of the four NPC racers. You can bet values of 500, 1000, 5000, or 10000 gil. This value is doubled and rewarded upon victory, but irrelevant to receiving the event swimsuits.\010When placing a bet with Racing Moogle , you know it was successful if you get the dialogue: \"So be it! Come and see me after the race. Good luck!\" If you did not get that message, then you need to talk to Racing Moogle again to lock in your bet (probably due to there being a huge influx of players placing bets at the same time).\010 If you have the NQ swim suit pieces from Whack-a-Mandie, you will additionally receive the HQ version on a successful bet\010Hume Top +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Shorts +1 (Hume F)\010Hume Gilet +1 (Hume M)\010Hume Trunks +1 (Hume M)\010Mithra Shorts +1 (Mithra)\010Mithra Top +1 (Mithra)\010Elvaan Shorts +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Top +1 (Elvaan F)\010Elvaan Gilet +1 (Elvaan M)\010Elvaan Trunks +1 (Elvaan M)\010Galka Gilet +1 (Galka)\010Galka Trunks +1 (Galka)\010Tarutaru Shorts +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Top +1 (Tarutaru F)\010Tarutaru Maillot +1 (Tarutaru M)\010Tarutaru Trunks +1 (Tarutaru M)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Vendors\010\010Marcella - sells different Snow Cones",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Clamming\010\010Clamming - Note: Clamming is not specifically part of the Sunbreeze event, and will still be available after the event ends.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Goldfish Scooping\010\010Goldfish Scooping is a mini-game that can be played during Sunbreeze Festival. It is a special type of fishing where your objective is to accumulate Goldfish Points by catching and trading these golfish to Special Event NPCs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Locations\010\010There are four locations where you can participate in the event. At each location is an NPC who sells event-related items and hands out rewards. Each NPC is sitting near the edge of a small body of water in which the goldfish may be caught.\010Location\010Event NPC\010Body of Water\010West Ronfaure (G-10)\010Saradorial\010Knightswell\010South Gustaberg (E-8)\010Fish Eyes\010The Fumaroles\010East Sarutabaruta (H-8)\010Kesha Shopehllok\010Nompipi River\010Rabao (F-7)\010Mei\010Rabao Spring",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Equipment\010\010In order to start with the mini-game you need to get a Goldfish Basket and several super scoops from the NPCs listed above.\010The Goldfish Basket is obtained for free by choosing the \"I'd like a bowl\" option.\010Super Scoops can be purchased for 100 gil each by choosing the \"I want to buy a scoop\" option, or in exchange for points obtained from trading fish (see below).\010Equip the Goldfish Basket in your Range slot, and Super Scoops in your Ammo slot.\010/fish in or next to water to begin. There is no goldfish scooping minigame on Horizon. Instead you just go through the regular fishing minigame for goldfish scooping.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Points\010\010Trade goldfish to any of the goldfish scooping NPCs to earn points. Points may be redeemed at the same NPCs.\010 Tiny Goldfish = 1\010 Black Bubble-Eye = 2\010 Lionhead = 10\010 Pearlscale = 30\010 Calico Comet = 30",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rewards [Wiki: needs verification]\010\010NOTE: The RSE swimsuit bottoms are not the same as the bottoms you get from Whack-a-Mandie! You cannot upgrade them to the +1 version that improves clamming results. The swimsuit bottoms from Goldfish Scooping are purely cosmetic.\010Attempting to redeem for a rare tagged item that you can't receive due to already having it will still consume your points.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="West Ronfaure Goldfishing Rewards\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE Swimsuit Bottoms for your character\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: 1-5 [Wiki: needs verification] White Butterfly\010 (80) Mystery box part four: Otokoeshi Yukata (If character is Male) OR Ominaeshi Yukata (If character is Female)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="East Sarutabaruta Goldfishing Rewards\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE Swimsuit Bottoms for your character\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: 1-5 [Wiki: needs verification] Glowfly\010 (80) Mystery box part four: Otokoeshi Yukata (If character is Male) OR Ominaeshi Yukata (If character is Female)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="South Gustaberg Goldfishing Rewards:\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE Swimsuit Bottoms for your character\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: 1-5 [Wiki: needs verification] Bell Cricket\010 (80) Mystery box part four: Otokoeshi Yukata (If character is Male) OR Ominaeshi Yukata (If character is Female)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rabao Goldfishing Rewards\010\010(5) A Super Scoop\010 (15) A bag of fireworks\010 (25) Large fans\010 (30) A colored drop\010 (50) The mystery box: 12 Spirit Masque\010 (60) A stellar surprise: RSE Swimsuit Bottoms for your character\010 (65) The other mystery box: Goldfish Set\010 (70) Yet another mystery box: 1-5 [Wiki: needs verification] White Butterfly, 1-5 [Wiki: needs verification] Glowfly, or 1-5 [Wiki: needs verification] Bell Cricket. \010 (80) Mystery box part four: Otokoeshi Yukata (If character is Male) OR Ominaeshi Yukata (If character is Female)\010List of RSE Bottoms from Goldfish Scooping:\010Hume Male - Custom Trunks \010Hume Female - Custom Shorts \010Elvaan Male - Magna Trunks \010Elvaan Female - Magna Shorts \010Mithra - Savage Shorts \010Galka - Elder Trunks \010Tarutaru Male - Wonder Trunks \010Tarutaru Female -Wonder Shorts",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_18449",["title"]="Sushomi's Beginners Guide to Healing",["page_title"]="Sushomi's Beginners Guide to Healing",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Sushomi%27s_Beginners_Guide_to_Healing",["revision"]="99776",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]\010So you want to learn more about healing in FFXI? Well, you've come to the right place. This guide will cover the basic aspects of healing, from stat priorities to MP management, as well as recommended addons to help you along the way.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Healing Jobs\010\010Currently on Horizon there are two jobs that are considered main healers; these being Red Mage and White Mage. While there are some slight differences in the playstyles of both, they are equally capable of filling the role as a party healer. In addition to these, there are also a couple of off-meta picks that can work in certain situations, such as Summoner, and even Paladin.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="White Mage\010\010The classic healing job. With access to area of effect cures, status healing, and regen spells, White Mages are a great all-round healer.\010Their high Mind stat also enhances the potency of their debuffs such as Slow and Paralyze.\010White Mages have access to the highest tiers of Clear Mind, making them able to regenerate MP while healing quicker than any other job.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Red Mage\010\010Red mage is often seen as a jack-of-all-trades. Their direct healing and regen abilities are not as strong as a White Mage, but they make up for it with higher enfeebling magic skill, and access to some unique spells.\010Refresh is a staple of the Red Mage toolkit, allowing them to slowly regenerate MP over time, even while not resting, and can be cast on party members as well.\010A Red Mage will not regen MP while healing as quickly as a White Mage, but they also have access to Convert, a job ability at level 40 that lets them swap their current HP and MP values, giving them the ability to carry on healing for longer.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Summoner\010\010Summoner has no innate access to healing spells (besides a few avatar abilities which are on a 1 minute timer), and so needs to rely on their subjob to fill this role. Using White Mage as the sub is generally recommended to give access to status cures and aoe healing where needed.\010Using Summoner as a main healer will be sub-optimal in a lot of party setups as they will not gain access to stronger healing spells until much later than a White/Red Mage due to their subjob always being half the level of their main. However, it can work well in parties where all the melee are using Ninja sub to mitigate the majority of the incoming damage. In this situation, the Summoner can offer support through Hastega and Earthen Ward to keep the healing needed to a minimum.\010Personally, I have main healed as Summmoner up to level 60, and it can work. But I wouldn't recommend it past the mid-levels if you have a Paladin tank (unless they have some crazy MP regen) as the regular incoming damage would likely make it hard to sustain your MP.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Addons\010\010Below are a few addons that I have found useful. This won't be an exhaustive list, nor are any of those listed required to be able to heal.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="HXUI\010\010Provides lots of handy UI options, including HP/MP/TP bars, a better party list, exp bar, etc. The main thing you'll want this for when healing is the ability to see buffs/debuffs on your party members, making it easier to see when someone is missing haste/protect/shell, or when to use those all-important status curing spells.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="hticks\010\010Provides a countdown showing when you will receive your next healing tick while resting. I've often found the countdown can be a second or so off, but it's still a good indication as to how close you are to the next tick so you're not losing out on extra MP you could have had if you'd stayed down 1 second longer.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="targetlines\010\010This is such a useful addon. It provides FFXII-style lines that show the target of specific actions. For example, if a monster decides to go after your Black Mage, it will show a red line going from the enemy to the Black Mage, essentially giving you a quick heads up that your wizard friend may need a bit of healing in the near future.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="ttimers\010\010Not to be confused with the recently deprecated timers addon. Ttimers allows you to see a countdown bar for your buffs on party members, letting you know when they need a new refresh/hate/protect/etc.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="MP Recovered While Healing\010\010Increases the amount of MP restored while resting (/heal). It's a good idea to set up an equipment set with as much MP Recovered While Healing as possible to macro in while you /heal.\010Parties will need to wait on your if you run out of MP, so recovering quickly and ensuring you always have MP can have a huge effect on the amount of experience per hour you and your party can achieve.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Max MP\010\010Your maximum MP affects one thing, and one thing only - how long you can heal before needing to rest (or Convert if you're a Red Mage. It's a useful thing to have, but as a healer it's important to recognise that you could have all the +MP gear in the world, but if you can never recover that MP fast enough then it's really just going to waste.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mind\010\010Mind does a few things for healers. Firstly, it increases the amount you heal for up to a certain point. For example, when you start using cure you'll likely only be healing for ~10 HP. Equip a few pieces of Mind gear, and this will increase slightly. However, your cures will likely cap out very quickly as you also increase your Healing Magic skill. This makes Mind not a great stat to stack purely for healing purposes (Cure V is an exception to this for high level White Mages).\010However, Mind also increases the potency of your white magic enfeebling spells, such as Slow and Paralyze. It can also make these spells easier to land, which in turn can greatly reduce the incoming damage taken by your tank, saving you MP in the long run.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="-Enmity\010\010Enmity is FFXI's version of \"aggro\" or \"hate\". It's a measure of how much the enemy wants to kill you. Every action you take against a monster, or on a player that has engaged that monster, generates enmity.\010Healers are general quite squishy, and therefore it's important that we try not to grab the monster's attention. They tend to notice the person constantly spamming cures on their party members. This is where -enmity gear comes in. The more you have, the less enmity your actions generate. And the more likely you are not to pull aggro off your tank.\010At low levels you are unlikely to find much -enmity on gear, but it's something to keep an eye out for as you level.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Food and Drinks\010\010Food is vitally important when you join an exp party. They offer large stat bonuses for a short time that can easily make the difference between a bad party and a great one. There's a lot of food to choose from, so let's look at some of the options available to you.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Pies\010\010[Template AH: Food / Meals / Sweets]\010Pies are generally use by Black Mages as they offer bonuses to MP and Intelligence, as well as MP recovered while healing if you opt for the High-Quality (+1) versions.\010They come in a variety of flavours such as:\010 Apple Pie / Apple Pie +1\010 Melon Pie / Melon Pie +1\010 Rolanberry Pie / Rolanberry Pie +1\010Can be good if you're mainly looking for a boost to max MP, however, I would say they're probably the worst of these options for healing.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rice Cakes\010\010[Template AH: Food / Meals / Sweets]\010Sweet Rice Cake are a decent food for any kind of mage. They offer MP, Intelligence, Mind, MP recovered while healing, and -Enmity. They're a good mix of all the stats you could possibly want, but don't provide as large a bonus to any one of the stats as other foods in this list. Depending on cost, they're worth a look at though.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Cookies\010\010[Template AH: Food / Meals / Sweets]\010Ginger Cookie and their HQ version, Wizard Cookie are very short duration foods, lasting only 5 minutes each, compared to the usual 30 mins that most foods last. However, they provide +5 and +7 MP recovered while healing respectively, and are quite cheap considering they also stack to 99.\010This is the largest +hMP bonus you will see from food, and is what you'll see a lot of mages using at lower levels. Their only real downside is the short duration means you have to keep eating them regularly and their buff can often wear off while you're resting.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mushroom and Stews\010\010[Template AH: Food / Meals / Vegetables]\010My personal favourite food for healing is the mushroom category, and this includes:\010 Roast Mushroom / Witch Kabob\010 Mushroom Soup / Witch Stew\010These foods provide MP, Mind, MP recovered while healing, and -Enmity. All the stats you could possibly want as a healer. The stews also have the added bonus of lasting for up to 3 hours, so are great for when you know you're going to be in a longer party.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Refresh Drinks\010\010[Template AH: Food / Meals / Drinks]\010I've put these in the food section, but they're technically separate as you can drink these even when you already have a food buff.\010Refresh drinks apply a short buff that regenerates MP slowly over time, which can be great for keeping your healing going in a pinch. They don't stack, but it can be handy to keep some on hand in case of emergency if you can afford them.\010 Orange Juice\010 Apple Juice\010 Pineapple Juice\010 Melon Juice\010 Grape Juice\010 Yagudo Drink",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="MP Management: Tips and Tricks\010\010I've mentioned a few times now that being able to recover your MP quickly is absolutely vital as a healer. After all, without MP we can't do much of anything, and having MP allows our party to continue fighting which increases how quickly we can all gain experience.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Regen spells\010\010These spells are super efficient in terms of MP spent vs healing done. As an example, Regen costs 15 MP, and restores 5 HP every 3 seconds for a total of 125 HP. You would need to cast a little over 4 Cure spells to reach the same amount of HP, which at 8 mp per cast would cost you 32 MP, over double.\010If you have a tank that doesn't have shadows to avoid damage, then keeping regen on them while they fight should definitely be a priority. This is even more important for White Mages as they later gain access to Regen II and Regen III, which can restore massive amounts of HP over time for their MP costs.\010This spell works great for your melee damage dealers as well if they're not actively taking hits. If they're not in danger of dying anytime soon, cast Regen on them and let their HP slowly tick up between fights so you can continue resting to get your MP back.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Watch your healing ticks\010\010When you rest, your first tick of MP regen happens at 20 seconds, and then every 10 seconds thereafter. If you sit down for 18 seconds, and then get up to cast then you've just wasted those 18 seconds and got nothing for it. If you had waited 2 more seconds, then you would have regenerated some MP at least.\010Having an addon like hticks that shows roughly when to expect your next MP regen is great for planning when to cancel your /heal.\010If you're only a few seconds away from that next MP tick, then ask yourself if you NEED to cast your next spell immediately. Is someone in danger of dying if you don't immediately cure them? If not, wait those extra few seconds before you get up to cast. That extra 20 MP you got back may not seem like a lot on its own, but it all adds up over the course of an exp party.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Debuffs Save MP!\010\010Paralyze and Slow are incredibly important debuffs when exping. The first can cause an enemy to completely skip an attack round, while the second increases the delay between their attacks. They cost 6 and 15 MP respectively, which is less than the cost of a Cure II, so even if they only cause an enemy to lose a couple of extra attacks per battle they are well worth it.\010As important as they are though, remember to try and get that extra MP tick off if you can before cancelling /heal. And if you're really running low on MP, don't be afraid to just leave the debuffs off for a fight or two to give yourself more time to recover.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Don't cure just for the sake of it\010\010Casting random cures on party members who don't need it is a good way to waste MP, especially if said party member is also a mage. Mages have to rest often to recover MP, and while doing this they also gain back some HP too. So if your Black Mage has taken a hit and aren't actively being pummeled by the enemy, let them regen some HP naturally rather than wasting MP.\010For non mages, there's nothing wrong with leaving them with a little missing HP if they're not actively taking regular damage. If someone is missing 30 HP out of their 400 HP pool, consider just leaving them like that until they start taking more damage. Even then, ask yourself if regen would be more efficient, even if it overheals a little.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Job Specific Advice\010\010For this section, I'm going to focus only on the two main healers - White and Red mage. The others are niche picks anyway, and likely won't see much use outside of low levels or specific party compositions.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="White Mage\010\010Regen, regen, regen. I've said it before, but regen spells are incredibly MP efficient. If a few of your damage dealers have taken some small hits, cast regen and let them heal over time. Only if they're in danger of taking some big hits or nasty aoe attacks should you worry about immediately curing them.\010 After level 50, you gain access to the Divine Veil trait. This makes Divine Seal double the potency and duration of your Regen spells. This makes for a great combo to help keep your tank topped up while you recover your MP.\010 At level 50 you get Devotion which sacrifices a portion of your HP to recover a party member's MP. Works great with a Paladin tank so they can continue healing themself.\010 Big Curaga spells generate a lot of enmity, and if you don't have a good tank can easily get you killed. If they are absolutely necessary, try to time them to finish casting just after the enemy dies.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Red Mage\010\010Don't just convert because it's off cooldown. Try and recover your MP normally first, you might find that you've still got a few fights left in you before you actually need to use it.\010 Refresh is nice, but if you're struggling for MP then don't cancel your /heal just to get it back up. You and your PLD/BLM/etc can manage with a couple of ticks missed.\010 If your max HP is lower than your max MP, then you won't get full MP back when you convert. Considering using some +HP gear to get the most out of this ability and its long cooldown.\010 A good rule of thumb is to try and keep your max HP/MP as even as possible for best results.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Weapons\010\010width=\"20%\"| Item\010width=\"15%\"| Level\010width=\"30%\"| Stats\010width=\"30%\"| Obtained From\010Pilgrim's Wand\010[Template stat: MP recovered while healing / +2]\010Nunyenunc ((NM))\010Pilgrim's Wand drops from a low level NM in West Sarutabaruta. You will probably want to be at least level 15 to solo it, possibly higher. But it's an amazing piece for any mage that will last at least to level 40 when you get your artifact weapon, possibly even until 51 when you gain access to Dark Staff. Well worth a little time investment to camp the NM to add to your /heal macros.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Head\010\010width=\"20%\"| Item\010width=\"15%\"| Level\010width=\"30%\"| Stats\010width=\"30%\"| Obtained From\010Traveler's Hat\010[Template stat: MND / +1]\010Quest: The Wisdom of Elders or [Template AH: Armor / Head]\010Very easy quest if you start in Bastok. If you didn't start in Bastok, you can also create a second character there and complete the quest, then mail the hat to yourself.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Body\010\010width=\"20%\"| Item\010width=\"15%\"| Level\010width=\"30%\"| Stats\010width=\"30%\"| Obtained From\010Seer's Tunic\010[Template stat: MP / +8]\010[Template stat: MP recovered while healing / +1]\010[Template AH: Armor / Body]\010Another great piece that you'll likely be using in your /heal gear right up until 72 when Errant Houpelande becomes available.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Hands\010\010width=\"20%\"| Item\010width=\"15%\"| Level\010width=\"30%\"| Stats\010width=\"30%\"| Obtained From\010Mycophile Cuffs\010[Template stat: INT / +1]\010[Template stat: MND / +2]\010[Template stat: Enmity / -2]\010Mycophile (NM)\010The NM for this is level 35, so you'll likely want a level 50+ to help you out. Also, the drop rate is 24% without Treasure Hunter, 48% with TH1, so having a THF or /THF really helps.\010No camping required, just need to pop the NM using a Sleepshroom, Woozyshroom, or Danceshroom at Carpenters' Landing (I-11).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Legs\010\010width=\"20%\"| Item\010width=\"15%\"| Level\010width=\"30%\"| Stats\010width=\"30%\"| Obtained From\010Baron's Slops\010[Template stat: MP recovered while healing / +1]\010[Template AH: Armor / Legs]\010This might seem a little expensive (currently 27,000 gil at the time of writing this) for a beginner's guide, but I still use this piece even at level 75 for my /heal macro. There's nothing else in the leg slot that provides more MP while healing until you get to endgame and beat Ultima weapon, so it's worth the investment if you can get the gil together.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Neck\010\010width=\"20%\"| Item\010width=\"15%\"| Level\010width=\"30%\"| Stats\010width=\"30%\"| Obtained From\010Justice Badge\010[Template stat: MND / +3]\010Star Onion Brigade Quest or [Template AH: Armor / Neck]\010A nice little piece that you'll probably use until you get Promise Badge. Easy to get, as the Rarab Tail from the quest comes from low level bees outside of Windurst, or you can buy the tail from auction house for a very small amount of gil.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_9668",["title"]="Sushomi's Clamming Guide",["page_title"]="Sushomi's Clamming Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Sushomi%27s_Clamming_Guide",["revision"]="51625",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template Guide: ]\010Adapted from Sushomi's Clamming Guide.\010If you're an absolute madman and want to read more in-depth on clamming and its potential gil/hr, you can also check out Clamming:_An_Analysis_By_Sushomi",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What is clamming?\010\010Clamming is a non-competitive gathering activity that has been popularised since recent changes to the loot pools and the addition of the HQ Clamming gear as part of the Sunbreeze Event 2023.\010Part of what makes it so popular is a large variety of NPC-able items, which can earn you a decent amount of gil. Also, unlike other gathering activities in Final Fantasy XI, players do not compete over nodes. So no matter how many other players are already clamming, you can still join in and earn gil as if you were the only one there.\010Clamming can be very good for making gil, especially as a lower level player. The isle is relatively safe, and the only monster that will aggro is the occasional bomb, which only spawns during certain weathers. Meaning you could do this on a level 1 character if you wished! Current estimates put clamming at up to 70,000 gil per hour, although it should be noted that despite being primarily luck based, it does take some know-how and skill to achieve these rates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I get started?\010\010Head over to Bibiki Bay docks and grab a manaclipper ticket (or 10), then wait for the boat to arrive to take you to Purgonorgo Isle. Warning: There are 3 different routes that the boat can take, and only one of them will take you to the correct place. Check with the NPC on the end of the pier to find out the boat\226\128\153s destination before boarding.\010Alternatively, if you have the event item from killing Midgardsormr during the Sunbreeze event, you can use this to teleport to the isle once every 20 hours, without needing a ticket or waiting for the boat.\010After a quick boat ride to the isle, head over to the mithra, Toh Zonikki at (Bibiki Bay - Purgonorgo Isle (H-7)) (should be just to your left as you come off the pier). You will need to talk to this npc to purchase clamming kits, and again after you have finished gathering to collect your spoils. But before you do that, read on to find out how the clamming mini-game works, because it\226\128\153s not as straightforward as simply clicking a node repeatedly until you\226\128\153ve had enough.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How does clamming work?\010\010You will purchase a clamming kit from Toh Zonikki for 500 gil, and also be given a bucket in which to store your clams. This bucket has a weight limit of 50pz, and if the items you collect go over that limit the bucket breaks and you lose everything you have gathered so far. As long as you stay under the weight limit, you can cash out at any point by returning to the NPC.\010Once you have cashed out and collected your items, you will need to pay another 500gil to the NPC in order to receive a new clamming kit if you wish to go again.\010But there\226\128\153s more! If your total weight when you cash out is within 5pz of the bucket\226\128\153s limit, then Toh will give you the option to upgrade to a bigger bucket, this time with a capacity of 100pz. In essence, you get to go clamming again with an extra 50 weight limit without having to pay 500gil for another attempt. There is literally no reason to ever decline this offer.\010The process of upgrading your bucket can be repeated. So if you reach 95pz-100pz in your 100pz bucket, you\226\128\153ll have the choice of upgrade to a 150pz bucket. Same again, if you reach 145-150pz, you can upgrade to a 200pz bucket.\010However, beware, as there\226\128\153s a chance while you have a 200pz bucket for a mandragora to jump out of nowhere and break your bucket. This will lose you all the items you have collected, so it can be risky.\010There are two addons that can be used to keep track of your current bucket weight, either of them will work so choose whichever you find most useful.\010 Clammy - https://github.com/m4thmatic/clammy\010 HXIclam - https://github.com/jimmy58663/hxiclam\010Once you have your bucket, head to the nearest clamming point directly east of the NPC and click on it to gather. You will need to wait approximately 10-15 seconds between attempts.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Is there gear to enhance clamming similar to the field gear?\010\010Yes, there is. However, clamming gear is not purchasable from vendors/AH/bazaars. The only way to obtain it is by participating in the Sunbreeze Festival event on Purgonorgo Isle. If you\226\128\153ve missed this, chances are you\226\128\153ll need to wait until next year to obtain it. You can still clam without it, but you may find your luck to be worse than someone with the gear.\010The HQ Swimsuit top reduces the chance of clamming \226\128\156incidents\226\128\157. Remember that pesky mandragora I mentioned that likes jumping into the 200pz buckets? Well, that\226\128\153s the incident it is referring to. This top lowers the chance of that happening from 10% to 5%.\010The HQ Swimsuit trunks/pants improve clamming results. Without these, your loot won\226\128\153t be as good, but even without the pants you may still find it worthwhile. You can check out the differences in drop rates over on the main clamming page: https://horizonffxi.wiki/Category:Clamming#Abundance",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What can I get from clamming?\010\010Below are the 31 items that can be obtained from clamming along with their respective weights, NPC values and assumed drop rates.\010Item\010Weight\010NPC Value\010Drop Rate\010Bibiki Slug\0103pz\01010gil\0109.2%\010Fish Scales\0103pz\01023gil\0104.6%\010Pugil Scales\0103pz\01024gil\0104.4%\010Bibiki Urchin\0106pz\010750gil\0101.1%\010Broken Willow Rod\0106pz\010N/A\0102.1%\010Coral Fragment\0106pz\0101,735gil\0100.9%\010Crab Shell\0106pz\010392gil\0102.1%\010Elm Log\0106pz\010390gil\0100.6%\010Goblin Armor\0106pz\010N/A\0102.3%\010Goblin Mail\0106pz\010N/A\0102.1%\010Goblin Mask\0106pz\010N/A\0102.2%\010Hobgoblin Bread\0106pz\01091gil\0102.3%\010Hobgoblin Pie\0106pz\010153gil\0102.3%\010High-Quality Crab Shell\0106pz\0103,312gil\0100.4%\010High-Quality Pugil Scales\0106pz\010253gil\0101.6%\010Lacquer Tree Log\0106pz\0103,578gil\0100.5%\010Maple Log\0106pz\01015gil\0101.2%\010Nebimonite\0106pz\01053gil\0102.4%\010Oxblood\0106pz\01013,250gil\0100.5%\010Pamtam Kelp\0106pz\0107gil\0106.7%\010Petrified Log\0106pz\0102,193gil\0100.5%\010Seashell\0106pz\01030gil\0102.5%\010Shall Shell\0106pz\010307gil\0104.9%\010Titanictus Shell\0106pz\010357gil\0101.2%\010Turtle Shell\0106pz\0101,190gil\0101.0%\010Uragnite Shell\0106pz\0101,455gil\0100.6%\010Vongola Clam\0106pz\010192gil\0105.5%\010Pebble\0107pz\0101gil\01020.7%\010White Sand\0107pz\010250gil\0102.9%\010Jacknife\01011pz\01058gil\0109.4%\010Tropical Clam\01020pz\0105,100gil\0101.4%\010Drop rates above as of Feb '24, based on 9274 clamming attempts and wearing the HQ swimsuit gear.\010I have not included AH value in the above as this will fluctuate over time, and therefore at the time you\226\128\153re reading this the prices may no longer be relevant. I would recommend researching the cost of these items on AH before starting to clam. This will give you an idea of which items you are best off NPC\226\128\153ing, and which may be better to hold and sell on AH.\010If you do decide to NPC certain items, you will find a mithra stood behind a bush to the west of Toh Zonikki. She sells a couple of fishing rods and is invaluable for getting rid of a lot of the items you\226\128\153ve collected and turning them into profit.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Tips for Beginners\010\010Note that the heaviest item on the list is the Tropical Clam at 20pz.This means that if your current bucket weight is 30pz or below, there is 0% chance that your bucket will break on your next clamming attempt. It might be tempting to cash out the second you find an oxblood, for fear of losing it. But if you\226\128\153re still below that magic 30pz number, then don\226\128\153t be afraid and keep clamming.\010Elm Logs and Coral Fragments are relatively rare, but they do sell very well on AH, often fetching 40-50k per stack at the time of writing this. This is a fair bit more than you would get from NPC\226\128\153ing them, so I would recommend saving these up and selling them on AH.\010You may be tempted to save items like HQ Pugil Scales to sell on AH - currently 10k per stacks, versus ~3k that you would make from NPC. However, consider that these don\226\128\153t sell as quickly as other items and therefore you will often find people undercutting by A LOT. The result is they clog up your auction house slots, cost fees having to relist for the new prices, and sometimes won\226\128\153t sell at all. And if they do sell, chances are you\226\128\153re not going to make as much as you hoped. Consider selling them straight to NPC instead.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I go from being an OK clammer to a GREAT clammer?\010\010Ok, now we\226\128\153re getting into the nitty gritty. While you can attribute a lot of your results to luck with clamming, there are things you can do to maximise your profits - namely by playing it smart when you gamble on whether or not to cash out. Taking that one extra attempt means increasing the value of your bucket, as well as potentially getting to upgrade and saving yourself the 500gil you would have paid for a new attempt.\010So, when do you fold and when do you push your luck and go for that extra clam? To answer that, you need to understand the drop rates and weights of each possible clamming result (see the drop table further up).\010As mentioned above, for example, if you are at 30 or less weight (or <=80 for the 100pz, <=130 for the 150pz, etc), then your bucket CANNOT break on the next attempt. There is no reason not to continue clamming if you still have 20 or more capacity. Unless of course you have a 200pz bucket, in which case every attempt is risking an \226\128\156incident\226\128\157.\010But let\226\128\153s take that a step further. I\226\128\153m now at 31pz (or 19 away from capacity). I had some OK luck in the first few clamming attempts, and I\226\128\153m already at 200g profit if I turn in my bucket. I know that the next attempt could break my bucket if I got a tropical clam (20pz), meaning I would lose not only the 500gil paid for the bucket, but also the 200g profit I\226\128\153ve gathered so far. Should I stop here and cash out? The answer is NO.\010Why no, you ask? The tropical clam is the only item that can break the bucket at this point, and it has a 1.6% drop rate. 98.4% of the time, the bucket will not break and I add at least one additional item to my profits for that bucket. It is more than worth it to continue on at this point.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How do I go from being an OK clammer to a GREAT clammer?\010\010The caveat to all this, of course, is that if you had gotten lucky and hit a big ticket item early on, the chance to save 500gil on having to buy a new bucket becomes less worth it. You\226\128\153ll have to come up with your own limits as to what profit value you think is worth it. I\226\128\153ll share some more info on what my personal limits are below.\010Current Weight\010Items that can break bucket\010Chance of break\010My cash-out threshold\010Notes\01030 or under\010None\0100%\010-\010Keep clamming!\01031-39\010Tropical Clam\0101.6%\0102,000+gil\010It\226\128\153s a small risk, sure, but it does happen from time to time.\01040-43\010Tropical Clam, Jacknife\01011.1%\0101,000+gil\010Still a bit of risk, but most times you\226\128\153ll come out on top!\01044\010Tropical Clam, Jacknife, White Sand, Pebble\01033.3%\010500+gil\010Getting riskier. 500gil gets you a new bucket though, so feel free to cash out if you\226\128\153ve hit that magic number!\01045-50\010Most items!\010Upgrade\010Upgrade\010Upgrade your damn bucket! There is 0 reason to clam past 45 without upgrading!\010Same logic applies to the larger buckets, just add 50 onto the weight values for each upgrade you\226\128\153ve done.\010Once you understand these \226\128\156breakpoints\226\128\157, you\226\128\153re on your way to becoming a master clammer!",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Other questions?\010\010Does bucket size affect drop rate?\010There is currently no evidence to suggest this is the case. Based on my data of ~4500 clamming attempts, there is an insignificant (likely due to luck) difference between the drop rates while using a 50pz and 100pz bucket. I do not have enough data while using the 150pz and 200pz buckets to draw any definitive conclusions from those.\010The ASB/LSB codebases show no difference in drop rate between bucket sizes also. While Horizon does have custom drop rates, based on rates observed so far there is insufficient evidence to suggest that Horizon has deviated from its predecessors in this instance.\010Why do I keep getting tropical clams at 31pz?\010Just lucky, I guess. It can sometimes feel like the chance of getting a tropical clam significantly increases as soon as you hit 31pz, but this isn\226\128\153t actually the case. You\226\128\153re just getting unlucky and remembering all the times you broke your bucket over the times you didn\226\128\153t.\010Can I have a D2?\010Dammit, why did I have to come here on BLM? Gonna change job to something else next time I\226\128\153m here \240\159\153\130",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_14183",["title"]="The Most Basic Fishing Guide Ever",["page_title"]="The Most Basic Fishing Guide Ever",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/The_Most_Basic_Fishing_Guide_Ever",["revision"]="111747",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template Guide: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Introduction\010\010This Guide is made to be simple. There is a lot that goes into fishing, but you don't care about any of that, you just want to know the path. If you want the extras look up one of the other guides or check out the 700 sections in the fishing section page.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What You Need to Fish\010\010You must be level 15 in at least one job.\010Have a fishing rod equipped.\010Have bait equipped: Live bait is lost as you use it. lures can be reused unless your line snaps.\010Water (Fresh / Salt is a thing)",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Tools\010\010Horizogenes: Horizon wiki has built its own timers. This page Has all the boats even the ones missing from pyogenes like: Bibiki Bay and Carpenters' Landing\010[GreatBlue] :Tells you location / skill / bait. have this bookmarked. If its in Japanese hit the big button up top that says English.\010HorizonXI Fishing Discord",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Rank Ups\010\010Like any craft you need to rank up every 10 levels. You can take your \"exam\" 2 levels early like everything else. see below for the rank up fish by rank.\010Level\010Item Requested\010Rank Acquired\0108-10  |  Moat Carp  |  Recruit\01018-20  |  Cheval Salmon  |  Initiate\01028-30  |  Giant Catfish  |  Novice\01038-40  |  Gugru Tuna  |  Apprentice\01048-50  |  Monke-Onke  |  Journeyman\01058-60  |  Bhefhel Marlin  |  Craftsman\01068-70  |  Bladefish  |  Artisan\01078-80  |  Three-eyed Fish  |  Adept\01088-90  |  Gigant Squid  |  Veteran",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="How Fishing works\010\010once you figure out how to equip a rod and bait, and learn what water is its time to fish.\010The mini game is simple, you can get items, get nothing, get a fish, or a monster. Below are the messages. I think you can tell what ones not to real up: Items are generally bad. However some gil locations such as Silver Ring fishing and Mythril Sword's are worth getting. Although that would be at close to the 100 cap anyways.\010 Small fish: \"Something caught the hook!\"\010 Large fish: \"Something caught the hook | !\"\010 Non-fish targets: \"You feel something pulling at your line.\"\010 Monsters: \"Something clamps onto your line ferociously!\"\010 When you hook a fish or item arrows point to the direction you need to click. The Fish/item will fight back and move from side to side. Gold Arrows = more HP drain from the fish. Click the right direction HP drains click the wrong and the HP goes up. \010Oh and if you are far below the skill cap the fish slowly recovers HP, and the reverse is true, if you are far above the fish's skill cap it drains HP.\010(Fishing Pool Refresh Times): 0:00, 4:00, 6:00, 7:00, 17:00, 18:00 and 20:00.\010There is a max number of each fish in a pool they slowly restock at the above times.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Gear And Rod's\010\010Angler's Tunica Set: HQ set, get it. almost the same price, seconds as a level 15 set of gear and gives a slight increase rate of gold arrows.\010Halcyon Rod: Your First rod really can be anything. However get the Halcyon asap.\010Composite Fishing Rod: second rod you should get.\010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod: final fishing rod you need. Hidden +15 to your fishing skill. Opens up a lot of spots. and nothing will break for the most part until skill 100\010Get the Lu Shang as fast as you can if you plan to make Fishing your money maker. its worth the 2mil+ You wont be using it until 50 as there appears to be some type of nerf to it prior to skill 50.\010the 4th Rod is pointless, its a Ponzi scheme. Get it if you want, but I would only do it when you are overflowing with gil. My deep hate for it might also have something to do with me holding the record for most turn-ins on BG for over a year... \010Regardless.... It is the Ebisu Fishing Rod by doing the Indomitable Spirit quest.\010that is all you need to know about gear and rods. the GP items and The Big One have uses but by that time you are not reading this.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Fish\010\010pick a fish and try and be around 10-14(14 is max with gear) levels under it prior to 50 post 50 more opens up if you get a lu shang with it's extra +15 skill to fishing.\010You do not have to take each fish to cap. If you are making good money off one just stick with that one, you are playing an MMO things change, that's why the list has multiple fish that overlap in your skill range that you can pick from. Do a tiny bit of homework and see if you should NPC or pick one that might AH better, or a combo of them for a change of pace.\010check greatblue for more locations\010= recommended\010Fish  |  Cap  |  Location  |  Rod  |  Bait  |  Notes\010Moat Carp \010| 11 \010|\010West Ronfaure*\010So many other locations. check the moat carp page.\010| \010Halcyon Rod*\010 use of cheaper rods is OK until you save gil for the Halcyon Rod\010| \010Insect Paste*\010Little worms are fine until you make a little gil to move to paste\010| Effective for making gil. Insect Paste is the preferred bait for better results.\010Forest Carp \010| 20 \010| \010Yhoator Jungle\010Yuhtunga Jungle \010| \010Halcyon Rod \010| \010Insect Ball \010| Profitable for continuous gil making. Use same setup as Moat Carp.\010Nebimonite's \010| 27 \010| \010Ship bound for Mhaura*\010Ship bound for Selbina*\010| \010Halcyon Rod \010| \010Crayfish Ball are the best*\010Shrimp Lure is a lure tho...\010| They sell decently on the Auction House (AH).\010Elshimo Frog \010| 30 \010| \010Yhoator Jungle(Yhoator Jungle (J-11))*\010Yuhtunga Jungle\010| \010Halcyon Rod\010| \010Fly Lure\010| Check the AH frequently for price fluctuations. Can be highly profitable at times.\010Giant Catfish \010| 31 \010| \010Port San d'Oria\010Many other locations but port sandy is in town, soooooo.... ya\010| \010Composite Fishing Rod\010| \010Minnow\010Sinking Minnow\010| Non-stackable, but sell to NPC if nearby, especially if nation outpost is close.\010Nosteau Herring \010| 39 \010| \010Qufim Island \010| \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Fish\010\010Halcyon Rod\010| \010Sardine Ball\010Shrimp Lure \010| Good for AH sales or if you have Cooking skilled up you can average around 400-500 per fish craft-NPCing Pickled Herring..\010Gugru Tuna \010| 41 \010| \010Ship bound for Mhaura\010Ship bound for Selbina\010| \010Composite Fishing Rod \010| \010Sinking Minnow \010| High AH value, continue up to cap if profitable, otherwise consider an earlier switch.\010Cone Calamary\010| 48 \010| \010Bibiki Bay \010Batallia Downs\010| \010Halcyon Rod \010| \010Sabiki Rig \010| Viable for NPC sales if AH is not profitable, found in Bibiki Bay.\010Bluetail \010| 55 \010|\010 Buburimu Peninsula*\010 Multiple other Locations \010| \010Halcyon Rod \010| \010Minnow \010| Check both NPC and AH for best selling option, available in many locations.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Lu Shang's Fishing Rod Time: Base Fishing Skill 50+\010\010You have your lu, Right? Do you have to have the rod? a fast look at the discord shows many 80+ fishers without a Lu Shang's, there are paths to follow if you really want too. I will try my best to give you that path here as well, but fishing prints gil, you should have saved the gil by now(or soon, start savings more). Also on retail and all private servers I have always aimed to get the Lu asap so I have very little personal experience without it at this point.\010Remember, you can access a fish up to 29 levels under the cap when using the Lu Shang's. You have not only more options with the Lu Shang's but can accesss the options normal rods have a lot sooner\010Fish  |  Cap  |  Location  |  Rod  |  Bait  |  Notes\010Noble Lady \010| 66 \010| \010Ship bound for Mhaura\010Ship bound for Selbina\010| \010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod* \010Composite Fishing Rod \010| \010Sinking Minnow\010| \010Can be caught with Composite Rod, but expect \"too small for this rod\" messages.\010NPC for good return. Check AH.\010Crescent Fish \010| 69 \010| \010 East Sarutabaruta \010| \010 Lu Shang's Fishing Rod* \010 Composite Fishing Rod \010| \010 Fly Lure \010| Can be caught with Composite Rod, but expect \"too small for this rod\" messages.\010Bladefish \010| 71 \010| \010 West Sarutabaruta*\010 East Sarutabaruta\010| \010 Lu Shang's Fishing Rod*\010 Composite Fishing Rod \010| \010 Slice of Bluetail*\010 Meatball\010| \010 Composite Rod will have Line breaks and \"Too Small\" message, not a good fish without the Lu.\010Non-stackable, but NPC well. Good option close to Windurst.\010Tavnazian Goby \010| 75 \010| \010Misareaux Coast \010| \010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010Halcyon Rod \010| \010Minnow*\010| \010 The Halcyon Rod works just fine here.\010If Gobies dry up, switch to Shrimp Lure for Gold Carp, a profitable alternative.\010Silver Sharks \010| 77 \010| \010 Batallia Downs*\010 Sauromugue Champaign\010| \010 Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010 Halcyon Rod \010| \010 Meatball*\010| \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Lu Shang's Fishing Rod Time: Base Fishing Skill 50+\010\010Good for NPC sales, slightly nerfed on the server.\010Three-eyed Fish \010| 81\010| \010Qufim Island\010| \010Composite Fishing Rod\010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod \010| \010Minnow \010| \010Expect \"too small\" messages without Lu Shang's, but rod and line should hold.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="You really should get a Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010\010Anything past this point falls into Lu Shang's or E-Rod territory. You \"can\" keep going if you do not mind dealing with silly amounts of line snaps, rod breaks and a very limited pool of fish.\010Fish  |  Cap  |  Location  |  Rod  |  Bait  |  Notes\010Bastore Bream \010| 86 \010| \010Sea Serpent Grotto\010Port Windurst \010| \010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010Ebisu Fishing Rod \010| Shrimp Lure \010| Ideal for fishing alongside Grimmonites. Both sell well on AH and NPC.\010Grimmonite \010| 90 \010| Sea Serpent Grotto \010| \010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010Ebisu Fishing Rod \010| Shrimp Lure \010| Good choice for both AH and NPC, especially when combined with Bastore Bream.\010Gigant Squid \010| 91 \010| \010Qufim Island\010Beaucedine Glacier \010| \010Composite Fishing Rod\010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod \010| Minnow \010| Useful for skill-ups. AH prices are reasonable, but expect \"too small\" messages with Composite Rod.\010Black Sole \010| 96 \010| Batallia Downs \010| \010Composite Fishing Rod\010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod \010| Sinking Minnow \010| Frequent \"too small\" messages without Lu Shang's. Various locations available.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Final Push:Lu Shang's or Ebisu's World\010\010if somehow you made it with all that \"to small\" fishing without one I just do not know what to say at this point there is nothing but line breaks and Rod snaps for anything 96+ thAll fish below will take you to the cap of 100\010Fish  |  Cap  |  Location  |  Rod  |  Bait  |  Notes\010Armored Pisces \010| 108 \010| \010 Oldton Movalpolos\010| \010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010Ebisu Fishing Rod \010| \010Frog Lure \010| Limited pool but a viable option. Alternative bait of Meatballs with Composite Rod, but expect line snaps.\010Giant Chirai \010| 103 \010| \010 Boyahda Tree \010| \010 Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010 Ebisu Fishing Rod \010| \010 Lufaise Fly*\010 Fly Lure \010| Good for reaching skill cap, fished near Cloister of Storms.\010Titanictus \010| 101 \010| \010Ship bound for Mhaura\010Ship bound for Selbina\010| \010Lu Shang's Fishing Rod\010Ebisu Fishing Rod \010| \010Meatball's \010| Less desirable due to boat downtime, but still an available option.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="More Pending\010\010Final Push to 100, more options\010100 cap fishing ideas? Not sure if I want to put that into \"the most basic guide\"",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_3206",["title"]="Tips & Explanations for New Players, Newbies, and n00bs",["page_title"]="Tips & Explanations for New Players, Newbies, and n00bs",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Tips_%26_Explanations_for_New_Players%2C_Newbies%2C_and_n00bs",["revision"]="52409",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Welcome to FFXI!\010\010Lots of us old timers play it to relive the glory days of the early- to mid-2000s, so it's great to see people who are completely new to FFXI come and play it. There's a pretty steep learning curve, so having a relevant guide can save you an enormous amount of frustration and days of wasted time.\010For reference, also make sure to check out the fantastic Beginner's Macro Guide and the Intermediate Macro Guide",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Keyboard Commands\010\010A couple quick tips for keyboard commands that can help immensely when creating macros (vital for playing the game) and communicating with people:\010/s = say. This will speak publicly to people within a certain radius around you\010/t = tell. This will send a message to someone privately. Try /t madsheep hi there. If I'm on, I'll get a message that says 'hi there.'\010CTRL + R = Respond to a /tell from someone\010/cm = chatmode. If you get a linkshell (guild or chat group) in slot 1, type /cm l (that's a lowercase 'L') to set your chat to default to linkshell1. It needs to be done each time you log in. If you want it set to ls2, do /cm l2. Then /cm p will set your chatmode to party, /cm s will set your chatmode to say, and so on.\010/c = check. For your safety you MUST check every mob you engage. If you don't, you will die because of it, guaranteed. You can check players and see their gear too, but it's considered rude by some people to be randomly examined. They're just being prissy, but it's a thing.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Searching for Players\010\010If you want to see if someone's online, type /sea all NAME. Look for me by typing /sea all madsheep\010/sea = search current area\010/sea all = search all areas\010/sea inv = search current area for people seeking a party\010/sea all inv = search all areas for people seeking a party\010/sea inv 12-22 = search current area for people seeking a party in level range 12-22\010/sea all inv 12-22 = search all areas for people seeking a party in level range 12-22\010If you need to leave a party, it can be very helpful to find a replacement for yourself before you go. Using these commands will help.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Enemy Difficulty\010\010too weak to be worthwhile = you won't get xp or crystals, but will still get drops and they might still be a challenge for certain jobs.\010easy prey = you'll get a small amount of xp. If you can kill them quickly it can actually be a good strategy when low lvl.\010decent challenge = below your lvl, but getting tougher. Be careful.\010even match = this mob is the same lvl as you. You will likely either struggle to beat it or it will kill you. When you're new, stick to EP and DC as much as possible.\010tough = you may luck out and scrape by a confrontation if it's a worm or something, but it'll be tight.\010very tough = you will die.\010incredibly tough = you will die before you can even unlock and start trying to run away.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Drops / Loot\010\010Percentage drops Loot drops from creatures based on a percentage. Some drops are 100% These are often for quests. Some drops are 100% drops so long as you don't already have the item being dropped. These two situations are rare, most items do not have a 100% drops rate.\010Drops on death Loot will drop from creatures when they die, even if they don't award experience points. A level 75 killing a level 1 monster cannot gain experience points for the kill but can gain items. This is often called Farming.\010Chests / Coffers- Chests and Coffers can give Gil, KI (Key Items), Artifact Gear, or treasure items. If a person with an active quest for AF (Artifact Armor) opens a coffer they personally will get the AF, even if in a party. If someone opens a chest/coffer and has a quest to receice a KI (BST AF1 bell for instance is a chest.) only that player will get a KI, even if in party. If a member of a party opens a chest/coffer and it awards gil, the gil will be evenly divided among all members of the party currently in the zone. If the chest/coffer awards an item, it will be added to the party's treasure pool to either be lotted on or randomly passed out.\010Beastmen's Seals will drop at a 20% drop rate (unaffected by Treasure Hunter) once one has dropped there will be a 5 minute (300 second) window of time where another Beastmen's Seal Cannot drop. Therefore it is a very popular method when leveling low level jobs to kill monsters until a seal drops and then go and fight in the next zone. E.g. West Sarutabaruta then zone into East Sarutabaruta or Giddeus and fight monsters there until another seal drops and zone again. In this way you can Farm seals while also earning experience points.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Drops / Loot\010\010Treasure Hunter is a Job Trait, earned by Thief @ level 15. It will increase the percentage chance of drops occuring. Meaning that as a Thief lvl 15+ you will get more items per kill (averaged out over multiple kills) than another job. This trait exists when Thief is set as a Sub Job as well. So many methods of farming items rely on being 30+ on main job with Thief set as jub job. Treasure Hunter II is a Thief level 45 Job Trait and further increases the drop chance percentages. THF can further increase this with a Horizon Only Neck Item Nanaa's Lucky Charm that gives TH+1 (for TH3) and a Dynamis drop for TH+1 to achieve TH4. In-era, TH4 is the highest level of TH attainable.\010Crystals will only drop if a member of the party who did damage (or attacked and missed) has signet.\010Conquest points Conquest points are earned without a record showing how much per kill. These are earned only if the kill awards experience points and you have Signet.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Mechanics & Commands\010\010/ja = Job Ability. A macro using this is something like /ja Berserk <me>. That will use the WAR ability Berserk. Different JAs and other commands require different targets.\010/ra = Ranged Attack. Type /ra <t> to use a ranged attack against the currently selected target. Type /ra <stnpc>. The <stnpc> part is for <select target npc> and gives you a cursor that lets you select a non-pc target.\010/ma = Magic. Use /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc> to cast cure 2 on a nearby player character. If the /ja, /ws, /ma or whatever is more than one word, put it in double quotes and capitalize appropriately. You can also use <stpt> to choose only people within your party. The Intermediate Macro Guide has great info on this.\010/ws = Weapon Skill. When you build 1000+ TP you can do a Weapon Skill. Since gear swaps are essential for maximizing damage, this is what an early WS macro might look like:\010/equipset 1\010/ws \"Fast Blade\" <stnpc> <wait 1>\010/equipset 2\010Equipset is an option that lets you preprogram gear sets for certain things. When auto-attacking and building TP, max out your Accuracy to hit as often as possible. When WSing, use /equipset to switch to gear with stats that benefit your weapon skill (typically STR for most DDs, though THFs benefit from DEX more for most of their weapon skills).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Early Grinding\010\010Play around with macros and commands and test it all out thoroughly. Have fun with it while leveling a bunch of jobs to ~15 or so around starting areas. Windurst is a lovely starting area to level up in because there are some great drops and it's pretty. San d'Oria has mobs which drop fire and dark crystals, which are very lucrative, too. Around Bastok you'll find yourself running around that dry, dusty butthole of a landscape, smacking all sorts of things like bees, worms, and Quadav beastmen. \010Personally, I strongly recommend leveling AT LEAST jobs like WAR, MNK, WHM, BLM, RDM, and THF to the 12-15 range on your own. Start with WAR so that you can build up a variety of skills in things like sword, dagger, club, shield, evasion, parrying. That will help you a little bit as you lvl other jobs that are less adept at melee. Because you're going to be doing a lot of meleeing with mage classes in the early solo lvls and every little bit helps. Having some skill in evasion before leveling a mage is very useful.\010Save ALL of your beastmen's seals. They're invaluable and will give you the opportunity to win fantastic items down the road. Look into BCNMs to learn more.\010Some beastman mobs will drop spells that you can learn, but they're generally dirt cheap anyway. Find the town vendors for spells and see if you can flip them on the Auction House for a profit. Buy weapons, spells, and cheap leather and cloth armour from vendors in town instead of the auction house. You'll save money that you desperately need when starting out. There's a typical progression for early melee classes that goes like this:\010 Level 1 - bronze gear\010 Level 7 - leather gear\010 Level 11 - brass gear\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Early Grinding\010\010As always, compare NPC prices to AH prices to get the most bang for your buck and to determine if you can make some money selling things at a markup.\010You probably won't get a standard party invite until you're 12+, and once you do you may need to make the perilous trek up to the Valkurm Dunes. If you do, see if you can get escorted by someone who can Raise you if you get KOed.\010GET SIGNET FROM GATE GUARDS BEFORE LEAVING TOWN. This will ensure you get crystals from mobs and build up your Conquest Points, which you'll need to get single-use warp scrolls and reraise scrolls. You'll want to constantly have those on hand pretty much forever.\010Reminder: ALWAYS CHECK MOBS. If you don't, you'll engage a Tough mob when you're a half health and it'll push your s#!7 in. \010If you're feeling stuck, try to /shout in town for a newbie linkpearl. That'll connect you with people who can answer more specific questions. Lots of linkshells are happy to take on new players and show them the ropes, so if you don't get any bites after a couple shouts, try again in an hour or two after killing some bees.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Notorious Monsters\010\010There are some really good Notorious Monsters around the starter towns that benefit low lvl jobs. Look up Swamfisk, Maighdean Uaine, Sharp-Eared Ropipi, Nunyenunc, and Spiny Spipi for viable low level NMs with good drops - some of which are 100% drop rates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Basic Leveling Progression Overview\010\010Level 18 - When you hit lvl 18 you can get your subjob, which is huge. \010Level 30 - At 30 you can unlock advanced jobs, which is another big step forward. You can hit rank 3 in your nation pretty easily by the time you're 30. Keep an eye open for people shouting for missions you're on, and ask linkshell mates if they'll help you with yours. \010Chains of Promathia (the first major expansion that non-JP players got, way back in 2004) missions start around lvl 30 with the promyvion areas. If you can find a group doing that when you're able to access them, jump on it because they're a pain. But read up ahead of time so you know what you're getting into. They're often referred to as CoP Mission 1-3, The Mothercrystals, or promy-dem/promy-mea/promy-holla since there are three of them you need to complete.\010Level 50 - Once you hit 50, you need to complete a quest to progress to 55. At 55 that happens again, and so on every 5 levels until you max at 75. That stuff and leveling sometimes keep you plenty busy before you really even need to think about storyline.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Making Money\010\010This is a perpetual challenge, but can be immensely rewarding. When trying to make money, there are a few things that are especially lucrative at lower levels. Look for things like various crystals (primarily fire, dark, lightning, wind), beehive chips (which drop relatively infrequently from bees all over the world), silk thread and yagudo necklaces (around the Windurst starter region), sheepskin (start crafting those with dark crystals into sheep leather right away for more profit), and various goblin drops which sell well on the Auction House. Farm those while getting xp and it'll be a big boon.\010There are a lot of crafters on this server, so the demand for materials is quite high. Finding items which sell quickly but are understocked is a quick way to make steady sales.\010A lot of other things like land crab meat, dhalmel hides, and any other drops which don't sell quickly on the Auction House are fine to sell to NPCs for quick cash. And if you're ambitious, look up some crafting guides on the FFXI Wiki or this wiki to use the crystals and drops you find to level up crafting. You will be really glad you did that later on.\010When selling on the AH, it's sometimes more efficient to sell stacks of things. A stack is either 12 or 99 of something. There are cases where singles are more lucrative, so check the AH to compare and make the most informed decision. When selling, don't undercut dramatically to make a quick sale. The economy is manipulable and you're only hurting yourself and others if you lowball excessively.\010If you can get to Bibiki Bay, Clamming can be very profitable. All you need is ~1000 gil to get there and buy your first bucket!",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Crafting\010\010Crafting Guilds There are many crafting guilds to choose from. Note that you do not need to have talked to any NPCs nor joined a guild to begin crafting.\010Break / Unsuccessful Synths the results of above crafting attempt might be an Copper Ingot (on success) or a \"break\" on a failure. When you fail you 100% lose the crystal powering the synth, but there is a chance to lose ingredients as well from 0->100% of them.\010Successful Synths This is often called a NQ for Normal Quality. For Copper Ingot, there is only NQ synth. Many crafts only have a NQ synth, meaning if you combine 3 grass thread into a square of grass cloth it doesn't matter if a level 1 clothcrafter or a level 100 crafter did the syth. On success you will ONLY and ALWAYS get the NQ item. On the other hand some items have a HQ result.\010HQ Success Success tiers are 11+/31+/51+ above the recipe level, with each tier increasing the likelihood of HQs. Anyone with skill <11 levels above the recipe will have a 1-2% of HQ. 11-30 levels above a recipe is in tier 1, and their % chance to HQ is 8-10%. At 31-50 levels above the recipe, HQ chance is 25%. And then at 51+ levels above it your HQ chance is 50%.\010Guild Points Some items are traded to the guild to earn guild points. These are accumulated and then traded to the NPCs to receive items which can increase your crafting skill by 1 (per item) and various s\010Profit! In general, items that are HQ sell for a higher profit. When leveling or farming, prioritizing mobs and items which are used for guild points or high demand recipes can help to pad the wallet. \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Crafting\010\010Consumables often contain good profits... but typically only if you can HQ them. Which means sometimes skipping them until you reach the 11/31/51 tiers. Some crafting test items (see below) are hard to make, so making them and putting them on the AH can be profitable. Likewise running around to make a guild test item can be a drag or waste of time if if can be found cheaply on the AH or from an NPC.\010Recipe Level Cap Skillups can be attempted within 15 levels of a recipe level. This is not advised as you will break a lot. If you do succeed you have a chance to skill up, BUT ONLY ON SUCCESS.\010Skillups From Breaking Can only happen within 5 levels of a skill cap. You'll break a lot in your crafting career, so it's advised to try to get skill on recipes which are within 5 levels of your current (unmodified) craft level.\010Synth Support There is synth support and advanced synth support. Normal is free and adds one level to your craft. Paid (advanced) support is 3 levels.\010Desynthesis Some recipes are considered 'desynthesis' by the game. These are handled differently. They typically result in a higher rate of failures and ingredients lost. Skilling up from a desynth is only possibly within 3 levels of the recipe cap. \010That's the downside. The upside is Desynth HQ a lot more than a normal craft and that if you get advanced support your desynth success and skillup chances will improve significantly if you're 0-3 levels below the recipe cap.\010Starting Out In many cases there are many starting crafts you can do for profit. Copper ingots (fire xtal + 4x copper ore), bronze ingots (fire xtal + 3x copper ore, 1x tin ore), grass thread (lightning xtal + 2x moko grass)\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Crafting\010\010Crafting Ranks Every 10 levels you will be restricted from gaining more levels until you provide a \"Guild Test Item\". You can turn them in 2 levels below cap e.g. 8,18,28, etc for the 10,20,30, caps. Don't forget to do this!\010Suggestions Windurst citizens can start with CC (Clothcraft) by desynthing Yagudo Necklaces into grass thread and making grass cloth. Bastokan citizens can start with copper ores into ingots in Goldsmithing, or copper ores + tin or for bronze ingots and Smithing. Sandy citizens can start in leatherworking with rabbit hide + grass thread = rabbit mantle, or woodworking with arrowwood log into arrow wood lumber. You do not HAVE to start out with the guilds in your city. You can be a Sandy citizen and start out with cooking. Guilds do provide the normal and advanced crafting support and you will need to visit a guild to progress past level 10 in that craft.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level Sync\010\010Level sync is typically 10 levels and is a feature that will let your party sync down to the same level - not up. So you can have someone who's 22 sync down to 12 to party with you in the dunes. You can sync with larger ranges, but it starts to impact the amount of xp you get and generally isn't worth it.\010You will have better luck getting invited to a party if you're already in the zone, too. Try to run to Valkurm while seeking, and then rest and explore Selbina while you wait. It's a good place to skill up fishing and make a small amount of money, too.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Misc. Notes\010\010Get all teleport crags you can. Touch the crystals whenever you're near the giant white structures to get the key items, which you will keep forever.\010 The Holla crag is closest to San d'Oria\010 Dem is closest to Bastok\010 Mea is closest to Windurst\010 The gate guards that give signet will also offer supply quests. \010 You can only carry supplies for one region at a time, so prioritize well\010 If your nation controls the Zulkheim region (where Valkurm Dunes is), offer to take supplies there. Once you've done that, you can teleport there from lvl 10, which is a HUUUUGE benefit\010 Always watch out for where you can deliver supplies until you have all outpost warp locations available to you\010 It can take a long time to get some since the nation control resets every Saturday night at JP midnight (which means Sunday morning time in North America and afternoon in Europe). \010 Sometimes you can set your homepoint closer to where you're going. If you die, you won't have to redo the whole run. The downside is that it can bite you in the butt when trying to return home until you have more efficient means of transportation.\010I hope this helped!\010by Madsheep",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_4791",["title"]="Treasure Chest and Coffer Guide",["page_title"]="Treasure Chest and Coffer Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Treasure_Chest_and_Coffer_Guide",["revision"]="8476",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010In order to open a chest/coffer you need either the chest/coffer key for that zone, or you need to be a thief with lockpicking tools (see: Picking your Coffers and Chests).",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Timing\010\010After being opened, the chest or coffer will respawn in one of the designated locations after approximately five minutes; there are two exceptions to this, both of which cause the chest/coffer to respawn immediately:\010If a chest is opened and a Magical Pattern is obtained (for use in the quest The Goblin Tailor)\010A Thief attempts to pick the lock of a coffer and fails, producing a Mimic. \010See Treasure Chest and Treasure Coffer for more details.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Treasure Chests\010\010Treasure Chests can be found in the lower level dungeons. They can contain some good items and give decent gil. \010Beadeaux\010[Template :Beadeaux/Treasure Chest: ]\010Castle Oztroja\010[Template :Castle Oztroja/Treasure Chest: ]\010Castle Zvahl Baileys\010[Template :Castle Zvahl Baileys/Treasure Chest: ]\010Castle Zvahl Keep\010[Template :Castle Zvahl Keep/Treasure Chest: ]\010Crawlers' Nest\010[Template :Crawlers' Nest/Treasure Chest: ]\010Dangruf Wadi\010[Template :Dangruf Wadi/Treasure Chest: ]\010Davoi\010[Template :Davoi/Treasure Chest: ]\010Delkfutt's Tower\010[Template :Middle Delkfutt's Tower/Treasure Chest: ]\010The Eldieme Necropolis\010[Template :The Eldieme Necropolis/Treasure Chest: ]\010Fei'Yin\010[Template :Fei'Yin/Treasure Chest: ]\010Fort Ghelsba\010[Template :Fort Ghelsba/Treasure Chest: ]\010Garlaige Citadel\010[Template :Garlaige Citadel/Treasure Chest: ]\010Giddeus\010[Template :Giddeus/Treasure Chest: ]\010Gusgen Mines\010[Template :Gusgen Mines/Treasure Chest: ]\010Horutoto Ruins\010[Template :Inner Horutoto Ruins/Treasure Chest: ]\010King Ranperre's Tomb\010[Template :King Ranperre's Tomb/Treasure Chest: ]\010Labyrinth of Onzozo\010[Template :Labyrinth of Onzozo/Treasure Chest: ]\010Maze of Shakhrami\010[Template :Maze of Shakhrami/Treasure Chest: ]\010Oldton Movalpolos\010[Template :Oldton Movalpolos/Treasure Chest: ]\010Ordelle's Caves\010[Template :Ordelle's Caves/Treasure Chest: ]\010Palborough Mines\010[Template :Palborough Mines/Treasure Chest: ]\010Pso'Xja\010[Template :Pso'Xja/Treasure Chest: ]\010Sacrarium\010[Template :Sacrarium/Treasure Chest: ]\010Sea Serpent Grotto\010[Template :Sea Serpent Grotto/Treasure Chest: ]\010Yughott Grotto\010[Template :Yughott Grotto/Treasure Chest: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Treasure Coffers\010\010Treasure Coffers are generally found in higher level dungeons and contain better items and generally more gil.\010Beadeaux\010[Template :Beadeaux/Treasure Coffer: ]\010The Boyahda Tree\010[Template :The Boyahda Tree/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Castle Oztroja\010[Template :Castle Oztroja/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Castle Zvahl Baileys\010[Template :Castle Zvahl Baileys/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Crawlers' Nest\010[Template :Crawlers' Nest/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Den of Rancor\010[Template :Den of Rancor/Treasure Coffer: ]\010The Eldieme Necropolis\010[Template :The Eldieme Necropolis/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Garlaige Citadel\010[Template :Garlaige Citadel/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Ifrit's Cauldron\010[Template :Ifrit's Cauldron/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Kuftal Tunnel\010[Template :Kuftal Tunnel/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Monastic Cavern\010[Template :Monastic Cavern/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Newton Movalpolos\010[Template :Newton Movalpolos/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Quicksand Caves\010[Template :Quicksand Caves/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Ru'Aun Gardens\010[Template :Ru'Aun Gardens/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Sea Serpent Grotto\010[Template :Sea Serpent Grotto/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Temple of Uggalepih\010[Template :Temple of Uggalepih/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Toraimarai Canal\010[Template :Toraimarai Canal/Treasure Coffer: ]\010Ve'Lugannon Palace\010[Template :Ve'Lugannon Palace/Treasure Coffer: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_56831",["title"]="Ultimate Summoner Macro Guide by RedBaren",["page_title"]="Ultimate Summoner Macro Guide by RedBaren",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Ultimate_Summoner_Macro_Guide_by_RedBaren",["revision"]="119083",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Introduction\010\010[Template guide: ]",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Summoner Macro Guide\010\010A guide to organizing your Summoner macro books for the level 75 cap. One Avatar per page, one button per ability, no fumbling mid-fight.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Overview\010\010Summoner has more abilities than any other job \226\128\148 every Avatar comes with its own Blood Pact set. Rather than cramming everything into one macro set, this guide gives each Avatar its own page with a central \"Hub\" to navigate between them.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Tips & Tricks\010\010Alt line is identical on every page. Assault, Retreat, support spells, Avatar's Favor, and Astral Flow are always in the same spot. Muscle memory saves lives!\010 Blood Pact: Rage and Blood Pact: Ward have separate timers. Use a Rage and a Ward back-to-back. Smart rotation: Rage \226\134\146 Ward \226\134\146 wait \226\134\146 repeat.\010 Release before resummoning. You can't summon a new Avatar while one is out.\010 /wait 7 for summoning. Ensures the page doesn't switch before the spell finishes.\010 Label your macros clearly. Names like \"FlmCrsh\" or \"Hastega\" help you find things at a glance.\010 Don't spam party chat. Only announce what the party needs \226\128\148 Sleepga warnings, Hastega_(Blood_Pact), key debuffs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Timer Check Macro\010\010/echo Rage: <recast=Blood Pact: Rage>\010 /echo Ward: <recast=Blood Pact: Ward>\010 /echo Astral: <recast=Astral Flow>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Macro Basics\010\010Each macro has 6 lines of commands.\010 20 slots per set \226\128\148 10 on Ctrl, 10 on Alt.\010 Switch pages with /macro book # and /macro set #.\010 /pet \"Ability\" <t> \226\128\148 command your Avatar.\010 /ma \"Spell\" <me> \226\128\148 cast a spell yourself.\010 /wait # \226\128\148 delay between lines (decimals like /wait 0.5 work).\010 /p \226\128\148 party chat. /echo \226\128\148 only you see it.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Command Reference\010\010Command  |  What It Does  |  Example\010/ma  |  Cast a spell  |  /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010/ja  |  Use a Job Ability  |  /ja \"Astral Flow\" <me>\010/pet  |  Command your Avatar  |  /pet \"Flaming Crush\" <t>\010/equip  |  Equip a single item  |  /equip main \"Fire Staff\"\010/equipset  |  Equip a saved gear set  |  /equipset 5\010/macro book  |  Switch macro book  |  /macro book 1\010/macro set  |  Switch macro set (page)  |  /macro set 3\010/wait  |  Pause between commands  |  /wait 7\010/p  |  Party chat  |  /p Hastega incoming!\010/echo  |  Private message (only you)  |  /echo Timer ready",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Targeting\010\010Code  |  Targets\010<me>  |  Yourself\010<t>  |  Current target\010<stpc>  |  Select a party member (cursor appears)\010<stnpc>  |  Select a monster/NPC (cursor appears)\010<p0> to <p5>  |  Party member by slot\010Avoid <bt> (battle target) \226\128\148 it causes targeting issues in parties with multiple mobs.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Macro Book Layout\010\010Each book has 10 pages (sets), and each page has 10 Ctrl + 10 Alt slots = 20 macros. All 9 Avatars plus a Hub fit in one book.\010Set  |  Contents\010Set 1  |  Hub \226\128\148 Summon macros + navigation\010Set 2  |  Carbuncle\010Set 3  |  Ifrit\010Set 4  |  Shiva\010Set 5  |  Garuda\010Set 6  |  Titan\010Set 7  |  Ramuh\010Set 8  |  Leviathan\010Set 9  |  Fenrir\010Set 10  |  Diabolos",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Standard Alt Line\010\010The Alt line is identical on every Avatar page for muscle memory. Slots 3-8 are White Magic support spells \226\128\148 swap these to whatever you prefer for your sub job.\010Slot  |  Macro  |  Notes\010Alt+1  |  Assault  |  Pet command\010Alt+2  |  Retreat  |  Pet command\010Alt+3  |  Cure II  |  Personal preference\010Alt+4  |  Cure III  |  Personal preference\010Alt+5  |  Poisona  |  Personal preference\010Alt+6  |  Silena  |  Personal preference\010Alt+7  |  Aquaveil  |  Personal preference\010Alt+8  |  Regen  |  Personal preference\010Alt+9  |  Avatar's Favor  |  Future-proofed \"Hopefully Someday\" \226\128\148 not yet on HorizonXI\010Alt+0  |  Avatar's Astral Flow ability  |  Changes per Avatar",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Hub Page (Set 1)\010\010Your command center. Summon any Avatar and auto-switch to their macro page. Make Ctrl+1 on each page a summon of that Avatar again for just in-case and so you always know whose page you're on.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line \226\128\148 Summon Macros\010\010Ctrl+1: Carbuncle\010 /ma \"Carbuncle\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Light Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 2\010Ctrl+2: Ifrit\010 /ma \"Ifrit\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Fire Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 3\010Ctrl+3: Shiva\010 /ma \"Shiva\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Ice Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 4\010Ctrl+4: Garuda\010 /ma \"Garuda\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Wind Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 5\010Ctrl+5: Titan\010 /ma \"Titan\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Earth Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 6\010Ctrl+6: Ramuh\010 /ma \"Ramuh\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Thunder Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 7\010Ctrl+7: Leviathan\010 /ma \"Leviathan\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Water Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 8\010Ctrl+8: Fenrir\010 /ma \"Fenrir\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Dark Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 9\010Ctrl+9: Diabolos\010 /ma \"Diabolos\" <me>\010 /equip main \"Dark Staff\"\010 /wait 7\010 /macro set 10\010Ctrl+0: Siphon\010A hopeful future proof idea then I added all the elementals to the end of another book for their own line- Pick What you want.\010 /macro book 2\010 /macro set 10",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Alt Line\010\010Alt+1: Assault\010 /pet \"Assault\" <t>\010Alt+2: Retreat\010 /pet \"Retreat\" <me>\010Alt+3: Cure II (personal preference)\010 /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc>\010Alt+4: Cure III (personal preference)\010 /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010Alt+5: Poisona (personal preference)\010 /ma \"Poisona\" <stpc>\010Alt+6: Silena (personal preference)\010 /ma \"Silena\" <stpc>\010Alt+7: Aquaveil (personal preference)\010 /ma \"Aquaveil\" <me>\010Alt+8: Regen (personal preference)\010 /ma \"Regen\" <stpc>\010Alt+9: Avatar's Favor (future-proofed hopefully)\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: MP Remaining Call\010 /p MP:<mpp> <mp> | ! <call14>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line\010\010Ctrl+1: Carbuncle\010 /ma \"Carbuncle\" <me>\010Ctrl+2: Poison Nails\010 /pet \"Poison Nails\" <t>\010Ctrl+3: Meteorite\010 /pet \"Meteorite\" <t>\010Ctrl+6: Healing Ruby\010 /pet \"Healing Ruby\" <stpc> \010Ctrl+7: Healing Ruby II\010 /pet \"Healing Ruby II\" <me>\010Ctrl+8: Shining Ruby\010 /pet \"Shining Ruby\" <me>\010Ctrl+9: Glittering Ruby\010 /pet \"Glittering Ruby\" <me>\010Ctrl+0: Release \226\134\146 Hub\010 /pet \"Release\" <me>\010 /macro set 1",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Alt Line\010\010Alt+1: Assault\010 /pet \"Assault\" <t>\010Alt+2: Retreat\010 /pet \"Retreat\" <me>\010Alt+3: Cure II\010 /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc>\010Alt+4: Cure III\010 /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010Alt+5: Poisona\010 /ma \"Poisona\" <stpc>\010Alt+6: Silena\010 /ma \"Silena\" <stpc>\010Alt+7: Aquaveil\010 /ma \"Aquaveil\" <me>\010Alt+8: Regen\010 /ma \"Regen\" <stpc>\010Alt+9: Avatar's Favor\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: Searing Light (Astral Flow)\010 /pet \"Searing Light\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line\010\010Ctrl+1: Ifrit\010 /ma \"Ifrit\" <me>\010Ctrl+2: Punch\010 /pet \"Punch\" <t>\010Ctrl+3: Fire II\010 /pet \"Fire II\" <t>\010Ctrl+4: Burning Strike\010 /pet \"Burning Strike\" <t>\010Ctrl+5: Double Punch\010 /pet \"Double Punch\" <t>\010Ctrl+6: Fire IV\010 /pet \"Fire IV\" <t>\010Ctrl+7: Flaming Crush\010 /pet \"Flaming Crush\" <t>\010Ctrl+9: Crimson Howl\010 /pet \"Crimson Howl\" <me>\010Ctrl+0: Release \226\134\146 Hub\010 /pet \"Release\" <me>\010 /macro set 1",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Alt Line\010\010Alt+1: Assault\010 /pet \"Assault\" <t>\010Alt+2: Retreat\010 /pet \"Retreat\" <me>\010Alt+3: Cure II\010 /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc>\010Alt+4: Cure III\010 /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010Alt+5: Poisona\010 /ma \"Poisona\" <stpc>\010Alt+6: Silena\010 /ma \"Silena\" <stpc>\010Alt+7: Aquaveil\010 /ma \"Aquaveil\" <me>\010Alt+8: Regen\010 /ma \"Regen\" <stpc>\010Alt+9: Avatar's Favor\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: Inferno (Astral Flow)\010 /pet \"Inferno\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line\010\010Ctrl+1: Shiva\010 /ma \"Shiva\" <me>\010Ctrl+2: Axe Kick\010 /pet \"Axe Kick\" <t>\010Ctrl+3: Blizzard II\010 /pet \"Blizzard II\" <t>\010Ctrl+4: Double Slap\010 /pet \"Double Slap\" <t>\010Ctrl+5: Blizzard IV\010 /pet \"Blizzard IV\" <t>\