's Favor\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: Judgment Bolt (Astral Flow)\010 /pet \"Judgment Bolt\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line\010\010Ctrl+1: Leviathan\010 /ma \"Leviathan\" <me>\010Ctrl+2: Barracuda Dive\010 /pet \"Barracuda Dive\" <t>\010Ctrl+3: Water II\010 /pet \"Water II\" <t>\010Ctrl+4: Tail Whip\010 /pet \"Tail Whip\" <t>\010Ctrl+5: Water IV\010 /pet \"Water IV\" <t>\010Ctrl+6: Spinning Dive\010 /pet \"Spinning Dive\" <t>\010Ctrl+8: Slowga\010 /pet \"Slowga\" <t>\010Ctrl+9: Spring Water\010 /pet \"Spring Water\" <me>\010Ctrl+0: Release \226\134\146 Hub\010 /pet \"Release\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /macro set 1",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Alt Line\010\010Alt+1: Assault\010 /pet \"Assault\" <t>\010Alt+2: Retreat\010 /pet \"Retreat\" <me>\010Alt+3: Cure II\010 /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc>\010Alt+4: Cure III\010 /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010Alt+5: Poisona\010 /ma \"Poisona\" <stpc>\010Alt+6: Silena\010 /ma \"Silena\" <stpc>\010Alt+7: Aquaveil\010 /ma \"Aquaveil\" <me>\010Alt+8: Regen\010 /ma \"Regen\" <stpc>\010Alt+9: Avatar's Favor\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: Tidal Wave (Astral Flow)\010 /pet \"Tidal Wave\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line\010\010Ctrl+1: Fenrir\010 /ma \"Fenrir\" <me>\010Ctrl+2: Moonlit Charge\010 /pet \"Moonlit Charge\" <t>\010Ctrl+3: Crescent Fang\010 /pet \"Crescent Fang\" <t>\010Ctrl+4: Eclipse Bite\010 /pet \"Eclipse Bite\" <t>\010Ctrl+6: Lunar Cry\010 /pet \"Lunar Cry\" <t>\010Ctrl+7: Lunar Roar\010 /pet \"Lunar Roar\" <t>\010Ctrl+8: Ecliptic Howl\010 /pet \"Ecliptic Howl\" <me>\010Ctrl+9: Ecliptic Growl\010 /pet \"Ecliptic Growl\" <me>\010Ctrl+0: Release \226\134\146 Hub\010 /pet \"Release\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /macro set 1",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Alt Line\010\010Alt+1: Assault\010 /pet \"Assault\" <t>\010Alt+2: Retreat\010 /pet \"Retreat\" <me>\010Alt+3: Cure II\010 /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc>\010Alt+4: Cure III\010 /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010Alt+5: Poisona\010 /ma \"Poisona\" <stpc>\010Alt+6: Silena\010 /ma \"Silena\" <stpc>\010Alt+7: Aquaveil\010 /ma \"Aquaveil\" <me>\010Alt+8: Regen\010 /ma \"Regen\" <stpc>\010Alt+9: Avatar's Favor\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: Howling Moon (Astral Flow)\010 /pet \"Howling Moon\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Ctrl Line\010\010Ctrl+1: Diabolos\010 /ma \"Diabolos\" <me>\010Ctrl+2: Camisado\010 /pet \"Camisado\" <t>\010Ctrl+3: Nether Blast\010 /pet \"Nether Blast\" <t>\010Ctrl+5: Somnolence\010 /pet \"Somnolence\" <t>\010Ctrl+6: Nightmare\010 /pet \"Nightmare\" <t>\010 /p Nightmare on <t>! DO NOT WAKE!\010Ctrl+7: Ultimate Terror\010 /pet \"Ultimate Terror\" <t>\010Ctrl+8: Noctoshield\010 /pet \"Noctoshield\" <me>\010Ctrl+9: Dream Shroud\010 /pet \"Dream Shroud\" <me>\010Ctrl+0: Release \226\134\146 Hub\010 /pet \"Release\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /macro set 1",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Alt Line\010\010Alt+1: Assault\010 /pet \"Assault\" <t>\010Alt+2: Retreat\010 /pet \"Retreat\" <me>\010Alt+3: Cure II\010 /ma \"Cure II\" <stpc>\010Alt+4: Cure III\010 /ma \"Cure III\" <stpc>\010Alt+5: Poisona\010 /ma \"Poisona\" <stpc>\010Alt+6: Silena\010 /ma \"Silena\" <stpc>\010Alt+7: Aquaveil\010 /ma \"Aquaveil\" <me>\010Alt+8: Regen\010 /ma \"Regen\" <stpc>\010Alt+9: Avatar's Favor\010 /ja \"Avatar's Favor\" <me>\010Alt+0: Ruinous Omen (Astral Flow)\010 /pet \"Ruinous Omen\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Simplified Grouped Macros\010\010Don't want a whole book per Avatar? These \"smart macros\" stack abilities from different Avatars into one button. Only the ability matching your current summon will fire \226\128\148 the rest are ignored.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Physical Damage\010\010/pet \"Flaming Crush\" <t>\010 /pet \"Predator Claws\" <t>\010 /pet \"Mountain Buster\" <t>\010 /pet \"Spinning Dive\" <t>\010 /pet \"Chaotic Strike\" <t>\010 /pet \"Eclipse Bite\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Tier IV Nuke\010\010/pet \"Fire IV\" <t>\010 /pet \"Blizzard IV\" <t>\010 /pet \"Aero IV\" <t>\010 /pet \"Stone IV\" <t>\010 /pet \"Thunder IV\" <t>\010 /pet \"Water IV\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Tier II Nuke\010\010/pet \"Fire II\" <t>\010 /pet \"Blizzard II\" <t>\010 /pet \"Aero II\" <t>\010 /pet \"Stone II\" <t>\010 /pet \"Thunder II\" <t>\010 /pet \"Water II\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Party Armor Buffs\010\010/pet \"Aerial Armor\" <me>\010 /pet \"Frost Armor\" <me>\010 /pet \"Lightning Armor\" <me>\010 /pet \"Earthen Ward\" <me>\010 /pet \"Shining Ruby\" <me>\010 /pet \"Noctoshield\" <me>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Party Offensive Buffs\010\010/pet \"Hastega\" <me>\010 /pet \"Glittering Ruby\" <me>\010 /pet \"Rolling Thunder\" <me>\010 /pet \"Crimson Howl\" <me>\010 /pet \"Dream Shroud\" <me>\010 /pet \"Ecliptic Growl\" <me>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Healing\010\010/pet \"Healing Ruby\" <stpc>\010 /pet \"Whispering Wind\" <me>\010 /pet \"Spring Water\" <me>\010 /pet \"Healing Ruby II\" <me>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="All-in-One Debuff\010\010/pet \"Sleepga\" <t>\010 /pet \"Slowga\" <t>\010 /pet \"Thunderspark\" <t>\010 /pet \"Ultimate Terror\" <t>\010 /pet \"Lunar Roar\" <t>\010 /pet \"Lunar Cry\" <t>",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Quick Release & Resummon\010\010/pet \"Release\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /ma \"Carbuncle\" <me>\010 /wait 10\010 /pet \"Assault\" <stnpc>\010Swap \"Carbuncle\" for whichever Avatar you need.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Elemental Siphon Macro\010\010Note:This isn't available on HorizonXI but I can dream they will hook us up someday...\010Summon a spirit, siphon MP, release. Match the spirit to the current day/weather for maximum MP return.\010Example (Fire Spirit):\010 /ma \"Fire Spirit\" <me>\010 /wait 4\010 /ja \"Elemental Siphon\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /pet \"Release\" <me>\010Swap \"Fire Spirit\" for whichever element matches the day or weather.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Gear Swap Tips\010\010Equip your matching elemental staff to reduce Avatar perpetuation cost. The Hub summon macros above already include staff swaps.\010Avatar  |  Staff\010Ifrit  |  Fire Staff\010Shiva  |  Ice Staff\010Garuda  |  Wind Staff\010Titan  |  Earth Staff\010Ramuh  |  Thunder Staff\010Leviathan  |  Water Staff\010Fenrir  |  Dark Staff\010Diabolos  |  Dark Staff\010Carbuncle  |  Light Staff",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Blood Pact Gear Swap\010\010Equip bonus gear before the pact fires, then swap back after:\010 /equipset 5\010 /pet \"Flaming Crush\" <t>\010 /wait 4\010 /equipset 6\010Allow at least /wait 0.5 between swaps for lag.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Funny & Goofy Summoning Quotes\010\010Add these to your summon or Blood Pact macros for party entertainment. Mix and match as you please.\010Again in most situations for spammy spells and in general its best to not spam party chat and focus on helpful info on abilities like Sleepga or Hastega but not really needed.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Chat Etiquette\010\010Use /echo for jokes only you see \226\128\148 never annoys anyone.\010 Use /p sparingly \226\128\148 funny the first time, not the 400th.\010 Use /say for flavor \226\128\148 nearby players see it but it won't clutter party chat.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="General Lines\010\010/p I choose you, {Ifrit}! Use Flaming Crush!\010 /p Arise, chicken! Arise! ...wait wrong summon.\010 /p Summoning backup because I sure can't solo this.\010 /p Don't worry, I brought a friend. He bites.\010 /p *rubs lamp furiously* COME FORTH!\010 /p Hold my drink, I'm about to do something magical.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Avatar-Specific\010\010Ifrit:\010 /p {Ifrit} says: \"Did someone order a well-done monster?\"\010 /p Someone call the fire department \226\128\148 oh wait, I AM the fire department.\010 /p It's not arson if a god does it. Probably.\010Shiva:\010 /p {Shiva} says: \"Let it go, let it go~\"\010 /p Ice ice baby. Too cold. Too cold.\010 /p {Shiva}: Colder than your ex's heart.\010Garuda:\010 /p {Garuda} says: \"You're all too slow.\"\010 /p BIRD UP!\010 /p Hastega deployed. You're welcome, melee.\010Titan:\010 /p {Titan} says: \"I'm not fat, I'm big-boned.\"\010 /p THE ROCK has come back to Vana'diel!\010 /p He protecc, he attacc, but mostly he Stoneskin.\010Ramuh:\010 /p {Ramuh} says: \"Back in MY day, we walked uphill BOTH ways.\"\010 /p Grandpa Thunder reporting for duty.\010Leviathan:\010 /p {Leviathan} says: \"Something smells fishy.\"\010 /p Release the Kraken! ...close enough.\010Fenrir:\010 /p {Fenrir} says: \"Awoooo~\"\010 /p Who let the dogs out? I did. With summoning magic.\010 /p Fenrir is a good boy. Yes he is.\010 /p Here, boy! Fetch me their buffs! *Lunar Roar*\010Diabolos:\010 /p {Diabolos} says: \"Sweet dreams~\"\010 /p Nightmare fuel, literally.\010 /p Don't fall asleep. Oh wait, that's the plan.\010Carbuncle:\010 /p {Carbuncle} says: \"I'm cute AND useful!\"\010 /p Don't underestimate the glowing hamster.\010 /p He's not a rabbit. Stop calling him a rabbit.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Community Favorites\010\010From the FFXI forums \226\128\148 actual player macros:\010Leviathan (Kunimatsu):\010 /ma \"Leviathan\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /say OH MASTER OF THE TIDES! BESTOW UPON ME YOUR DIVINE WATERY GOODNESS TO SMITE MY FOES | !\010Carbuncle (Ethics, Cerberus):\010 /ma \"Carbuncle\" <me>\010 /p roses are red, violets are blue,\010 /wait 3\010 /p i traveled through six zones just to be able to summon you.\010Ramuh (Alaron):\010 /ma \"Ramuh\" <me>\010 /wait 1\010 /p Thunder! Thunder! THUNDERCATS, HO!\010Odin (Shadowwind):\010 /ja \"Astral Flow\" <me>\010 /p If you think the Knights Who Say Nee are bad, <t>...\010 /wait 1\010 /ma \"Odin\" <me>\010 /p Then get a load of this guy. HASAN CHOP |  | \010Echo-Only Nicknames (only you see these):\010 /echo [BROOKLYN RAAAAAAAAAAAGE] \226\134\144 Ifrit\010 /echo [QUEEN OF THE COLD SHOULDER] \226\134\144 Shiva\010 /echo [WATERGUN EXTRAORDINAIRE] \226\134\144 Leviathan\010 /echo [DANCES WITH WOLVES] \226\134\144 Fenrir\010 /echo [GET OUT OF MY HEAD, CHARLES] \226\134\144 Diabolos\010 /echo [OH GOD A MUTANT FROM CHERNOBYL] \226\134\144 Carbuncle",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Astral Flow Lines\010\010/p ASTRAL FLOW! Everyone get behind something!\010 /p THIS ISN'T EVEN MY FINAL FORM! ...okay it is.\010 /p ALL MY MP FOR ONE GLORIOUS MOMENT!\010 /p \"The secret ingredient is ALL OF MY MANA.\"",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="See Also\010\010Summoner \226\128\148 Job overview, traits, and equipment\010 Blood Pact \226\128\148 Full ability lists for all Avatars\010 Astral Flow \226\128\148 Two-hour ability details\010 Elemental Siphon \226\128\148 MP recovery mechanics OOE: Someday if we're lucky\010 Macro \226\128\148 General macro guide",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="References\010\010This guide was compiled from the following community resources... So much reading:\010 FFXIclopedia \226\128\148 Summoner Macro Guide\010 FFXIclopedia \226\128\148 Summoner Macro Tricks\010 BG-Wiki \226\128\148 The Heretical Art: Comprehensive Summoner Guide\010 Katella's Corner \226\128\148 FFXI Macro Ideas\010 FFXI Online Forum \226\128\148 Blood Pact Macros\010 Square Enix Forums \226\128\148 Macros for Summoner\010 FFXIAH \226\128\148 Summon Macros\010 FFXIAH \226\128\148 Epic SMN Macros\010 MMOGAH \226\128\148 Complete Guide to FFXI Macros\010 Square Enix Forums \226\128\148 SMN Macros Discussion",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_57707",["title"]="White Mage Guide by Valvette",["page_title"]="White Mage Guide by Valvette",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/White_Mage_Guide_by_Valvette",["revision"]="123858",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Foreword\010\010This guide is meant to contain my lessons learned throughout playing White Mage on horizon. I don't claim superior experience or expertise and you absolutely don't have to listen to me. Instead, treat this as the collection of notes it is. This game is old and therefore quite \"solved\", but I'm aware of this and the intention is to help those few who are still trying new things. Besides being a collection of my own notes.\010Valvette, August 29th, 2026",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="About the job\010\010You've come here expecting to learn about healing. And learn about healing you shall. As a White Mage, you'll heal. And heal. And heal some more. And you'll like it. But, the developers did give the White Mage more tools than just restoring HP. Any White Mage worth their salt will cast party-encompassing protective spells, enfeeble, deal substantial damage with divine spells and even, rarely but still notably, provide MP. The weapon of choice for whacking things is the Club and that, coupled with a shield, provides a White Mage with a solid if slow way of holding their own in solo. \010Other perks with this job include: free Sneak and Invisible which are tremendous for questing and just clearing content in general and Teleport-spells which can make you quite a bit of cash once acquired. \010And besides, the cloth armor in this game looks awesome. Let's get into it!",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Magic Skills\010\010<div class=\"float-container\">\010Magic Skill\010Rank\010Healing\010A+\010Divine\010A-\010Enhancing\010C+\010Enfeebling\010C\010We are a mage job, and the table to the right shows us our rank in the different Magic Skills we have access to. See Skill caps for a per level breakdown.\010We can see that we are as good as it's possible to be at healing with an excellent A+. Juicy. We're also the best at Divine Magic among all jobs with our A-, just not quite as good as we are at healing. At enhancing we are second to the Red Mage with a somewhat poor C+ and at enfeebling we are tied with the Dark Knight at last place with a measly C. And this is important because enfeebling is arguably half your job in a party. The other half being healing.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010This is your bread and butter, as expected. You cast spells as most of your game actions when playing after level 10. Here they are. \010+ Healing Magic\010Level\010Name\010Cost\010Duration\010Recast time\010Comment\010They're all strong and you'll use these a lot. For the heal/MP efficiency I will be assuming high cap and no +potency. Cure cast on undead targets will deal light damage, too.\0101\010Cure\0108MP\010Instant\0105s\010What can I say? It's Cure. It's solid even until later levels. Toss it, skill your healing and restore some HP. Caps at 30HP which for 8MP gives 3.75 HP per MP you spend. Cure spells also wake up sleeping allies.\01011\010Cure II\01024MP\010Instant\0105.5s\010With this, you have access to two Cure spells and can chain them a bit better. Cure II does draw a lot of hate in comparison, so should be reserved for nothing-else-works situations. Caps at 90HP which for 24MP gives 3.75 HP per MP you spend, again.\01021\010Cure III\01056MP\010Instant\0106s\010By the time you get this, you rely on Cure II a lot, and this comes as an expensive panic heal that doesn't draw that much more hate than Cure II does. Very welcome addition to the healing arsenal. Caps at 190HP which for 56MP gives ~3.39 HP per MP you spend, lowest of all Cure spells.\01041\010Cure IV\01088MP\010Instant\0108s\010Similar to Cure II, this draws more hate per HP restored than Cure V, but you have so many alternatives at this point it mostly increases your strongest heal power. Caps at 390HP which for 88MP gives ~4.43 HP per MP you spend, highest yet.\01061\010Cure V\010135MP\010Instant\01010s\010The thing that makes you heal over 50% of even the toughest Galka in one go. You do pay for it in MP, but it's quite cool. Caps at 690HP which for 135MP gives ~5.11 HP per MP you spend, highest yet.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010Situational and very often not MP or hate efficient. The caps for Curaga are Cure #+1, so Curaga has the same as Cure II, Curaga II has the same as Cure III, and so on.\01016\010Curaga\01060MP\010Instant\01010s\010With this, you can heal everyone in the party, gain tons of hate if a monster is alive, and pay precious MP for it. The ideal situation is probably out of combat to top up everyone if they're all healing. At Cap, to reach the MP efficiency of Cure and Cure II, you need to heal for 225HP total. The theoretical cap is 90x6=540HP.\01031\010Curaga II\010120MP\010Instant\01010.25s\010More of the same. To reach the efficiency of Cure III, you have to heal for 407HP. The theoretical cap is 190x6=1140HP.\01051\010Curaga III\010180MP\010Instant\01010.5s\010To reach the efficiency of Cure IV, you have to heal for 798HP. The theoretical cap is 390x6=2340HP.\01071\010Curaga IV\010260MP\010Instant\01010.75s\010To reach the efficiency of Cure V, you have to heal for 1329HP. The theoretical cap is 690x6=4140HP.\010Most MP efficient healing in the game is found in this category. You'll love these.\01021\010Regen\01015MP\01075s\01012s\010Really good. Heals for 125HP over 75s, 125HP/15MP=8.334, more efficiency than any cure spell.\01044\010Regen II\01036MP\01060s\01016s\010Now you can have two Regens at once - and you should. 240HP over 60s. 240HP/36MP=9.444, even more efficiency. If there's no need for bigger cures, use this.\01066\010Regen III\01064MP\01060s\01020s\010Three Regens at once! 400HP over 60s. 400HP/64MP=6,25. We're back to lower efficiency, but what can you do? It's good.\010Invaluable when the situation calls for it. I never leave home without Reraise on.\01025\010Raise\010150MP\010Instant\01060s\010Your first raise. Revives and restores 50% of Experience lost on dying.\01025\010Reraise\010150MP\0101hr\01060s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010When you die, you can raise yourself. Can save an entire party from having to travel to camp, because you can just raise yourself, then raise everybody.\01056\010Raise II\010150MP\010Instant\01060s\010Recovers 75% of Experience lost.\01056\010Reraise II\010150MP\0101hr\01060s\010Same effect as Raise II, but as a Reraise.\01070\010Raise III\010150MP\010Instant\01060s\010Recovers 90% of Experience lost.\01070\010Reraise III\010150MP\0101hr\01060s\010Same effect as Raise III, but as a Reraise.\010+ Enfeebling Magic\010Level\010Name\010Cost\010Duration\010Recast time\010Comment\010Perhaps the spell you'll cast most often outside Cure-type spells.\0103\010Dia\0107MP\01060s\0105s\010This has the amazing ability of lowering the target's defense by ~5%. Yes. A percentage. So it scales into lategame, basically. It's really, really good. Besides ticking 1 damage per 3s.\01036\010Dia II\01030MP\010120s\0106s\010The tick is now 2 damage/s and defense reduction ~10%\01018\010Diaga\01012MP\01060s\0106s\010Apply the first level of Dia in an area. I've never once cast this but there might be a use case for it?\010Arguably better than Cure-type spells, in the right situation.\0104\010Paralyze\0106MP\0102m\01010s\010Prevents the target from attacking 25% of the time. This is perhaps the best enfeeble of all. It's so good, so, so, so good. Get it. Cast it. \01013\010Slow\01015MP\0103m\01020s\010A strong secondary to Paralyze. Instead of preventing attacks it slows them down. Attempt it when you can afford the MP.\01015\010Silence\01016MP\0102m\01010s\010Makes the target unable to cast spells. In Qufim against worms, in Quicksand Caves against Antica, and other places. It's really good when it's needed.\010+ Divine Magic\010Level\010Name\010Cost\010Duration\010Recast time\010Comment\010Your tools to deal direct damage, plus an enfeeble. You were never meant to be a damage dealer, so don't expect massive options here.\0105\010Banish\01015MP\010Instant\01020s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010It's big, it's expensive, it has a long cooldown, and will get resisted so it doesn't deal a lot of damage at all at the start. I'm sure it's powerful when Divine Magic skill is capped.\01030\010Banish II\01057MP\010Instant\01030s\010More of the same, but now you have two spells to chain together.\01065\010Banish III\01096MP\010Instant\01045s\010And the third one in the same category. I think you need to get the Banishgas in here to chain properly, given the long cooldowns.\01015\010Banishga\01041MP\010Instant\01015s\010Banish but in an AoE. This one has great potential, I'm sure, and is your real second Divine Magic spell that deals damage. Finnicky to get right, though.\01040\010Banishga II\010120MP\010Instant\01030s\010Similarly finnicky, but more power, which is nice, right?\01045\010Flash\01025MP\01012s\01045s\010This is a stereotypical Paladin spell - but it has uses for you too. Tank casting Utsusemi? Help out with this. You will take a little more hate, but it dissipates very quickly.\01050\010Holy\010100MP\010Instant\01060s\010Big honkin' divine nuke with one full minute cooldown. Get it in between some Banish to blast things.\010+ Enhancing Magic\010Level\010Name\010Cost\010Duration\010Recast time\010Comment\010The only single target enhancing spell you can use on others outside protective magic, and everybody wants it.\01040\010Haste\01040MP\0103m\01018s\010Reduce attack delay and magic recast time by 15%. All, all, all melee DDs want this. Ranged attack users don't benifit. The reduction to magic recast time benefits you, too.\010Erase is also here. All of these are very good when you need them.\0106\010Poisona\0108MP\010Instant\0105s\010Removes poison on an ally. Very useful from Dunes onward, and can be considered a cheap, quick casting Cure, as poison can sometimes tick for 5 or more over a minute's time.\0109\010Paralyna\01012MP\010Instant\0105s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010Removes Paralyze on an ally. Paralyze is one of the strongest debuffs in the game, so removing it is a naturally powerful tool.\01014\010Blindna\01016MP\010Instant\01010s\010Removes Blind on an ally. Another strong debuff that players hate to have on them. Strong tool.\01019\010Silena\01024MP\010Instant\0105s\010Removes Silence from an ally. Casters are reduced to nothing, and /NIN users can't cast Utsusemi. Strong tool.\01029\010Cursna\01030MP\010Instant\01010s\010Removes Curse from an ally. Less use because Curse is a sparingly used debuff. But because Curse is so powerful, this has its place.\01032\010Erase\01018MP\010Instant\01015s\010The most versatile of debuff-removing spells. Removes one detrimental effect on an ally or self, which can be, for example, Slow or Attack Down or anything else that might happen. The potential is very wide, and checking for Erase targets, evaluating whether it's worth a cast are two delightful pastimes while partying. \01034\010Viruna\01048MP\010Instant\0105s\010Removes Virus effects like Disease and Plague from an ally. Less use because they don't happen very often, but when they do people will be glad you have it.\01039\010Stona\01040MP\010Instant\0105s\010Removes Petrification from an ally, which typically is dodgable anyway. When it's not this is really useful.\010...and you only cast them on yourself. They're pretty good.\01010\010Aquaveil\01012MP\01010m\01010s\010Reduces the chance that your casting is interrupted by getting hit, by about 20%. If this is the only such effect, you'll go from 0/5 to 1/5 spells go through despite being hit. Could be worth it if you're under pressure, but my gut says that you shouldn't be. Good in soloing situations besides that.\01019\010Blink\01020MP\0105m\01010s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010Gives you two shadows that absorbs 100% of the incoming damage of a direct hit. Activation chance is 70% per shadow. Saves you from the occasional stray hit with decent guarantee. Very good use of 20MP.\01028\010Stoneskin\01029MP\0105m\01030s\010Absorbs damage taken, a certain value depending on MND and Enhancing magic skill. The calculation is complicated but Enhancing Magic gives 1HP per 3 skill levels and MND 2 or 3 points depending which formula is correct - something we don't know completely.\010You cast these in every party you play.\0107\010Protect\0109MP\01030m\0105s\010The first protection spell available. So, how much does this protect? It adds 10 DEF is the answer. How much damage reduction does that lead to? Tough question. But imagine gear pieces adding 10 more DEF and this does that. To you or target ally.\0107\010Protectra\0109MP\01030m\01015s\010The same except party-wide, saving time and MP.\01027\010Protect II\01028MP\01030m\0105.25s\010Adds 25 DEF. Good improvement.\01027\010Protectra II\01028MP\01030m\01016s\010You'll nearly always prefer the party-wide version.\01047\010Protect III\01046MP\01030m\0105.5s\010Adds 40 DEF. Good improvement.\01047\010Protectra III\01046MP\01030m\01017s\010More of the same stuff.\01063\010Protect IV\01065MP\01030m\0105.75s\010Adds 55 DEF.\01063\010Protectra IV\01065MP\01030m\01018s\010Thank you for reading this.\01075\010Protectra V\01084MP\01030m\01019s\010The last one exists only as -ra form, adding 65 glorious DEF to the party. Very cool.\010You cast these in every party you play, too.\01017\010Shell\01018MP\01030m\0105s\010Now we protect against magic attacks, and this translates to pure damage reduction: ~9.8%. There are plenty of attacks with magic in them, so this helps out nicely.\01017\010Shellra\01018MP\01030m\01015s\010The party-wide version. You will cast this a lot.\01037\010Shell II\01037MP\01030m\0105.25s\010Damage reduction is ~14.8%. Quite a bit better.\01037\010Shellra II\01037MP\01030m\01016s\010Party-wide yet again.\01057\010Shell III\01056MP\01030m\0105.5s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010Damage reduction is ~19.9%. We see that the improvement is about 5% per Shell level.\01057\010Shellra III\01056MP\01030m\01017s\010Party-wide once more.\01068\010Shell IV\01075MP\01030m\0105.75s\010Damage reduction is ~23.4%. The higher we go, the more each % is worth, so it's tapering off a bit.\01068\010Shellra IV\01075MP\01030m\01018s\010Party-wide once more.\01075\010Shellra V\01093MP\01030m\01019s\010The last one is party-wide only, and provides the glorious 25% magic damage reduction. Very, very good.\010Besides Deodorize, these are absolutely amazing.\01015\010Deodorize\01010MP\010Varies\0108s\010When you get far enough away from a monster, run through water or if it rains, this spell might end the pursuit. How often has that happened to you? Rare use case. I have never cast it.\01020\010Sneak\01015MP\010Varies\01010s\010This, on the other hand, makes you not get detected by enemies that rely on sound. Very, very useful. \01025\010Invisible\01015MP\010Varies\01010s\010This makes you not get detected by enemies that rely on sight. Very, very useful as well.\010Useful. Experience is the best guide, here. When are there big elemental damage hits coming? Elementga spells coming in and you couldn't silence the caster? Use these for those times. The base duration is written here. Enhancing skill increases this by 0.8s per point, giving a maximum of 4 minutes at max skill.\0105\010Barstonra\01012MP\0102m30s\01010s\010I think the Tremors weapon skill is AoE, so maybe against worms? Stonega is the spell to defend against with this.\0109\010Barwatera\01012MP\0102m30s\01010s\010I'm thinking pugils. Maybe sahagin? Or Waterga.\01013\010Baraera\01012MP\0102m30s\01010s\010Nothing comes to mind. Aeroga.\01017\010Barfira\01012MP\0102m30s\01010s\010Lizard fireball. Goblin bomb toss. Probably bomb stuff as well. Firaga.\01021\010Barblizzara\01012MP\0102m30s\01010s\010Gigas Ice roar is ice element damage in a cone. Blizzaga.\01025\010Barthundra\01012MP\0102m30s\01010s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010Gigas Thunder roar is thunder element damage in a cone. Thundaga.\010Barely useful. They don't protect very well and there's usually a corresponding -na spell (explained in the table below this one) to just remove the effect.\0107\010Barsleepra\01014MP\0102m30s\01010s\010I've used this against Sheep Song and Dream Flower, but honestly the effect is limited. Maybe one or two more partial resists? Cure to wake people up.\01010\010Barpoisonra\01018MP\0102m30s\01010s\010I've tried against Lizard Poison Breath and Venom. Trouble is, they get poisoned anyway, so Poisona is better.\01012\010Barparalyzra\01022MP\0102m30s\01010s\010You could do this if fighting against something with Ice Spikes and no dispel, and that one Dynamis boss fight. Straight Paralyze casts we counter with Paralyna.\01018\010Barblindra\01026MP\0102m30s\01010s\010Black Cloud. And we use Blindna on those affected. \01023\010Barsilencera\01030MP\0102m30s\01010s\010If you get silenced you can't cast spells. If you know it's a danger, keep this up. Echo Drops on hand allows you to break through and cast Silena on others.\01039\010Barvira\01050MP\0102m30s\01010s\010Fun, but I've never seen this in use and never seen it work. Protection against something very rare.\01043\010Barpetra\01040MP\0102m30s\01010s\010More situational, unlikely to ever be used. Baleful Gaze can be countered by looking away, which is good because Stona comes in at level 39 for us.\010Worth their weight in gold, literally.\01036\010Teleport-Dem\01075MP\010Instant\01010s\010Teleports you and your party to the Crag of Dem.\01036\010Teleport-Holla\01075MP\010Instant\01010s\010Teleports you and your party to the Crag of Holla.\01036\010Teleport-Mea\01075MP\010Instant\01010s\010Teleports you and your party to the Crag of Mea.\01038\010Teleport-Altep\01075MP\010Instant\01010s\010Teleports you and your party to the telepoint crystal in Altepa Desert.\01038\010Teleport-Yhoat\010100MP\010Instant\01010s\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spells\010\010Teleports you and your party to the telepoint crystal in Yhoator Jungle\01042\010Teleport-Vahzl\010100MP\010Instant\01010s\010Teleports you and your party to the telepoint crystal in Xarcabard.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Benediction\010\010This saves everyone and if you're in combat, likely kills you by drawing more enmity than anyone could ever get back from you. You heal everyone within range 14 of you for, we think, (max HP of target) * (level of WHM) / (level of target). Assume you're level 15 and you're healing some Galka with 225 HP also at 15, we get that your and the Galka's level cancel out, and you simply heal them for their max HP.\010Using this in combat is like saying \"I'm now permanently the tank and the enemy has to die before I do.\" Spoilers: WHM don't tank very well.\010Outside of combat, this just heals everyone instantly for the cost of 0 MP. Quite cool.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Divine Seal\010\010A quite versatile little job ability. It has two phases: Pre-Divine Veil and Post-Divine Veil.\010Pre-Divine Veil: \010 Doubles the potency of your next Cure spell with a 15-minute cooldown. A panic button or MP-saver button. You choose.\010Post-Divine Veil is the same as regular, with the following additions: \010 Your -na spells become party-wide\010 Your Regen spells have doubled potency AND double duration, giving a 4x increase to total HP restored.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Devotion\010\010Another relatively strong job ability. This cuts your current by HP 25% and gives that much MP to the target at a cooldown of 10m. Use it at MaxHP for best effect.\010So, a MP user in your party can become very happy, once every ten minutes. The two likely targets I've found are SMN and PLD. Perhaps BLU ought to be mentioned here, too. RDMs are rarely used alongside a WHM, BLMs are rarely used in parties overall. In a WHM Cure burn party you can all Devotion each other, which is nice.\010Communicate with your target before using it, as everyone, especially PLDs, will pay special attention to managing their MP and you don't want to get in their way.\010Don't worry about the HP cut. Maximize it with HP gear if you want. You can Cure that up or just wait for your Auto-Regen to catch up. Nothing of value is lost here.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Traits\010\010We are okay with what we get here, but nothing in here stands out as amazingly powerful, besides maybe Divine Veil.\010Level\010Trait\010Comment\010The ones that do good things.\01025 \010Auto Regen\010You now heal yourself at 1HP/3s. We would kill to make this 1MP/3s, but we are White Mages. We don't kill. This is mostly a convenience thing rather than a power thing.\01050 \010Divine Veil\010Improves Divine Seal as explained above.\010The ones that do weak things but improve as you level.\01015\010Clear Mind\010Gives MP Recovered While Healing. 3, to be exact. Very nice and you won't notice it. \01035\010Clear Mind II\010Replaces 3 with 6.\01045\010Clear Mind III\010Replaces 6 with 10. I think around here your total MP will have outscaled this, but it's still nice.\01055\010Clear Mind IV\010Replaces 10 with 13.\01065\010Clear Mind V\010Replaces 13 with 17.\01075\010Clear Mind VI\010The final version, 21 more MPH. Not bad at all.\01010\010Magic Defense Bonus\010This gives us more defense against Magic Damage, which is a good thing. This gives 10 MDB, that stacks with Shell.\01030\010Magic Defense Bonus II\010Second version of this trait, and now gives 12 MDB. Not 12 more, replaces 10 with 12. \01050\010Magic Defense Bonus III\010Replaces 12 with 14. I'm thinking the +10 from the first trait is strong, and each subsequent tier is very, very lackluster.\01070\010Magic Defense Bonus IV\010Replaces 14 with 16.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Abridged version\010\010Will be added once the main guide has enough meat to just copy over. Highlight everything by level so a person can know what level they should look for what thing.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Race\010\010This is the least impactful of all choices pertaining to your White Mage. Can you play it as Galka and Elvaan, the two races with worst MP pool? Yes, is the answer. What about Hume and Mithra? The ones that get slightly more? Yes. Will a Tarutaru outshine you in the MP pool department before factoring in gear? Also yes, but not by much. The end result is: play whichever race you want. \010If you want to optimize everything, get a Tarutaru, but don't assume you can slack off on any other aspect of your character just because of your race: It doesn't matter that much. Let's break it down anyway!\010For what it's worth, Race Specific Equipment will make up for the differences between the races. Find them here:\010Hume RSE - Legs piece stands out as a +32MP piece.\010Galka RSE - Every piece gives substantial MP with Body and Hands giving +32MP each. Crazy.\010Elvaan RSE - Body and Hands pieces give +24MP, with Head +20MP.\010Mithra RSE - Legs piece stands out as a +32MP piece, like for Hume.\010Tarutaru RSE - Pfft. You thought you'd get more MP down here. No. You get +MND on the Legs piece and be happy.\010A personal note: I can't stress enough how much I like my +32MP from Savage loincloth.\010Stat breakdown of each race just starting out as White Mages, level 1.\010</div>\010+ Hume\010colspan=\"7\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | HP: 27 MP: 22 \010STR  |  DEX  |  VIT  |  AGI  |  INT  |  MND  |  CHR\0106  |  5  |  6  |  6  |  6  |  8  |  7\010+ Mithra\010colspan=\"7\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | HP: 27 MP: 22 \010STR  |  DEX  |  VIT  |  AGI  |  INT  |  MND  |  CHR\0106  |  7  |  6  |  7  |  6  |  8  |  6\010+ Elvaan\010colspan=\"7\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | HP: 29 MP: 20 \010STR  |  DEX  |  VIT  |  AGI  |  INT  |  MND  |  CHR\0107  |  5  |  7  |  5  |  5  |  9  |  7\010+ Galka\010colspan=\"7\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | HP: 32 MP: 16 \010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Race\010\010STR  |  DEX  |  VIT  |  AGI  |  INT  |  MND  |  CHR\0107  |  5  |  8  |  6  |  6  |  8  |  6\010+ Tarutaru\010colspan=\"7\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | HP: 23 MP: 28 \010STR  |  DEX  |  VIT  |  AGI  |  INT  |  MND  |  CHR\0105  |  5  |  6  |  7  |  8  |  8  |  7\010Very interesting stuff. What this tells us is that in the MP-race, the pecking order is as follows: \010Tarutaru >> Hume = Mithra > Elvaan >> Galka \010Poor galka.\010Another important race is, and you'll see why later, the MND race. Which gives us:\010Elvaan > Tarutaru = Mithra = Huma = Galka\010Huh. Interesting. HP and MND are the two most important numbers, with the MND race being very even and MP cancelled out by gear acquired in the late 20s / early 30s. Okay. What about the other stats? Well, STR, DEX and CHR count for basically nothing. A quick secondary analysis of the other stats gives us:\010HP - Useful. You will take hate and suffer damage. \010Galka > Elvaan > Hume = Mithra >> Tarutaru.\010Tarutaru, get some RSE to fight this.\010INT - Useful. There are enfeebles (looking at you, Blind) that use INT in their calculation. Tarutaru wins here.\010AGI - Arguably useful. A monster might miss you when they otherwise should've hit. If you're evasion-capped. If you accidentally took hate. Might, if, if. Slim chances. Mithra along with Tarutaru win here.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="MP\010\010Basic. Simple. Get more of this. Many of your gear pieces will lead to more MP.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="MND\010\010Good for enfeebles. There's mathematics involved.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="INT\010\010Good for one enfeeble in particular.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Cure Potency\010\010Just the best stat available for a White Mage. It has the largest impact on your number one role - which is restoring HP.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Auto-Refresh\010\010Second best stat, and is more readily available than Cure Potency. The engine of Good Stuff.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="MP Recovered While Healing\010\010The smaller cousin of Auto-Refresh. It's good, it helps out, it has impact. You still have to rest to get it though.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="-Enmity\010\010After around level 50, this becomes staggeringly important. -Enmity works as % reduction to the enmity you generate. -50 Enmity total would equal generating 50% enmity for the same actions. It's really, really good.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Main line\010\010We are talking mostly about the other Mage jobs here. Why? Because the MP of the subjob adds to yours. MP being one of the most sought after stats for you as a White Mage as explained above.\010+ Black Mage\010Pros\010Large MP pool.\010Conserve MP. Occasionally saves you MP when casting.\010Sleep. One of the best disables. Elemental seal nearly guarantees a hit.\010Clear mind. More MP when resting.\010Warp, Escape and Tractor. Yum.\010Good INT for that one enfeeble. And it gives that enfeeble too.\010Dark magic Aspir. Requires a bit of skilling, but it's one more tool in the toolbox.\010Elemental enfeebles, among other enfeebles like Bind, If you decide to use them.\010Cons\010No obvious ones. No additional HP I guess? What, you want everything?\010+ Red Mage\010Pros\010Large MP pool.\010Fast Cast. You'll occasionally toss out an otherwise long casting Regen very quickly. Yay.\010Dispel. Situational, but invaluable when required.\010Sleep. But later than Black Mage. No Elemental seal.\010Clear mind. But later and worse than Black Mage. Oh wait, both Black Mage and Red Mage are worse than us at this. Nevermind.\010Good INT for that one enfeeble. And it gives that enfeeble too. Gravity as a unique enfeeble. I'm sure it has more than niche use. All other enfeebles /RDM gets later than /BLM would.\010Dark magic Aspir. But worse than Black Mage.\010I guess Phalanx for more defense? A bit late, but... sure?\010Cons\010Isn't black Mage. There are a lot of things Black Mage just does better. I wish I could type Enfeebling Magic as a pro, but it doesn't help since Red Mage can't buff White Mage past its limit.\010+ Summoner\010Pros\010Large MP pool. The largest, in fact. Max MP Boost is the formal name for this.\010Auto-refresh at level 50. Yum. Yummy yum. This is enough to put Summoner in the discussion against Red Mage. That's how good this is.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Main line\010\010Blood pacts with summoned Avatars. They'll take a long time and cost quite a bit of MP. But, might be your style, you know?\010Cons\010Isn't Black Mage. No sleep. No Warp. No Conserve MP. No Dispel. No plethora of enfeebles. You become the whitest of White Mages.\010My conclusion is that Black Mage rules. It just does. Red Mage has a place, but you're choosing Dispel over a lot of good stuff. And Summoner arguably has a spot after level 50 with its Auto-refresh. I personally value Summoner slightly over Red Mage, but I've never been in the situation where Dispel was sought after.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Off-beat\010\010Situational things that you can do for more solo-oriented things and a small analysis of what it gives you. I wouldn't seek a party with any of these as my subjob.\010+ Ninja\010Pros\010Survivability like no other. Utsusemi: Ichi and Ni are just... Unbelievable. Your solo-potential just went up by a factor of a few thousand. It is useful for Black Mage-esque soloing.\010Dual wield. Equip two wands. No grip or staff, but there has to be some situation where this is useful. There always is.\010Second highest evasion in the game second to Thief.\010Cons\010Isn't any of the mage jobs. So no extra MP. Or extra spells. Nothing substantial to cover up for it.\010+ Blue Mage\010Pros\010Large MP pool. It's a mage job, yay!\010Resist Sleep for... resisting some sleeps? Properly situational. You can play 1-75 and never encounter a situation where this would've had impact.\010Cons\010Doesn't do anything that overlaps with what you do as a White Mage. Pollen and Wild Carrot do not outperform Cure.\010If you're soloing and want to just survive forever and fight forever and ever and ever and ever and ever and ever... Have Ninja as your subjob. I bet it can take down surprisingly large things. After a while. Bursting down pet monsters like this ought to work as well. Blue mage is... well. It gives you extra mana. And that's it. What, you leveled that instead of the normal mages and you don't have a better subjob ready?",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What are you doing?\010\010Okay, we've gone through the sensible options. Here is what you might expect from really far-fetched ones. We don't level with these. At all. We do some strange other content and hopefully have a blast doing it.\010+ Thief\010Pros\010Okay, you're obviously farming. Make sure to be 30 for Treasure Hunter.\010Best evasion in the game. It should help out a little at least.\010Cons\010Not a mage job. Don't party as this. Solo effort stuff.\010+ Warrior\010Pros\010You get to Provoke now! And Warcry! So you can take damage for sure, not just when you occasionally overheal.\010Oh, and you can Double Attack. Maybe cheesy stuff is possible with an Octave Club here?\010Cons\010Not a mage job. Don't party as this. Be sure to have a really, really good plan.\010+ Dark Knight\010Pros\010Stun. There's likely some occasion where this is needed.\010Absorb spells. Fun? And some Black and Dark magic too. Worse than Black Mage.\010Cons\010Not a mage job. Don't party as this. If it floats your boat then go for it solo, I guess.\010+ Bard\010Pros\010Songs. But just one. And the Refresh effect comes at the same time as Summoner's Auto-refresh does. Without the MP and Avatars. So no thanks. Paeon does heal for quite a bit in dunes. Maybe give that a go for a few levels?\010Powerful sleep, I suppose. I'm not convinced.\010Resist silence as a trait. Good...? Traits like these usually don't matter much.\010Cons\010Not a mage job, no MP, illusory goodness with the songs but it won't amount to much.\010+ Paladin\010Pros\010Undead Killer. Useful if you're doing undead killing in a solo adventure.\010Shield Bash. When meleeing you can stun and interupt things. TP moves usually get used again, but spells don't always.\010Cons\010Worse at Auto-refresh than Summoner. Worse at surviving than Ninja. It might be possible to do cool things here but I don't see it.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="What are you doing?\010\010We get what you might expect from subjobs that are too far away from your main job to matter. The outlier is that Bard could convince you it's better than it is. I hope I have dispelled that notion. Honorable mentions:\010Puppetmaster - I'm not sure what this would do? I'll be honest and say I don't really understand how the job works, or how it would work as a subjob. The recent addition of PUP and some rumours I've heard has this as better than off-beat. I'll explore this someday.\010Beastmaster - Similar to Puppetmaster. What would you do with your charmed monster that's quite a bit worse than it needs to be? White Mage is a great subjob for Beastmaster. Not the other way around.\010Dragoon - Now you hit harder. Okay, and then?\010Ranger - Wide scan for when the situation calls for it.\010Samurai - Meditate for TP and then run in melee and perform a club weapon skill to restore MP. Convoluted. Maybe fun?\010Monk - Dodge to dodge and Chakra to self-heal and remove debuffs. Both things we have spells for in Blink, Cure and Erase.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Addons\010\010I don't use addons much myself, but I figure a bare-bones list ought to be present in a guide that tries to cover everything that I know. You find these in the Addons list in the HorizonXI launcher.\010tTimers - Keep track of Regen and Haste buffs on party members.\010targetlines - See who in your party is attacking what. Reduces confusion and guesswork.\010distance - See the distance between you and your target. Useful for dodging Area of Effect-abilities and knowing whether you're in cast range quickly.\010nameplate - Reduce the size of player names to avoid clutter.\010There's supposed to be one where you can see time until your next HP/MP tick when resting. Don't know the name of it, but it sounds useful.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Spell acquisition\010\010Buying from vendor, buying from AH or killing monsters for it. Some spells drop in BCNMs or other special events. Vendor buying gets you very far and check prices on both vendor and AH to prevent being bamboozled. People do like to do this.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Food\010\010+MP food vs + MP while healing. There are many possible options with different usefulness and general price.\010Cookies.\010Pies\010Mushroom soup <-- goated.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Leveling\010\010All right, the meat of the thing. You've decided you want to play White Mage. Great. Happy to have you. Below you'll find a building-on-what's-before guide on playing the job, with noteworthy changes and things to consider when playing. I recommend cross-referencing this with the gear section below.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="1-9\010\010The start is similar to most other jobs in that you do the following:\010Melee Skill\010Rank\010Club\010B+\010Staff\010C+\010Shield\010D\010 Grab Signet.\010 Take your starter gear and a weapon - I recommend a Club. Get a shield, too. Doesn't slow you down and does give you some stats.\010 Optional: Get a Sanction buff via an Exp Ring or something else.\010 Whack stuff just outside your city. Solo or with a friend. Whack, whack.\010To see why I recommend a club, all we have to do is to check the White Mage's melee skill ranks. Moreover, clubs hit faster than staves while dealing just about the same damage per hit. Club gives you Shining Strike which has 20% MND & 20% STR scaling whereas Heavy Swing from staves has 30% STR scaling. So your weapon skill should deal more damage as well. Because we can wear shields we should. It helps out.\010Killing many Easy Preys is always going to be faster than killing many Decent challenges or higher. You'll clear more monsters, get more loot, have shorter combats, have less downtime and therefore get more experience per minute.\010Where the White Mage differs from other jobs is your available spells:\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment\0101\010Cure\0108MP\010Very handy because you are mostly hitting things, mostly using melee. Extra hp recovered before you need to sit does increase the time you can whack before sitting down, and sitting down gives you MP as well, so it's double good. Strategy: It's better to cast it outside of combat to avoid interruptions and more time spent whacking.\0103\010Dia\0107MP\010Dubious handiness. It is better to spend your MP on Cures here. The theoretical max is 20 damage which relies on the 60 base duration to run its course. (1 damage/tick (3 seconds) over 60 seconds is 20 damage). You don't want a battle to last a full minute.\0104\010Paralyze\0106MP\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="1-9\010\010Somewhat handy. Before the first half of the 1-10 stretch you have the ability to inflict Paralyze, maybe the best status effect in the game. 25% chance for the enemy to just not take an action. As your MP pool grows and as the damage enemies deal increases, this spell becomes a healing spell in disguise. It's random. And it's great.\0105 [Horizon change] \010Banish\01015MP\010Interesting pickup. You now have a way to skill up Divine magic. For 15 MP, cast time and likely 1-4 damage dealt. Won't be better than Cure for a long time.\0107\010Protect / Protectra\0109MP\010Very handy. +10DEF for 30 minutes for 9MP? Sign us up. In looking up how much damage we expect this to reduce, it's apparently hard to say and has many factors. Defense is good, though. And hard to come by outside gear and Protect. The -ra version hits all your party members, which will come in handy later.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="10-19\010\010Okay. We are out of the strict soloing zone and you can now turn on your LFP-flag and search for a party. Congratulations!\010...While you quite likely continue soloing, unfortunately. Because you get access to Cure II on level 11 for panic heal moments, there is a sentiment that waiting until the White Mage reaches level 11 is a big deal. I disagree with this: you'll spam regular Cure most of your time anyway, but it means players might not invite you as often as they should. There are ways around this problem: You can always add another White Mage or Red Mage or do whatever. It's the first level in the Dunes. Or Ghelsba outpost. Most often Dunes.\010With this follows a re-evaluation of the spells listed above. Cure, Dia, Paralyze and Protectra are essential. There are also -na spells well worth acquiring.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Partying basics\010\010Anyway, speaking of partying in the Dunes, here is the outline of how you should play as a White Mage in a party, which will be how you play for the rest of the game. The core remains the same, while the surrounding stuff, the bells and whistles, will change. This is what happens in chronological order:\010 You arrive at the party. Level sync goes up. You cast Protectra and Shellra, Shellra being available from level 17 onwards. Reapply when it expires.\010 Activate your food of choice.\010 Someone pulls - you don't. You cast Dia and Paralyze as the target comes in. Dia always sticks, Paralyze doesn't - don't worry too much about it not sticking. You skill enfeebling this way. Cast in that order. Dia => Paralyze => Cures.\010 Cure the tank when they take a hit. One cure corresponds to slightly less than a hit, but there are parries and misses to cover for this. And off-healers.\010 Remove harmful status effects on allies affected. At level 19, you have access to Poisona, Paralyna, Blindna and Silena.\010 If, and I mean if, you have MP left over - you can attempt to stick Paralyze if it got resisted or attempt Slow. This is a bit of a luxury thing in the Dunes.\010 Rest MP during downtime as soon as you're not casting. Resting gives you MP back every 12 seconds. Ideally you rest once you know the mob is going to die, with no more heals needed, and until the next pulls comes in. Squeezing every bit of time for resting is a crucial White Mage skill. Repeat from Step 3.\010If we look at partying from a priority perspective, with descending priority, giving us a so called rotation, we have:\010 Cure the tank. Your first job. You're a healer.\010 Remove harmful status effects on allies. Poison in particular can deal a lot of damage.\010 Cure non-tank allies. Others take a stray hit on occasion. Top them up.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Partying basics\010\010Cast Bar-spells when the enemy has some AoE elemental damage you'd like to reduce. This is usually a very good use of time and MP.\010 Cast enfeebles. You do have access to many great enfeebles. Dia for the defense drop and near-100% stick, Paralyze and Slow are basically healing spells. Silence on a monster that can cast spells effectively neutralizes that danger. It's good. Enfeebles are good.\010 Rest for MP. MP is what you need to do your thing.\010 Recast / Reattempt enfeebles. Only when you have full MP and nothing else to do.\010 Nuke with Elemental Magic. You can time this for Magic Bursts during Skillchains. If you have nothing else to do, and if you have the MP to spare, go for it.\010 Activate expired food / expired Sanction effects / Juice. Find some window to do this when nobody needs you to stay alive.\010 Cast Protectra / Shellra when they expire.\010In the beginning, you will be stuck in step 1 and 2 for mob after mob after mob - and that's okay. That's what your party is. You'll have to call when you need to rest for MP. Later down the line you and other party members will get tools to achieve that glorious sustain.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment\01011\010Cure II\01024MP\010Very good. Gives you a nice solid boost to your healing power. To be used in tough moments when you know a single Cure won't be enough only. The reason is that even-numbered cures, 2 and 4, generate more hate for what they do. Or conversely, odd numbered curse generate less hate for what they do.\01013\010Slow\01016MP\010Very good. It acts alongside Paralyze as a passive sort of heal, but instead of blocking whole attacks, it slows the frequency of them. Expensive MP-wise during these levels.\01015\010Silence\01016MP\010Very good, if a little situational. If a mob can cast spells, this makes them just, you know, not do that. If a mob can't cast spells, skip this.\01017\010Shell / Shellra\01018MP\010Very good. On par with Protect, but for Magic Defense. This one is known, it reduces magic damage taken by ~9.8%. On everyone. Every little bit helps, and this belongs in that category.\01019\010Blink\01020MP\010Pretty good. It gives you shadows that absorbs hits for you. Not 100% like some jobs, but still good enough to keep you alive for when you get struck accidentally.\01020\010Sneak\01015MP\010Invaluable. Money-wise. Sneak oil is the item used to reach this status effect and that costs money. This one costs MP. Paired with Invisible and you can go to nearly any corner of the world unscathed - and you can help others to do it too! Crazy.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="20-24\010\010Around here you'll likely go to Qufim Island. What you can and should bring to the table is Silence, because starting against worms at levels 19-21 ish is very common. And they cast Rasp, Stone, Stonega, all kinds of dreadful stuff. So, Level up your enfeebling, stick your Silence and play normally after that.\010The priority order of enfeebling spells is: Silence (if applicable) > Dia > Paralyze > Slow. It's unlikely you'll cast all these without a Cure in between. If you can make Dia and Paralyze, that's often good enough.\010Regen gets a special mention in your your spell rotation:\010 Heal the tank. Your first job. You're a healer.\010 Apply Regen to the tank. Will help out how much healing you'll have to do. This is an MP conservation strategy.\010 Remove harmful status effects on allies. Poison in particular can deal a lot of damage.\010 Apply Regen to non-tank allies. Everyone will take damage sometime. You've cured them. Now you have a new tool - Regen them.\010 Cure non-tank allies. Others take a stray hit on occasion. Top them up.\010 Rest of rotation continues as normal.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment\01021\010Cure III\01056MP\010Next tier Cure. You'll always get these when one presents itself. WHM gets it earlier than everyone else. It's kind of our thing.\01021\010Regen\01015MP\010Healing over time. In terms of HP recovered for MP spent, this is better than all Cures.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment\01025\010Invisible\01015MP\010Completes the Sneak/Invisible set. This protects against sight and at just level 25 you can travel nearly anywhere and take anyone with you, without fear of being slaughtered.\01025\010Raise\010150MP\010Make someone dead be not so dead. They'll be resurrected sick, but will be fine after that.\01025\010Reraise\010150MP\010Same as raise but on yourself as a buff, to be used when you accidentally get K.Od.\01027\010Protect II\010?MP\01028\010Stoneskin\010?MP",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment\01030\010Banish II\010?MP\01031\010Curaga II\010?MP\01032\010Erase\010?MP\01037\010Shell II\010?MP",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="New spells you have to get\010\010Level\010Spell\010Cost\010Comment",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="General strategy\010\010Cure potency is by far the best stat. It comes in after all other calculations and caps have been done, so it just expands on how much you can cure. Invaluable. It's also rare so grab it when you can take it.\010Auto-refresh is a close second. This contributes to you recovering as much as you spend and never needing downtime - where needing downtime is the enemy of fun and progress.\010In the later half of the game (maybe after level 50 and beyond), -enmity starts to play a role. It is arguably more important than MP while healing, but it's going to be close either way. It's a rare stat to find, so consider picking it up on pieces where it is available.\010MP while healing is similar to Auto-Refresh, but it only happens when you actively rest and can't do anything else. Still good. Still valuable. Shortens rest time, as one would expect.\010MP is a solid choice just about always. You have as much gas in the tank as you have MP. Bigger chain when you don't recover as much as you spend. Bigger margin for big spending when you do recover as much as you spend. You recover more MP when resting the more MP you have - technically, as you can rest for longer accumulating a larger bonus, resulting in more MP per second the longer you sit for. This length is governed by your total MP pool.\010MND helps with sticking enfeebles, which is what you do at the start of each fight or when you don't have anyone to heal and have a decent supply of MP.\010INT helps with sticking an enfeeble, which helps out.\010HP makes you less killable when you're in danger.\010AGI and evasion makes you less killable when you're in danger.\010VIT gives you more HP which makes you, somewhat at least, less killable.\010We get the following priority.\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="General strategy\010\010Cure potency > Auto-Refresh > -Enmity ~= MP while healing > MP >>> MND > INT >>> HP = AGI = VIT\010You'll find the above strategy in the item list below. There's also a small comment as needed to evaluate each piece.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Gear by slot and level\010\010Rather than min-maxing each level, the guide focuses on giving strong choices with budget options attached.\010+ Main hand\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Off hand\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Grips\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Ammunition\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Head\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Neck\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Earrings\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Rings\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Body\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Hands\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Waist\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Back\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Legs\010Level\010Name\010Comment\010+ Feet\010Level\010Name\010Comment",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Equipment sets\010\010Make macros that change gear to get the most out of the rather large selection of gear available and our big wardrobes.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Quests\010\010+ Artifact armor\010colspan=\"3\"| This is a good set overall. Three pieces give -enmity which is very useful. All pieces give +MP with MND and VIT stat bonuses acting as good supporting stats. Some RSE arguably outperform these pieces, even at level 50-60. Should you get it? Yes.\010AF1\010Messenger from Beyond\010Very solid choice at the level you can get it, giving +MP, MND and MP while healing.\010AF2\010Prelude of Black and White\010The +MP stat is the noteworthy one on this piece. Still Good.\010AF3\010Pieuje's Decision\010Very good piece, giving +MP, -enmity and +Enfeebling skill. That last one really hits it home for me.\010AF4\010Borghertz's Healing Hands\010The regular +MP and -enmity stuff here. In addition, the cap gives MND which is good, the pantaloons give VIT which is good and the last piece, the mitts, give healing magic skill. Very, very solid all around. \010+ Teleport spells\010colspan=\"3\" | Teleport spells are nearly always good. The quests are mostly soloable and provide a solid way of getting money, apart from just helping people out when they need it. I recommend getting all of these and no White Mage is complete without them.\010Dem\010Altana's Sorrow\010Provides quick access to Gusgen Mines and the easy side of Valkurm Dunes.\010Holla\010Healing the Land\010For the hard side of Valkurm Dunes and Jugner Forest. Not the most common of requests when teleporting for money. Popular during Eco-Warrior times, though.\010Mea\010Acting in Good Faith\010Maze of Shakhrami and Buburimu Peninsula and Mhaura access.\010Yhoat\010Missionary Man\010Useful for getting to Kazham, Temple of Uggalepih, Ifrit's Cauldron, Norg or just leveling in Elshimo generally. Somewhat but not entirely replaced by the OP warps.\010Altep\010The Missing Piece\010Saves a LOT of time when partying in the desert. \010Vahzl\010Sorcery of the North\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Quests\010\010Useful mainly for doing things up in the cold north. Fei'Yin for example.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Melee\010\010It's offbeat. I won't hold myself off for very long.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="wiki_6915",["title"]="Xlight's BST Burn Guide",["page_title"]="Xlight's BST Burn Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Xlight%27s_BST_Burn_Guide",["revision"]="110332",["requirements"]="Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="This Is A Work In Progress\010\010Welcome, my name is Xlight and I am a 75BST on HorizonXI and have mastered the dark skills of BST Burning. Do you want to get great xp and join the million other BST on Horizon? Well you have come to the right place then. This guide is to help you get from 1 to 75 BST on horizon faster than any other job, except maybe BRD.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 1-10\010\010This part is pretty simple, grab an axe and kill level 1 mobs in starter zones until you hit level 10. The trick is when you hit level 4+ try to find a level 4 beast (bat, hare, etc.) and charm it to help you kill level 1 mobs faster.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 10-20\010\010Ghelsba Outpost \010This is a common area where you form an alliance and just murder everything for low xp but very fast. Usually around 6k/hxp.\010Valkurm Dunes\010Oh the dunes, noob capital of Vana'diel. If you are a masochist and love crappy xp and a lot of death then go here for around 2-6k/hxp.\010La Theine Plateau [Horizon change] \010K-11 Worm Party where you \"Range Tank\". Range tanking is when someone with provoke goes within 17' and provokes and then runs out of range of 30'. Rinse and repeat every 30s until mob is dead. Healers keep mainly provoker alive.\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + any job /war + 4 DD's\010Pick DD's in this order in preference:\010DRG > WAR > RNG > DRK > MNK > SAM. Avoid BST unless they voker and especially THF unless they voker.\010Sync: 10-14. Exp: 10-15k/hxp\010Solo BST\010I heard rumors that BST can solo and charm mobs for xp. Honestly I believe it to be lies just like the female orgasm.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 20-23\010\010Qufim Island\010I consider this Dunes 2.0. Do this at your own risk! XP can vary from 4-12k/hxp\010Korroloka Tunnel (15-18)\010Similar to La Theine Plateua you perform a \"Range Tank\" pt. Will most likely need a WHM with silence.\010Only cons is sometimes people solo worms and some people are rude and wont leave worms alone.\010Sync: 15-18. Exp: 10-15k/hxp",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 23-30\010\010This is when you can start the real juicy XP because BST now have the most OP ability in HorizonXI CALL BEAST | !\010Korroloka Tunnel (23-27)\010Mobs: Greater Pugils (30-33) Link, Clipper (29-32)\010Rank: A (10-12k/hxp)\010Pets: NQ sheep > NQ crab > NQ hare\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010At level 23-24 just do Clippers \010When you hit 25 you have SIC moves and just spam single target moves. You can also now add Greater Pugils to your route but be very careful, they link.\010Meriphataud Mountain (23-27) D-10 or H-9 [Horizon change] \010Mobs: Puma Aggro Sight, Ridge Lizard, Dogs\010Rank: S (12-15k/hxp)\010Pets: NQ sheep only!\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010This is probably the first time you will taste BST burn and will never go back to being a SOLO BST | ! The bonus is there is 2 camps where you can exp too!\010From 23-24 Just kill the Coeurls and the odd dogs (when they spawn at nite). Your healer will need to be fast on the paralyna mainly and use regen (if they have it) or throw cure 1 only and throw cure 2 in between fights. The party should be roaming. When you hit 25 add the Lizzads into the mix and spam your Lamb Chops from your Sheeps and watch the mobs melt.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 30-40\010\010Yhoator Jungle (30-37)\010Mobs: Goblin's Bee (38-40)\010Rank: A+ (13-16k/hxp) Not an S because can be dangerous and possible deaths. \010Pets: NQ sheep with a few Beta biscuits \010Party Comp: 3x BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST THF OR puller + 2x BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST THF\010This is a little different type of Burn which people usually call a PET BURN which was usually what BLM's did soloing or duoing. The key to this is that you will have a MAX of 3 people otherwise xp will drop substantially if you have 4 or more in a party. What you do is pull a pet (BEE) while the Goblin is looking away. If the pet paths in front of it's master than the Goblin will link and aggro, so it is best to pull when the Goblin finished moving. BE CAREFUL during water weather a Water Elemental Spawns at the pond and it will magic aggro your party if you cast mage spells (ninjitsu is not mage magic).\010Crawler's Nest (32-38)\010Mobs: Death Jacket (40-42) Link, Worker Crawler (40-44) Link, Maze Lizard (41-43) Link\010Rank: C (8-12k/hxp)\010Pets: NQ tigers with a few Beta Biscuits > NQ sheep\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010I personally do not like this camp that much but it is still decent. The only reason I do not like it is because a lot of normal pts xp here so you might be competing for mobs. The other problem is all your mobs you fight link so can be dangerous. Beyond that it aint that bad.\010Yhoator Jungle (36-41) F/G-9 \010Mobs: Worker Crawlers (43-46) Link\010Rank: B (9-12k/h) Not higher because possible Coeurl aggro or Crawler links\010Pets: NQ tiger, NQ sheep, or if your name is Elon NQ Antlions\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 30-40\010\010You can fight around Teleport-Yhoat Crystal or one of the tunnels around the crystal. It is not that bad but the links and especially the Sight aggro Coeurls can be annoying.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 40-50\010\010Yhoator Jungle (39-45) F/G-9 \010Mobs: Worker Crawlers (43-46) Link, Master Coeurl (47-50) Aggro Sight \010Rank: A (9-14k/h)\010Pets: NQ tiger, 41+ NQ crab or if your name is Elon NQ Antlions\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010Go around in a circle and fight Coeurls first since they aggro and add some Crawlers in between. 41+ should just focus on Master Coeurls\010Beaucedine Glacier (41-48) J-4 Out in front of Fei'yin [Horizon change] \010Mobs: Lynx (50-53) Aggro Sight \010Rank: B+ (9-14k/h) Just a pain to get to\010Pets: NQ crab or if your name is Elon NQ Antlions\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010Basically fighting roaming and fighting Lynx. Be careful that there are some roaming dolls that aggro magic.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 50-60\010\010Bostaunieux Oubliette (50-52) F-8 Map 2\010Mobs: Werebat (55-59) Aggro Sound No Link, Hecatomb Hound (56-59) Aggro Sound LowHP\010Rank: B (9-12k/h) Can get links and/or aggro which can overwelm you\010Pets: HQ Sheep, HQ Tigers\010Party Comp: 1 Healer + 5 BST/NIN > BST/WHM > BST/THF\010Make sure you sneak before you drop down the gate. Can get links, aggro and the escape routes are far with plenty of mobs in the way. Also, only really one good camp although not many people come here and it is only decent xp for 2 level ranges 50 and 51. Be careful fighting Hounds since they put virus which can hurt your healer if not positioned properly\010King Ranpere's Tomb (52-57)\010Mobs: Tomb Worn (60-62) No Aggro No Link, Bats (???) Aggro Sound Link\010Rank: A- (10-15k/h)\010Pets: HQ Sheep, Hq Tigers\010Party Comp: SMN + RDM + 4 BST/NIN, WHM + RDM + 4x BST/NIN but not ideal\010This party requires a certain setup because you need a RDM or WHM to silence the worm while SMN hastega and whispering wind (aoe heal). The worms die very fast but can do some nasty aoe moves. Be very careful of the Quake or Stone4, fortunately they take a long time to cast so you can run out of range. Most Ideal camp is upper floor of Vrtra map in center.\010Western Altepa Desert (52-62) E/F-11 [Horizon change] \010Mobs: Dhamel (???) No Aggro Link\010Rank: A+ (8-18k/h)\010Pets: HQ Sheep ONRY\010Party Comp: Healer (any job can /whm with enough MP) + 5x BST/NIN ONLY\010",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 50-60\010\010This is one of my favorite camp because at 55 BST have rampage and the damage output increases a lot. There is 2 parts to this camp, the east (F-11) has 4 dhamels while the western (E-11) part has 7 dhamels. At 52-54 can be brutal and slow 8-10k/h but doable, just expect to lose quite a few jugs so it can get expensive. When you hit 55 it is time where you might need to roam west to east to maintain chains, the healer is there to help pull those far chains. You will get around 10-14k/h from 55-57. From 57-62 you can get 14-18k/h if your puller can help pull consistantly and heal while well geared BST do massive DMG.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Level 60-70\010\010Uleguerand Range (60-62) I-10\010Mobs: Nival Raptor (68-70) Sound Aggro No Link\010Rank: B (10-14k/h)\010Pets: HQ Sheep\010Party Comp: WHM or RDM or SMN + 5x BST/NIN\010This camp can only support 1 party and you fight within the ramp. Raptors don't have much TP but the pulls could be a bit far. Would be nice to change one of your BST as a BRD/NIN to buff and pull if you are lucky to get one.\010Labryinth Of Onzozo (61-63) G-6 or I-6\010Mobs: Torama (70-73) Sight Aggro No Link\010Rank: B (10-14k/h)\010Pets: HQ Sheep\010Party Comp: WHM or RDM or SMN + 5x BST/NIN\010This camp can be rough for healer in a NORMAL party since the Toroma spams a lot of silence or paralyze. Fortunately with BST burns you don't have to worry much about silence/paralyze so it is a good camp for BST's, only problem is one of the BST needs to pull (highly suggest bring tons of peebles). Could sub one of the BST/NIN with a BRD. Another bonus about this camp is that killing certain Torama's will have a chance to pop OSE which can be killed by a BST burn party even at 60. Ose drops an Assault Jerkin which is very nice for a BST.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Community reference: may include outdated, retail-only or conditional information. It is not a verified recommendation.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="starter_sandoria",["title"]="San d'Oria - Starter path",["page_title"]="San d'Oria - Starter path",["kind"]="path",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Getting_Started_Guide",["revision"]="49013",["requirements"]="Choose the nation you started in. Nation, level, fame and quest eligibility are not detected. Complete or skip checklist steps manually.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="Suggested starter checklist for San d'Oria. Choose your own pace. This is not an optimized leveling route.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Before leaving town: learn the controls, check your equipment and spells, set a useful Home Point, and learn about Signet. Use the Getting Started Guide as a reference; some sections reflect older server information.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_14973",["link_title"]="Getting Started Guide"},{["text"]="Start with a short city quest: speak to Ailbeche and follow Father and Son. This introduces NPC conversations without requiring a long expedition.\010\010Start: Ailbeche \226\128\148 Northern San d'Oria (J-9)\010Fame: San d'Oria\010Fame level: 1",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_6196",["link_title"]="Father and Son"},{["text"]="While leveling around Ronfaure, keep rabbit hides. Trade three to Parvipon when you have them; you do not need to buy items just to follow this path.\010\010Start: Parvipon \226\128\148 Southern San d'Oria (E-8)\010Fame: San d'Oria\010Fame level: 1\010Items: Rabbit Hide x 3",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_6856",["link_title"]="The Merchant's Bidding"},{["text"]="When ready for combat, begin your first national mission with a San d'Orian gate guard. Read the walkthrough and arrange help if the enemies are too strong.\010\010Start: Any San d'Oria Gate Guard\010Items: Orcish Axe",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_361",["link_title"]="San d'Oria Mission 1-1"},{["text"]="Next: prepare for experience parties. Read the party guide for roles and preparation. Choose camps appropriate to your level and group; the addon does not check your equipment or combat readiness.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_1830",["link_title"]="New to XP Parties Guide"},{["text"]="Level 18+: choose ONE support-job quest. Elder Memories is the Selbina option; The Old Lady is the Mhaura alternative in the next step. Read both before accepting: accepting one locks out the other. Arrange help for dangerous drops.\010\010Start: Isacio - Selbina Selbina (G-10)\010Requirements: Level 18+\010Items: Damselfly Worm\010Magicked Skull\010Crab Apron",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_4805",["link_title"]="Elder Memories",["skip_if_any"]={"wiki_4805","wiki_4844"}},{["text"]="Alternative at level 18+: use this Mhaura support-job quest instead of Elder Memories, not in addition to it. Skip this checklist step if you chose Selbina.\010\010Start: Vera - Mhaura Mhaura (G-10)\010Requirements: Level 18+\010Items: Wild Rabbit Tail\010Dhalmel Saliva\010Bloody Robe",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_4844",["link_title"]="The Old Lady",["skip_if_any"]={"wiki_4805","wiki_4844"}},{["text"]="Level 20+: plan a safe trip to Upper Jeuno for your chocobo license. Read the full quest first and gather the required grass; this is a later milestone, not a level-one trip.\010\010Start: Brutus \226\128\148 Upper Jeuno (G-7)\010Level: 20+\010Items: Gausebit Grass x4\010Previous: None",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_280",["link_title"]="Chocobo's Wounds"},{["text"]="After these first milestones, consult the progression guide for advanced jobs and limit breaks. Its advice is community-written and may need updates.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_7304",["link_title"]="Character Progression Guide 1-75 for New Players"}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Suggested order assembled from the linked wiki pages. Not tested as a complete route. Manually completed linked quests are skipped; completing a checklist step does not mark a quest completed.",["group"]="Getting started",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={},["nation"]="San d'Oria"},{["id"]="starter_bastok",["title"]="Bastok - Starter path",["page_title"]="Bastok - Starter path",["kind"]="path",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Getting_Started_Guide",["revision"]="49013",["requirements"]="Choose the nation you started in. Nation, level, fame and quest eligibility are not detected. Complete or skip checklist steps manually.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="Suggested starter checklist for Bastok. Choose your own pace. This is not an optimized leveling route.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Before leaving town: learn the controls, check your equipment and spells, set a useful Home Point, and learn about Signet. Use the Getting Started Guide as a reference; some sections reflect older server information.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_14973",["link_title"]="Getting Started Guide"},{["text"]="Speak to Gerbaum and accept Minesweeper. Collect three Zeruhn Soot while fighting enemies you can safely handle in Zeruhn Mines.\010\010Start: Gerbaum \226\128\148 Bastok Mines (F-8)\010Fame: Bastok\010Fame level: 1\010Items: Zeruhn Soot x3",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_7527",["link_title"]="Minesweeper"},{["text"]="Optional item hand-in: speak to Talib about Beauty and the Galka. Complete it when you obtain the required Zinc Ore; check the walkthrough before spending gil.\010\010Start: Talib \226\128\148 Port Bastok (F-6)\010Fame: Bastok\010Fame level: 1\010Items: Zinc Ore\010Key item: Palborough Mines Logs",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_7496",["link_title"]="Beauty and the Galka"},{["text"]="Begin your first national mission with a Bastok gate guard. Follow The Zeruhn Report walkthrough and complete its NPC conversations.\010\010Start: Any Bastok Gate Guard",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_321",["link_title"]="Bastok Mission 1-1"},{["text"]="Next: prepare for experience parties. Read the party guide for roles and preparation. Choose camps appropriate to your level and group; the addon does not check your equipment or combat readiness.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_1830",["link_title"]="New to XP Parties Guide"},{["text"]="Level 18+: choose ONE support-job quest. Elder Memories is the Selbina option; The Old Lady is the Mhaura alternative in the next step. Read both before accepting: accepting one locks out the other. Arrange help for dangerous drops.\010\010Start: Isacio - Selbina Selbina (G-10)\010Requirements: Level 18+\010Items: Damselfly Worm\010Magicked Skull\010Crab Apron",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_4805",["link_title"]="Elder Memories",["skip_if_any"]={"wiki_4805","wiki_4844"}},{["text"]="Alternative at level 18+: use this Mhaura support-job quest instead of Elder Memories, not in addition to it. Skip this checklist step if you chose Selbina.\010\010Start: Vera - Mhaura Mhaura (G-10)\010Requirements: Level 18+\010Items: Wild Rabbit Tail\010Dhalmel Saliva\010Bloody Robe",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_4844",["link_title"]="The Old Lady",["skip_if_any"]={"wiki_4805","wiki_4844"}},{["text"]="Level 20+: plan a safe trip to Upper Jeuno for your chocobo license. Read the full quest first and gather the required grass; this is a later milestone, not a level-one trip.\010\010Start: Brutus \226\128\148 Upper Jeuno (G-7)\010Level: 20+\010Items: Gausebit Grass x4\010Previous: None",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_280",["link_title"]="Chocobo's Wounds"},{["text"]="After these first milestones, consult the progression guide for advanced jobs and limit breaks. Its advice is community-written and may need updates.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_7304",["link_title"]="Character Progression Guide 1-75 for New Players"}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Suggested order assembled from the linked wiki pages. Not tested as a complete route. Manually completed linked quests are skipped; completing a checklist step does not mark a quest completed.",["group"]="Getting started",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={},["nation"]="Bastok"},{["id"]="starter_windurst",["title"]="Windurst - Starter path",["page_title"]="Windurst - Starter path",["kind"]="path",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Getting_Started_Guide",["revision"]="49013",["requirements"]="Choose the nation you started in. Nation, level, fame and quest eligibility are not detected. Complete or skip checklist steps manually.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="Suggested starter checklist for Windurst. Choose your own pace. This is not an optimized leveling route.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Before leaving town: learn the controls, check your equipment and spells, set a useful Home Point, and learn about Signet. Use the Getting Started Guide as a reference; some sections reflect older server information.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_14973",["link_title"]="Getting Started Guide"},{["text"]="Begin with Kopuro-Popuro in Windurst Woods. Keep the three requested items as you explore and level; return when you have all of them.\010\010Start: Kopuro-Popuro \226\128\148 Windurst Woods (H-10)\010Fame: Windurst\010Fame level: 1\010Items: Rabbit Hide\010Two-Leaf Mandragora Bud\010Insect Wing",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_2025",["link_title"]="The All-New C-2000"},{["text"]="Try a city sightseeing quest: speak to Baren-Moren in southern Windurst Waters. Read the restrictions in the walkthrough before leaving the area.\010\010Start: Baren-Moren \226\128\148 Windurst Waters (H-7) / South\010Fame: Windurst\010Fame level: 1\010Items: Key item: New Model Hat",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_2550",["link_title"]="Hat in Hand"},{["text"]="When ready to explore outside the city, accept your first national mission from a Windurst gate guard. Read the ruins route carefully and get help when needed.\010\010Start: Any Windurst Gate Guard",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_341",["link_title"]="Windurst Mission 1-1"},{["text"]="For a more detailed Windurst route, open this level 1-11 community guide. Treat its prices and level targets as suggestions rather than guarantees.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_24260",["link_title"]="Efficient Windurst Starting Guide 1-11"},{["text"]="Next: prepare for experience parties. Read the party guide for roles and preparation. Choose camps appropriate to your level and group; the addon does not check your equipment or combat readiness.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_1830",["link_title"]="New to XP Parties Guide"},{["text"]="Level 18+: choose ONE support-job quest. Elder Memories is the Selbina option; The Old Lady is the Mhaura alternative in the next step. Read both before accepting: accepting one locks out the other. Arrange help for dangerous drops.\010\010Start: Isacio - Selbina Selbina (G-10)\010Requirements: Level 18+\010Items: Damselfly Worm\010Magicked Skull\010Crab Apron",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_4805",["link_title"]="Elder Memories",["skip_if_any"]={"wiki_4805","wiki_4844"}},{["text"]="Alternative at level 18+: use this Mhaura support-job quest instead of Elder Memories, not in addition to it. Skip this checklist step if you chose Selbina.\010\010Start: Vera - Mhaura Mhaura (G-10)\010Requirements: Level 18+\010Items: Wild Rabbit Tail\010Dhalmel Saliva\010Bloody Robe",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_4844",["link_title"]="The Old Lady",["skip_if_any"]={"wiki_4805","wiki_4844"}},{["text"]="Level 20+: plan a safe trip to Upper Jeuno for your chocobo license. Read the full quest first and gather the required grass; this is a later milestone, not a level-one trip.\010\010Start: Brutus \226\128\148 Upper Jeuno (G-7)\010Level: 20+\010Items: Gausebit Grass x4\010Previous: None",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_280",["link_title"]="Chocobo's Wounds"},{["text"]="After these first milestones, consult the progression guide for advanced jobs and limit breaks. Its advice is community-written and may need updates.\010\010Reference material. Check the linked wiki for updates.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]="",["link_id"]="wiki_7304",["link_title"]="Character Progression Guide 1-75 for New Players"}},["review"]="Not verified in game.",["warnings"]="Suggested order assembled from the linked wiki pages. Not tested as a complete route. Manually completed linked quests are skipped; completing a checklist step does not mark a quest completed.",["group"]="Getting started",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={},["nation"]="Windurst"},{["id"]="quest_pup_lb5_20260918",["title"]="Achieving True Power",["page_title"]="Achieving True Power",["kind"]="quest",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Achieving_True_Power",["revision"]="",["requirements"]="Start: Shamarhaan - Bastok Markets (F-9)\010Requirements: Puppetmaster level 66+\010Item: Puppetmaster's Testimony\010Previous: Puppetmaster Blues",["rewards"]="Puppetmaster limit break / raises level cap to 75",["repeatable"]="See current HorizonXI quest page.",["summary"]="Puppetmaster's Genkai 5 quest. Bring a Puppetmaster's Testimony to Shamarhaan and complete the Puppetmaster limit-break battlefield.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="Set Puppetmaster as your main job at level 66 or higher and speak with Shamarhaan at Bastok Markets (F-9) to begin the limit-break challenge.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Obtain a Puppetmaster's Testimony. The HorizonXI quest/category data identifies this as the required PUP limit-break quest after Puppetmaster Blues.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Return to Shamarhaan with the testimony and proceed to the Puppetmaster Genkai 5 battlefield. HorizonXI's Goblin Footprints page confirms Achieving True Power cutscenes at Navukgo Execution Chamber.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="Complete the battlefield and finish the quest to raise the level cap to 75. Check the linked HorizonXI page for any current Horizon-specific fight/mechanic changes before attempting it.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from current HorizonXI quest/category references on 2026-09-18. Detailed battlefield mechanics are intentionally not presented as verified.",["warnings"]="HorizonXI currently indexes this quest as PUP Limit Break, but the detailed page was not available to the importer during this update. No modern-retail strategy text was imported.",["group"]="Quests",["jobs"]={"Puppetmaster (PUP)"},["job_sources"]={{["job"]="Puppetmaster (PUP)",["kind"]="Limit break quest",["page"]="Achieving True Power",["revision"]=""}},["start_zones"]={"Bastok Markets"},["step_zones"]={"Bastok Markets","Navukgo Execution Chamber"}},{["id"]="guide_thf_endgame_20260918",["title"]="Thief Endgame Gear Guide",["page_title"]="Thief Endgame Gear Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Thief_Endgame_Gear_Guide",["revision"]="63791",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="HorizonXI Wiki endgame THF gear reference covering dagger choices and specialized equipment sets.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="OVERVIEW\010\010Endgame THF reference organized around multiple purpose-built sets rather than one all-purpose loadout. Prioritize doing the THF role correctly before chasing expensive upgrades.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="CORE SETS\010\010The page separates TP/accuracy-haste, evasion, weapon-skill, Sneak Attack, Trick Attack, ranged/status-bolt, and Steal sets. Keep Treasure Hunter pieces available to swap when needed.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="ERA WARNING\010\010Some work-in-progress tables on the source page still include out-of-era equipment. Treat those rows as non-Horizon recommendations unless the item is confirmed on HorizonXI.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="HorizonXI Wiki marks parts of this guide as work-in-progress and explicitly warns that some lower sections may still contain out-of-era equipment. Use the current Horizon-compatible sections and verify questionable items.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="guide_thf_exp_20260918",["title"]="EXP Party Guide - Thief",["page_title"]="EXP Party Guide - Thief",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/EXP_Party_Guide_-_Thief",["revision"]="115935",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="HorizonXI leveling-party guide for THF roles, pulling, hate control, bolts, skillchains and party preparation.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="ROLE\010\010THF is presented as a hybrid damage/utility job: pull, help control enmity, debuff with bolts, and contribute to skillchains rather than judging the job only by raw DPS.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="PARTY PREP\010\010Keep dagger and marksmanship skills current. Acid Bolts are emphasized for defense down, with Sleep/Bloody Bolts adding control and sustain. Build separate melee and ranged-accuracy swaps as you level.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="LEVELING FLOW\010\010Sneak Attack at 15 begins the core party role; later levels add Trick Attack and stronger dagger weapon skills. The guide encourages coordinating skillchains and using THF utility to reduce pressure on the healer.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="Community guide: tactics are recommendations, not server rules.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="guide_thf_terminalbloom_20260918",["title"]="Terminalbloom's THF gear guide",["page_title"]="Terminalbloom's THF gear guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Terminalbloom%27s_THF_gear_guide",["revision"]="120196",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="HorizonXI-specific THF guide focused on Era+ job changes, endgame roles, merits, weapons and gear sets.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="HORIZON FOCUS\010\010This guide is written specifically around HorizonXI changes. It highlights Treasure Hunter tagging, hate control, status bolts and THF defensive/off-tank utility.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="BUILDING THF\010\010The guide covers subjobs, merits, dagger weapon skills, accuracy tiers and specialized TP/WS/SA/TA/ranged/Steal/evasion sets. Accuracy needs and target level should drive which set you use.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="PLAYSTYLE\010\010Treat TP generation, Sneak Attack and Trick Attack as a loop. Use bolts for defense down/control and swap defensive gear proactively when you expect physical, magical or breath damage.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="Community guide and work in progress. Recommendations can change as Horizon balance/content changes.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="guide_blm_standard_exp_20260918",["title"]="Standard EXP Party Guide - Black Mage",["page_title"]="Standard EXP Party Guide - Black Mage",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Standard_EXP_Party_Guide_-_Black_Mage",["revision"]="122713",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="HorizonXI guide for playing BLM in standard EXP parties instead of only solo/manaburn setups.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="ROLE\010\010BLM can add burst damage, debuffs, crowd control and backup healing/cleansing in a normal party. The page emphasizes watching melee TP and coordinating magic bursts.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="PREPARATION\010\010Keep nukes, Drain/Aspir and useful support spells current. INT is the main offensive stat; MND helps white-magic enfeebles. Elemental staves become major upgrades beginning at level 51.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="PARTY RHYTHM\010\010Do more than rest between bursts: debuff, support when needed, free-nuke when efficient, and burst reliable skillchains. The guide treats standard-party BLM as an active, technical role.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="Community guide: party compositions and recommendations are situational.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="guide_drk_riverking_20260918",["title"]="Dark Knight Guide by Riverking",["page_title"]="Dark Knight Guide by Riverking",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Dark_Knight_Guide_by_Riverking",["revision"]="120854",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="Large HorizonXI-specific DRK reference covering Era+ changes, abilities, spells, subjobs, leveling, merits, weapons and endgame sets.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="OVERVIEW\010\010Riverking frames DRK as a heavy damage dealer with meaningful crowd control, stuns, absorbs and situational tanking utility on HorizonXI.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="HORIZON CHANGES\010\010The guide documents Horizon-specific changes to abilities, artifact/relic gear and weapons. These sections are more useful than importing modern retail DRK advice.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="REFERENCE SECTIONS\010\010Use the source for abilities/spells, subjobs, leveling, merits, weapon skills, X-hit planning and endgame gear/resistance sets. The ToAU-specific chapter on this revision is incomplete.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="Horizon-specific guide, but its ToAU section was still unfinished on the cited revision. Use the completed Horizon sections and verify expansion-era additions against current pages.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="guide_pld_exp_20260918",["title"]="EXP Party Guide - Paladin",["page_title"]="EXP Party Guide - Paladin",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/EXP_Party_Guide_-_Paladin",["revision"]="118276",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="HorizonXI EXP-party guide for PLD tanking, healing support, enmity, shields, gear and MP management.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="ROLE\010\010PLD is treated as a supportive healer-tank: hold the enemy, reduce incoming damage, cure yourself/allies, Cover teammates and contribute to skillchains when appropriate.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="PREPARATION\010\010Favor defense/VIT and an appropriate shield while keeping enough accuracy to connect and build TP. WAR is the common leveling subjob; later setups may use NIN or other specialized options.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="ENMITY & MP\010\010Curing is a major hate tool, so coordinate healing with the main healer. Bring refresh drinks/MP recovery options and use PLD abilities to keep the group stable rather than chasing damage charts.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="Community guide: exact party setups and gear choices are situational.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}},{["id"]="guide_bst_leveling_20260918",["title"]="Beastmaster Leveling Guide",["page_title"]="Beastmaster Leveling Guide",["kind"]="guide",["availability"]="unverified",["source"]="https://horizonffxi.wiki/Beastmaster_Leveling_Guide",["revision"]="119626",["requirements"]="HorizonXI community reference. Open the linked wiki for the complete live page.",["rewards"]="Not applicable",["repeatable"]="Not applicable",["summary"]="HorizonXI community leveling reference for BST main and /BST, including camps, charm, jugs, subjobs and practical solo advice.",["changes"]="",["steps"]={{["text"]="BASICS\010\010The guide focuses on independent leveling, Charm/Jug Pet use and minimizing downtime. It discusses common subjobs, food, pet food and maintaining both axe/scythe skills.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="CHARM & PET MANAGEMENT\010\010Build CHR/Charm options early, learn safe decharm handling and use pet families/abilities for utility. Keep recovery items available so resting does not dominate your EXP time.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""},{["text"]="CAMPS\010\010The source includes level-range camp suggestions through 75 for BST main and /BST. Competition and mob level rolls can change the practical EXP rate, so treat each camp as a starting point rather than a guarantee.",["zone"]="",["grid"]="",["npc"]=""}},["review"]="Added from the current HorizonXI Wiki Job Specific guide index on 2026-09-18; concise in-addon summary, not a verbatim copy.",["warnings"]="Community-maintained guide. Some camp notes are personal experience and one /BST subsection notes that some locations originated from older retail guidance; verify targets on Horizon.",["group"]="Reference guides",["jobs"]={},["job_sources"]={},["start_zones"]={},["step_zones"]={}}}
